insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Journey Map — Overview

AUTISM SPECTRUM DISORDER (ASD) — Complete Family Navigation Journey (California)

16 steps across 6 phases:
* 1 Concern & Screening: Parent Notices Developmental Concerns, Well-Child Visit Screening (M-CHAT-R/F), Referral to Regional Center (Self-Refer — No Docto
* 2 Evaluation & Diagnosis: RC Intake Assessment, Multidisciplinary Evaluation (RC-Arranged), Private Diagnostic Evaluation (Optional but Valuab, Eligibility Determination (RC Decision)
* 3 Service Planning: IPP Development (Initial Service Plan), Apply for Medi-Cal, SSI, IHSS, CalABLE (Full Benef
* 4 School District IEP: Request School District Evaluation (Written Reques, School District Evaluation & IEP Development, IEP Dispute Resolution (If Needed)
* 5 Insurance & Therapy: Insurance Authorization for ABA Therapy, Begin and Coordinate All Therapy Services
* 6 Financial Protection & Lifelong Management: CalABLE, SNT & Age-18 Planning, Ongoing Authorization Renewals, Reviews & Lifetime$kb$, $kb$journey_autism$kb$, $kb$jrn-autism-overview$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-autism-overview","title":"Autism Journey Map — Overview","category":"journey-autism","subcategory":"overview","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 1.1: Parent Notices Developmental Concerns

Autism — Step 1.1: Parent Notices Developmental Concerns

WHAT TO DO: START DOCUMENTING IMMEDIATELY: (1) Keep a dated journal of specific behaviors — 'Jan 15: Teddy did not respond to name called 5 times at park.' (2) Video record concerning behaviors on your phone — these are INVALUABLE for evaluators. (3) Track milestones against CDC/AAP checklist: by 12 mo = responds to name, points, waves; by 18 mo = at least 6 words, points to show you things, copies others; by 24 mo = 2-word phrases, follows 2-step instructions, notices when others are hurt. (4) Note any REGRESSION — loss of previously acquired words or skills is an urgent red flag requiring immediate action. (5) Talk to your pediatrician at the NEXT scheduled visit — do not wait for a special appointment. (6) Trust your instincts — parents are right about developmental concerns more often than not.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Download CDC Milestone Tracker app (free, cdc.gov/milestones) and complete age-appropriate checklist TODAY.
STEP 2: Start a 'concerns notebook' — write date + specific observation + context. Example: 'Feb 3: At playgroup, Teddy lined up all cars instead of rolling them. Did not look at me when I said his name 3x.'
STEP 3: Record 2-3 short videos (30-60 sec each) showing concerning behaviors in natural settings.
STEP 4: At next pediatrician visit, say: 'I have specific developmental concerns I'd like to discuss. I've been tracking milestones and have notes and videos to share.'
STEP 5: If pediatrician says 'wait and see,' say: 'I understand, but I'd like a referral to the Regional Center and a developmental screening today. Early intervention has the strongest evidence base, and I don't want to lose time.'

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN: Your child's primary care doctor — bring your notes and videos to the next visit.
REGIONAL CENTER (RC): Find yours at dds.ca.gov/rc (21 RCs in California). You can self-refer — NO doctor referral needed. Call the intake line directly.
CDC MILESTONE TRACKER: cdc.gov/milestones (free app, iOS/Android)
FIRST SIGNS: firstsigns.org — video library of autism red flags

DOCUMENTS: No documents required at this stage. BEGIN CREATING:
• Developmental concerns journal (dated entries)
• Video recordings of behaviors (save to dedicated phone folder)
• Printed CDC milestone checklist with notes
• List of specific questions for pediatrician
• Baby book/medical records with developmental history

TIMELINE: Act NOW — do not wait. Every month of delay during ages 0-3 is critical lost intervention time. The AAP recommends universal autism screening at 18 and 24 months, but if you have concerns at ANY age, act immediately. Call your Regional Center TODAY if you have concerns.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) 'Wait and see' — this is the #1 cause of delayed diagnosis. Every month of delay is lost intervention during the critical brain plasticity window (ages 0-3). (2) Compare to siblings or other children — autism presents differently in every child. (3) Let anyone dismiss your concerns with 'boys talk later' or 'Einstein didn't talk until 4.' (4) Rely solely on verbal milestones — social engagement, play skills, and sensory responses are equally important. (5) Assume bilingualism is causing delays — it does NOT cause autism-like symptoms. (6) Wait for school to notice — you know your child best.

RISKS: ★ CRITICAL DELAYS: (1) Pediatrician says 'wait and see' — delays intervention by 6-12 months on average. (2) Cultural stigma prevents families from seeking evaluation — Latino/Black children diagnosed 2+ years later than White children. (3) Family members (grandparents, spouse) dismiss concerns — 'My brother didn't talk until 3 and he's fine.' (4) Girls with autism missed — they mask/camouflage social difficulties better. (5) Child meets SOME milestones so parent assumes all is fine — autism is about quality of social interaction, not just presence/absence of skills.

RESOURCES: FREE PARENT TRAINING:
• CHADD Parent-to-Parent: chadd.org (covers neurodevelopmental concerns broadly)
• Autism Speaks 100 Day Kit: autismspeaks.org/tool-kit (free download at diagnosis)
• First Signs: firstsigns.org (video library of early signs)
• CDC 'Learn the Signs, Act Early': cdc.gov/actearly
• CA PTI Network: Your local Parent Training & Information center — find at parentcenterhub.org
• TASK (Team of Advocates for Special Kids): taskca.org
• Matrix Parent Network: matrixparents.org (Bay Area)

PRIMARY AGENCY: None
(Parent-initiated)
OTHER AGENCIES: Pediatrician$kb$, $kb$journey_autism$kb$, $kb$jrn-autism-s11$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-autism-s11","title":"Autism Step 1.1: Parent Notices Developmental Concerns","category":"journey-autism","subcategory":"step-11","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 1.2: Well-Child Visit Screening (M-CHAT-R/F)

Autism — Step 1.2: Well-Child Visit Screening (M-CHAT-R/F)

WHAT TO DO: REQUEST M-CHAT-R/F SCREENING: (1) At the 18-month and 24-month well-child visits, ASK your pediatrician: 'Will you be administering the M-CHAT autism screening today?' (2) If they don't offer it, request it explicitly — it's a simple 20-question parent questionnaire that takes 5 minutes. (3) Complete the M-CHAT honestly — do not 'help' your child's score by assuming what they CAN do vs what they ACTUALLY DO. (4) If M-CHAT is positive (score 3+), insist on the Follow-Up Interview (M-CHAT-R/F) — this reduces false positives. (5) If screening is positive, immediately request referral to Regional Center AND a developmental pediatrician. (6) Even if M-CHAT is negative but you still have concerns, pursue evaluation anyway — M-CHAT misses ~20% of children who are later diagnosed.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before your child's 18-month visit, call the office and ask: 'Will the M-CHAT-R/F be administered at this visit? If not, I'd like to request it.'
STEP 2: Complete the M-CHAT-R/F questionnaire honestly in the waiting room. Score is 0-20 — scores of 3-7 = medium risk (follow-up needed), 8+ = high risk (refer immediately).
STEP 3: If score is 3+, the pediatrician should administer the Follow-Up Interview. If they don't, request it or score it yourself at mchatscreen.com.
STEP 4: If screening is positive, say to your pediatrician: 'Based on this screening, I'd like two things today: (a) a referral to a developmental pediatrician, and (b) the phone number for our Regional Center so I can self-refer for evaluation.'
STEP 5: Do NOT leave the appointment without a written plan for next steps. If the doctor says 'let's rescreen in 6 months,' say: 'I'd prefer to begin the evaluation process now and cancel if concerns resolve.'

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN: Your child's doctor administers the M-CHAT at well-child visits.
M-CHAT ONLINE: mchatscreen.com — free, you can complete and score it yourself at any time.
IF PEDIATRICIAN WON'T SCREEN: Complete M-CHAT yourself at mchatscreen.com and bring results to appointment. Say: 'I completed the M-CHAT and my child scored [X]. I'd like to discuss this and get referrals.'
DEVELOPMENTAL PEDIATRICIAN REFERRAL: Ask pediatrician for referral to developmental pediatrician at nearest children's hospital (e.g., UCSF, Stanford, Children's California).
REGIONAL CENTER: Self-refer directly — no doctor needed.

DOCUMENTS: • M-CHAT-R/F completed questionnaire (keep a copy)
• M-CHAT-R/F Follow-Up Interview results (if applicable)
• Well-child visit summary/after-visit note
• Referral to developmental pediatrician (written)
• Your developmental concerns journal and videos
• Any prior developmental screening results

TIMELINE: 18-month well-child visit: First M-CHAT screening.
24-month well-child visit: Second M-CHAT screening.
AAP also recommends general developmental screening at 9, 18, and 30 months.
If positive screen: referral should happen SAME DAY. Do not wait for next appointment.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Skip the 18- and 24-month well-child visits — these are the recommended screening windows. (2) Accept 'we don't do that screening' from your pediatrician — M-CHAT is AAP recommended and free. (3) Assume a negative M-CHAT means no autism — false negatives occur, especially for girls and milder presentations. (4) Let the pediatrician screen without completing the M-CHAT questionnaire (just asking 'does your child point?' in passing is NOT a valid screen). (5) Wait for a positive screen to act — if you have concerns regardless of screening result, self-refer to RC immediately.

RISKS: ★ SCREENING FAILURES: (1) M-CHAT not administered — many pediatricians skip it despite AAP recommendation. (2) Screening done casually (verbal questions only, not formal questionnaire). (3) False negatives — M-CHAT sensitivity is ~83%, meaning ~17% of autistic children screen negative. (4) Positive screen but no follow-up — pediatrician says 'let's wait.' (5) Latino and Black children 40% less likely to be screened and referred. (6) Screening at 18 months too early for some children — repeat at 24 months critical.

RESOURCES: M-CHAT ONLINE: mchatscreen.com (free, validated, score instantly)
AAP SCREENING GUIDELINES: aap.org/autism
CDC 'Learn the Signs, Act Early': cdc.gov/actearly — free materials in multiple languages
Autism Navigator: autismnavigator.com — video-based screening tool for providers

PRIMARY AGENCY: Pediatrician / PCP
OTHER AGENCIES: None at this stage$kb$, $kb$journey_autism$kb$, $kb$jrn-autism-s12$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-autism-s12","title":"Autism Step 1.2: Well-Child Visit Screening (M-CHAT-R/F)","category":"journey-autism","subcategory":"step-12","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 1.3: Referral to Regional Center (Self-Refer — No Doctor Needed)

Autism — Step 1.3: Referral to Regional Center (Self-Refer — No Doctor Needed)

WHAT TO DO: SELF-REFER TO YOUR REGIONAL CENTER TODAY: (1) Call your local RC intake line — you do NOT need a doctor referral, teacher letter, or any documentation. A parent phone call is sufficient. (2) Say: 'I am a parent and I'd like to refer my child for a developmental evaluation. My child is [age] and I have concerns about [speech/social interaction/behavior].' (3) Ask for the name and direct number of the intake coordinator assigned to your case. (4) Confirm: 'What is my intake appointment date? I understand you have 15 working days to complete intake.' (5) Request a Spanish/other language interpreter if needed — RC is required to provide this. (6) You can ALSO pursue a school district evaluation (for age 3+) simultaneously — these are separate processes and you should do both.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Find your RC at dds.ca.gov/rc/listing. Find your local RC from the 21 statewide centers.
STEP 2: Call the intake line during business hours (M-F 8am-5pm). If you get voicemail, leave: your name, child's name, DOB, phone number, brief concern, and request for intake appointment.
STEP 3: If no callback within 3 business days, call again. Document every call attempt (date, time, who you spoke with).
STEP 4: At intake, bring: child's birth certificate, proof of CA residency (utility bill), medical records if available (but NOT required), your concerns journal and videos.
STEP 5: After intake, ask: 'What is the next step? When will the evaluation be scheduled? Who is my service coordinator?' Get name and direct phone number.
STEP 6: Mark your calendar: 120 days from referral = deadline for RC to complete evaluation and make eligibility determination.

CONTACT: YOUR LOCAL REGIONAL CENTER: dds.ca.gov/rc/listing — California has 21 Regional Centers serving every county. Call your local RC's intake line.
CA DDS (oversees all RCs): dds.ca.gov
RC INTAKE HELP: If your RC is unresponsive:
• OCRA (Office of Clients' Rights Advocacy): (800) 390-7032
• Disability Rights California: (800) 776-5746
LANTERMAN ACT INFO: disabilityrightsca.org/publications — free guides, multiple languages.
UNDIVIDED NAVIGATOR: undivided.io — free family navigator service.
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — Family Resource Centers statewide, parent-to-parent support.

DOCUMENTS: TO BRING TO INTAKE:
• Child's birth certificate (copy)
• Proof of CA residency (utility bill, lease)
• Medi-Cal card or insurance card
• Any existing medical/developmental records
• Your concerns journal and videos
• Immunization records (optional)

DOCUMENTS RC WILL PROVIDE:
• Referral acknowledgment
• Intake assessment form
• Rights booklet (Lanterman Act rights)
• Consent for evaluation forms

TIMELINE: Lanterman Act requires: intake within 15 WORKING DAYS of referral. Complete evaluation and eligibility determination within 120 CALENDAR DAYS. If RC misses these deadlines, document and escalate to DDS or OCRA.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Wait for your pediatrician to make the referral — self-refer TODAY. (2) Assume you need a diagnosis first — RC evaluates and diagnoses. (3) Be intimidated by the intake process — you have a RIGHT to services under the Lanterman Act. (4) Accept 'we'll put you on a waitlist' without getting a specific intake date within 15 working days. (5) Skip the RC referral because you're already pursuing school evaluation — RC provides different services (ABA, respite, parent training) that school does not.

RISKS: ★ RC ACCESS BARRIERS: (1) Phone lines busy/understaffed — may take multiple calls. (2) Language barriers if intake staff don't speak family's language. (3) Rural families may be far from RC office. (4) RC may try to defer to school district — insist on RC evaluation for Lanterman eligibility. (5) Families with undocumented status may fear contact with government agency — RC does NOT share immigration information. (6) Intake coordinator may minimize concerns or discourage evaluation.

RESOURCES: LANTERMAN ACT RIGHTS GUIDE: disabilityrightsca.org/publications — free, in multiple languages
OCRA (Office of Clients' Rights Advocacy): (800) 390-7032 — free advocacy if RC is unresponsive
UNDIVIDED: undivided.io — free navigator service to help with RC process
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — Family Resource Center in your area for peer support
SERR MANUAL: serr.disabilityrightsca.org — complete special education and RC rights reference

PRIMARY AGENCY: Regional Center
OTHER AGENCIES: Pediatrician (optional referral)$kb$, $kb$journey_autism$kb$, $kb$jrn-autism-s13$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-autism-s13","title":"Autism Step 1.3: Referral to Regional Center (Self-Refer — No Doctor Needed)","category":"journey-autism","subcategory":"step-13","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 2.1: RC Intake Assessment

Autism — Step 2.1: RC Intake Assessment

WHAT TO DO: PREPARE FOR INTAKE MEETING: (1) Organize your documentation in a folder: medical records, concerns journal, videos, milestone tracking. (2) Write a 1-page 'Parent Concerns Letter' summarizing your specific concerns — this becomes part of the official record. (3) At intake, be specific and detailed: not 'he doesn't talk much' but 'at 22 months he has 3 words (mama, dada, ball), does not point to request items, does not follow simple commands like bring me your shoes, and does not look when I call his name.' (4) Ask the intake coordinator: 'Will my child be evaluated by a psychologist, speech-language pathologist, and occupational therapist? I want a comprehensive multidisciplinary evaluation.' (5) Request that evaluation be conducted in your home language if applicable.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before intake, write your Parent Concerns Letter. Include: child's age, specific concerns by domain (communication, social, behavior, sensory, motor), when you first noticed, what others have observed (daycare, family), any regression.
STEP 2: Call RC 1 week before intake to confirm: date, time, location, what to bring, whether interpreter will be present.
STEP 3: At intake meeting, give the coordinator your written concerns and ask them to include it in the file. Ask: 'Can I get a copy of the intake report when it's completed?'
STEP 4: Before leaving, confirm: 'When will evaluations be scheduled? How many evaluation sessions will there be? Will I receive a copy of all evaluation reports?'
STEP 5: Write down the name and direct contact of your intake coordinator. Follow up in writing (email) confirming what was discussed.

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER INTAKE COORDINATOR: You'll be assigned one at intake — get their name, direct phone, and email.
IF RC IS UNRESPONSIVE: Office of Clients' Rights Advocacy (OCRA): (800) 390-7032
DISABILITY RIGHTS CALIFORNIA: (800) 776-5746 — free legal advocacy
FAMILY RESOURCE NAVIGATOR: undivided.io — can help prepare for intake

DOCUMENTS: BRING TO INTAKE:
• Parent Concerns Letter (written, dated, keep copy)
• Medical records and birth records
• Developmental history notes and videos
• Prior evaluations (if any)
• Proof of CA residency
• Birth certificate
• Medi-Cal/insurance card

RC PROVIDES AT INTAKE:
• Intake assessment report
• Consent for evaluation
• Lanterman Act rights booklet
• Service Coordinator assignment (may come later)

TIMELINE: 15 WORKING DAYS from referral to intake appointment (Lanterman Act mandate). If RC schedules intake beyond 15 days, call and say: 'Under the Lanterman Act, intake must occur within 15 working days of referral. My referral date was [date]. Please schedule within this timeline.'

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Understate your concerns — this is not the time to be optimistic. Describe your child's DAILY challenges, not their best moments. (2) Fail to mention regression (loss of skills) if it occurred — this is an urgent indicator. (3) Sign consent forms without reading them. (4) Accept a screening-level evaluation when your child needs comprehensive assessment. (5) Forget to ask about the evaluation timeline — 120-day clock starts at referral.

RISKS: RC delays intake beyond 15 days. Intake is superficial — doesn't capture full picture of concerns. Records requests slow process. Parent doesn't know what to bring. Coordinator minimizes concerns. Bilingual family not offered interpreter.

RESOURCES: UNDIVIDED: undivided.io — free family navigator, intake preparation help
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — peer support, may have parent mentor who's been through RC process
DRC PUBLICATIONS: disabilityrightsca.org/publications — RC eligibility guides in English/Spanish
MATRIX PARENT NETWORK: matrixparents.org — Bay Area parent training on navigating RC

PRIMARY AGENCY: Regional Center
OTHER AGENCIES: Pediatrician (records)$kb$, $kb$journey_autism$kb$, $kb$jrn-autism-s21$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-autism-s21","title":"Autism Step 2.1: RC Intake Assessment","category":"journey-autism","subcategory":"step-21","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 2.2: Multidisciplinary Evaluation (RC-Arranged)

Autism — Step 2.2: Multidisciplinary Evaluation (RC-Arranged)

WHAT TO DO: ENSURE COMPREHENSIVE EVALUATION: (1) RC should arrange evaluation by a TEAM: psychologist (cognitive/autism diagnostic), speech-language pathologist (communication), occupational therapist (sensory/motor/adaptive). (2) Autism-specific tools should include: ADOS-2 (Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule — gold standard), ADI-R or DISCO (parent interview), cognitive testing (Bayley-4 for young children, WISC-V for older), adaptive behavior (Vineland-3 or ABAS-3). (3) You have the RIGHT to be present during evaluations. (4) Ask evaluators to document strengths as well as challenges. (5) If your child is bilingual, request bilingual evaluator or interpreter — evaluation in one language only may underestimate abilities. (6) Share your videos and concerns journal with evaluators.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: When RC schedules evaluations, ask: 'Which professionals will evaluate my child? What specific tests will be administered? How many sessions?'
STEP 2: Before each evaluation session, provide evaluators with: your Parent Concerns Letter, videos, milestone tracking notes, any medical records.
STEP 3: During evaluations, take notes on what you observe. Note anything that seems unusual about the testing environment or your child's behavior that day.
STEP 4: After evaluations are complete, request copies of ALL reports. You have a legal right to these. Say: 'I'd like copies of all evaluation reports as soon as they're available, before the eligibility meeting.'
STEP 5: Review reports carefully. If anything seems inaccurate, write a response letter to be included in the file.

CONTACT: RC-CONTRACTED EVALUATORS: RC arranges these — you don't choose the providers, but you CAN request specific qualifications (e.g., bilingual, autism specialist).
IF YOU DISAGREE WITH RC EVALUATION: You can request an Independent Evaluation — contact OCRA at (800) 390-7032.
PRIVATE EVALUATION (OPTIONAL): If you want a second opinion or faster diagnosis, ask your pediatrician for referral to developmental pediatrician. UC Davis MIND Institute: (916) 703-0280. UCSF Autism Clinic: (415) 353-2101. Stanford Autism Center.

DOCUMENTS: EVALUATION REPORTS (request copies of ALL):
• Psychological/Cognitive evaluation report
• Speech-Language evaluation report
• Occupational Therapy evaluation report
• Autism diagnostic report (ADOS-2 results)
• Adaptive behavior assessment (Vineland-3)
• Developmental history summary

PARENT SHOULD PROVIDE:
• Parent Concerns Letter
• Video recordings
• Medical records
• Prior evaluations
• Daycare/preschool reports

TIMELINE: 120 CALENDAR DAYS from initial referral to RC eligibility determination. Evaluations should be completed well before this deadline to allow time for the eligibility meeting. If evaluations are delayed, call your Service Coordinator and say: 'I'm tracking the 120-day timeline. When will evaluations be completed?'

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept a diagnosis based on a single brief office visit — comprehensive evaluation typically requires 4-8 hours across multiple sessions. (2) Let evaluators see your child only at their best — if mornings are hard, schedule morning sessions. (3) Fail to mention all your concerns to every evaluator — each specialist needs the full picture. (4) Accept an evaluation conducted entirely in English if your child's primary language is different. (5) Skip sharing your videos — evaluators can't observe everything in a clinic setting.

RISKS: ★ EVALUATION RISKS: (1) Evaluator not autism-qualified — may miss diagnosis or apply incorrect criteria. (2) ADOS-2 may miss girls/high-masking children — they score better on social items due to learned social strategies. (3) Bilingual child evaluated only in English — scores artificially depressed. (4) Evaluation conducted in unfamiliar clinical setting — child may perform differently than at home. (5) Evaluator relies on standardized tests only — misses real-world functional challenges. (6) Parent interview not thorough enough — evaluator doesn't understand home context.

RESOURCES: UNDERSTANDING YOUR CHILD'S EVALUATION:
• Autism Speaks 100 Day Kit: autismspeaks.org/tool-kit
• Undivided evaluation guide: undivided.io
• ASHA parent resources on evaluation: asha.org/public
• DRC guide to RC evaluations: disabilityrightsca.org
PARENT TRAINING ON ADVOCACY:
• Matrix Parent Network: matrixparents.org
• TASK: taskca.org — free workshops on evaluation and IEP

PRIMARY AGENCY: Regional Center
OTHER AGENCIES: RC-contracted evaluators
