insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 2.4: Eligibility Determination (RC Decision)

Autism — Step 2.4: Eligibility Determination (RC Decision)

WHAT TO DO: PREPARE FOR ELIGIBILITY MEETING: (1) Request copies of ALL evaluation reports at least 5 days before the meeting. (2) Review reports carefully — look for: DSM-5 autism diagnosis (299.00), severity level (Level 1/2/3), adaptive behavior scores (Vineland-3 composite), cognitive scores, speech-language findings. (3) Write a summary of any disagreements or corrections to the reports. (4) You may bring a support person (advocate, family member, friend) to the meeting. (5) If child is found eligible: immediately ask about service coordinator assignment, IPP scheduling, and interim services. (6) If child is found NOT eligible: do NOT accept this without understanding why and knowing your appeal rights.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: 2 weeks before eligibility meeting, call your RC contact: 'I'd like copies of all evaluation reports mailed/emailed to me at least 5 business days before the eligibility meeting.'
STEP 2: Review all reports. Highlight anything you disagree with. Note your questions.
STEP 3: At the meeting, take notes (or bring someone to take notes). Ask: 'Based on these evaluations, is my child eligible for Regional Center services under the autism category?'
STEP 4: IF ELIGIBLE: Ask: 'Who is my Service Coordinator? When will we schedule the IPP meeting? Can we begin receiving services immediately?' (RC must develop IPP within 60 days.)
STEP 5: IF DENIED: Say: 'I disagree with this determination. Please provide me with a written notice of denial with the specific reasons, and information about my fair hearing rights.' You have 30 days to request a fair hearing.

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER: Your assigned intake coordinator or service coordinator.
IF DENIED — APPEAL HELP:
• OCRA (Office of Clients' Rights Advocacy): (800) 390-7032 — FREE legal help with RC appeals
• Disability Rights California: (800) 776-5746
• Fair Hearing request: File with DDS Office of Administrative Hearings
FOR ADVOCACY SUPPORT:
• Undivided: undivided.io (free navigator)
• Your local Family Resource Center: frcnca.org

DOCUMENTS: RC PROVIDES AT ELIGIBILITY MEETING:
• Eligibility determination letter
• All evaluation reports (you should already have these)
• Rights notification
• If eligible: Service Coordinator assignment
• If denied: Written denial with reasons and fair hearing rights

PARENT SHOULD HAVE:
• Copies of all evaluation reports (reviewed)
• Your questions/disagreements written down
• Parent Concerns Letter (updated)
• Support person if desired

TIMELINE: 120 CALENDAR DAYS from initial referral to eligibility determination (Lanterman Act mandate). If RC misses this deadline: call OCRA at (800) 390-7032. RC must provide written determination. If eligible, IPP meeting within 60 days of eligibility.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Go to the eligibility meeting without having read the reports first. (2) Accept denial without requesting a written explanation (Prior Written Notice equivalent). (3) Assume denial is final — you have appeal rights including fair hearing. (4) Sign anything you don't understand — ask for time to review. (5) Agree to 'provisional eligibility' without understanding what it means for services.

RISKS: ★ ELIGIBILITY RISKS: (1) Denied eligibility despite clinical diagnosis — RC uses narrower criteria than DSM-5 in some cases. (2) 'Provisional eligibility' for ages 3-4 — may be used to delay full services. (3) Service Coordinator assigned with 60-80 caseload — limited attention to your child. (4) POS spending disparities: White families receive ~2x more in services than Black/Latino families. (5) Family doesn't understand the difference between RC eligibility and school IEP eligibility — BOTH are needed.

RESOURCES: RC RIGHTS AND APPEALS:
• OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — free attorney assistance
• DRC Fair Hearing Guide: disabilityrightsca.org
• SERR Manual Chapter on RC: serr.disabilityrightsca.org
• Lanterman Act full text: leginfo.legislature.ca.gov
PEER SUPPORT:
• Parents of autistic children who've been through RC: connect through FRCNCA, Undivided, or local autism parent groups

PRIMARY AGENCY: Regional Center
OTHER AGENCIES: DDS (oversight)
