insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 3.1: IPP Development (Initial Service Plan)

Autism — Step 3.1: IPP Development (Initial Service Plan)

WHAT TO DO: MAXIMIZE YOUR IPP: (1) IPP (Individual Program Plan) is your child's service plan at RC — equivalent to an IEP at school but for community/home services. (2) Before the IPP meeting, make a written list of EVERYTHING your child needs: ABA therapy (hours per week), speech therapy, occupational therapy, respite care, parent training, social skills, adaptive behavior support, crisis services. (3) Research what services RC CAN provide (visit dds.ca.gov or ask other parents). (4) At the meeting, present your list and advocate for each item. (5) IPP goals should be SPECIFIC, MEASURABLE, and TIME-BOUND. (6) Know that RC POS (Purchase of Service) spending should be equitable — if you're a family of color, you may need to advocate harder due to documented spending disparities.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: 2 weeks before IPP, prepare a written 'Parent Service Request' listing: requested services, hours/frequency, justification for each. Example: 'Requesting 20 hours/week ABA therapy — evaluation documents significant behavioral and adaptive skill deficits requiring intensive intervention.'
STEP 2: At IPP meeting, present your list. For each item, ask: 'Will this service be included in the IPP? If not, please document the reason for denial in writing.'
STEP 3: Review proposed IPP goals. For each goal, ask: 'How will we measure progress? What is the specific target? By when?'
STEP 4: Before signing, ask: 'Can I take this home to review? I have 30 days to sign.' Review with an advocate or other parents.
STEP 5: Once IPP is signed, ask: 'How quickly will services begin? Who are the approved providers? Can I choose my ABA company?'

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER SERVICE COORDINATOR: Your assigned SC manages your IPP — get their direct number and email.
ABA PROVIDERS: RC provides a vendor list. Ask for multiple options and interview providers before choosing.
RESPITE AGENCIES: RC provides vendor list for respite workers.
IF SC IS UNRESPONSIVE: Contact RC supervisor. If still unresponsive, contact OCRA: (800) 390-7032.
POS SPENDING DATA: dds.ca.gov/rc/purchase-of-service-data — check how your RC spends relative to demographics.

DOCUMENTS: IPP DOCUMENTS:
• Written IPP (your copy — keep in file)
• Service authorization letters for each service
• Provider vendor list
• POS codes for authorized services
• Parent rights notification

PARENT SHOULD PREPARE:
• Written Service Request letter
• List of requested services with justifications
• Questions about each proposed goal
• Research on available services

TIMELINE: IPP meeting within 60 DAYS of eligibility determination. IPP reviewed at least ANNUALLY. Parent can request IPP meeting at ANY time if needs change. Services should begin within 30 days of signed IPP (though ABA waitlists may cause delays).

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept whatever the SC offers without advocating for what your child needs. (2) Agree to vague goals ('improve communication') — demand measurable goals ('Child will use 50 functional words in 3 months'). (3) Let RC say 'school will provide that' for services RC should fund (e.g., ABA, respite, parent training). (4) Sign the IPP if you disagree — you can request changes and have 30 days to sign. (5) Assume the SC will proactively offer all available services — many families don't receive services they're entitled to because they don't know to ask.

RISKS: ★ IPP SERVICE GAPS: (1) SC doesn't inform parent of all available services — parent gets only what SC offers, not what child needs. (2) POS spending disparities well-documented — White families receive ~$14K more in services per year than Latino families at many RCs. (3) ABA hours approved at lower level than recommended. (4) Respite denied or given minimal hours. (5) Parent training not offered. (6) IPP goals too vague to measure progress. (7) SC caseload 60-80 clients — limited time for advocacy.

RESOURCES: IPP PREPARATION:
• Undivided IPP guide: undivided.io
• DRC IPP rights guide: disabilityrightsca.org
• SERR Manual: serr.disabilityrightsca.org
• POS spending equity data: dds.ca.gov
PARENT TRAINING ON ADVOCACY:
• Matrix Parent Network: matrixparents.org
• TASK: taskca.org
• Autism Society of America: autism-society.org
• Local ABA company parent training programs

PRIMARY AGENCY: Regional Center
(Service Coordinator)
OTHER AGENCIES: Therapy providers
