insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 4.1: Request School District Evaluation (Written Request)

Autism — Step 4.1: Request School District Evaluation (Written Request)

WHAT TO DO: SEND WRITTEN EVALUATION REQUEST: (1) Write a letter to your school district's Special Education Department requesting a comprehensive evaluation. This triggers legal timelines. (2) Be SPECIFIC about areas of concern: social communication, restricted/repetitive behaviors, sensory processing, adaptive behavior, academic readiness, speech-language, fine/gross motor. (3) Hand-deliver the letter AND send by email — keep copies of both with date stamps. (4) District must respond with an assessment plan within 15 CALENDAR DAYS. (5) You do NOT need the district's permission, teacher's agreement, or to wait for RTI. (6) Share RC evaluation reports with the school — they're required to consider them.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Write your evaluation request letter. Include: child's name, DOB, school, your specific concerns, request for 'comprehensive psychoeducational evaluation in all areas of suspected disability including but not limited to: cognitive, academic, speech-language, occupational therapy, social-emotional, adaptive behavior, and autism-specific assessment.'
STEP 2: Hand-deliver to school office AND email to Special Education Director. Get a date-stamped receipt.
STEP 3: Mark calendar: 15 days from delivery = deadline for assessment plan. If no plan received, send follow-up: 'I sent my evaluation request on [date]. Under California law, you have 15 calendar days to provide an assessment plan. Please respond immediately.'
STEP 4: Review assessment plan. If it doesn't include all areas, write back: 'I request the following areas be added to the assessment plan: [list areas].'
STEP 5: Sign the plan only after all requested areas are included. 60-day assessment clock starts when you sign.

CONTACT: SCHOOL DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION DEPARTMENT: Find contact at your district's website under 'Special Education' or 'Student Services.'
YOUR LOCAL SCHOOL DISTRICT: Special Education, 1000 Broadway Suite 680, California, CA 94607. Phone: your district SpEd office. Email: specialeducation@your local school district.org
SELPA (Special Education Local Plan Area): Your district's SELPA handles dispute resolution.
FREE ADVOCACY:
• Disability Rights California: (800) 776-5746
• DRC SERR Manual: serr.disabilityrightsca.org
• Community Alliance for Special Education (CASE): caseadvocacy.org (Bay Area)
• DREDF: dredf.org

DOCUMENTS: PARENT CREATES:
• Written evaluation request letter (dated, keep copy)
• Date-stamped delivery confirmation
• Email copy with read receipt

DISTRICT PROVIDES:
• Assessment plan (within 15 days)
• Parent rights notification (procedural safeguards)
• Prior Written Notice if refusing evaluation

SHARE WITH DISTRICT:
• RC evaluation reports
• Private evaluation reports
• Medical records
• Parent Concerns Letter

TIMELINE: 15 CALENDAR DAYS: District must provide assessment plan after receiving written request.
60 CALENDAR DAYS: From signed consent to completed evaluation (excluding school breaks >5 days).
30 CALENDAR DAYS: From completed evaluation to IEP meeting.
NO DEADLINE TO REQUEST: Parent can request evaluation at any time during the school year.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Make a verbal request only — it MUST be in writing to trigger legal timelines. (2) Let district say 'wait until next year' or 'let's try the classroom first.' (3) Accept an assessment plan that doesn't cover ALL areas of suspected disability. (4) Sign the assessment plan without reviewing it — you can request additions. (5) Assume school IEP replaces RC services — these are SEPARATE systems.

RISKS: ★ SCHOOL BARRIERS: (1) District delays response beyond 15 days. (2) Assessment plan too narrow — misses OT, adaptive behavior, or autism-specific testing. (3) District says 'we need RTI data first' — ILLEGAL to delay parent-requested evaluation for RTI. (4) Parent makes verbal request only — no paper trail. (5) District doesn't share assessment plan or parent rights. (6) District doesn't accept RC evaluations. (7) Assessment not conducted in child's primary language.

RESOURCES: EVALUATION REQUEST HELP:
• CASE (Community Alliance for Special Education): caseadvocacy.org — free advocacy, Bay Area
• DREDF: dredf.org — free advocacy
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — free legal help
• TASK: taskca.org — free parent training
• Matrix Parent Network: matrixparents.org — Bay Area IEP training
• Wrightslaw: wrightslaw.com — comprehensive SpEd law resource
• Understood: understood.org — parent-friendly IEP guides

PRIMARY AGENCY: Local School District
OTHER AGENCIES: Regional Center
