insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 4.2: School District Evaluation & IEP Development

Autism — Step 4.2: School District Evaluation & IEP Development

WHAT TO DO: PREPARE FOR AND PARTICIPATE IN EVALUATION AND IEP: (1) School psychologist conducts evaluation (cognitive, academic, social-emotional, adaptive). SLP evaluates communication. OT evaluates sensory/motor. (2) Provide school with RC reports, private evaluations, and your documentation. (3) After evaluation is complete, REQUEST REPORTS 5 days before IEP meeting. Review carefully. (4) At IEP meeting, advocate for: autism eligibility category, speech therapy, OT, behavioral support (BIP if needed), 1:1 aide if appropriate, specialized academic instruction, ESY (extended school year), and APPROPRIATE placement (LRE). (5) Bring a support person — advocate, spouse, friend who takes notes. (6) If you disagree with the IEP offer, you do NOT have to sign — request a continuation meeting.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: After evaluation, call school: 'When is the IEP meeting scheduled? I'd like copies of all reports at least 5 business days before.'
STEP 2: Read reports. Highlight areas of concern and disagreement.
STEP 3: Write an IEP preparation letter listing: your priorities, requested services, placement preference, specific goals you want addressed.
STEP 4: At IEP meeting: introduce your support person, present your priorities, review each goal, ask about placement options.
STEP 5: For each proposed goal, ask: 'How will this be measured? How often? What happens if my child doesn't make progress?'
STEP 6: If satisfied, you may sign. If not: 'I'd like to take this home to review. When can we schedule a follow-up meeting?'
STEP 7: After IEP is signed, confirm: 'When do services begin? Who are the providers? How will I receive progress reports?'

CONTACT: SCHOOL DISTRICT IEP TEAM: Teacher, school psychologist, special ed coordinator, SLP, OT, and parent.
ADVOCACY SUPPORT:
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free IEP meeting advocate (Bay Area)
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• TASK: taskca.org — IEP training and coaching
• Matrix Parent Network: matrixparents.org
• Wrightslaw: wrightslaw.com
IF DISPUTE:
• CDE Complaint: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp
• Mediation/Due Process: OAH at dgs.ca.gov/OAH
• SELPA for your district

DOCUMENTS: IEP DOCUMENTS (district provides):
• IEP document (full written plan)
• Assessment reports (psych, SLP, OT, etc.)
• Prior Written Notice of any decisions
• Parent rights (Procedural Safeguards)
• Behavioral Intervention Plan (BIP) if applicable
• ESY eligibility determination
• Placement offer letter

PARENT SHOULD BRING:
• RC IPP (for coordination)
• Parent Concerns Letter
• Requested services list
• Prior evaluations
• Work samples/videos

TIMELINE: 30 CALENDAR DAYS from completed evaluation to IEP meeting. IEP reviewed ANNUALLY. Triennial re-evaluation every 3 years. Parent can request IEP meeting at ANY time.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Go to the IEP meeting without reading reports first. (2) Go alone — bring support. (3) Accept an IEP that doesn't match your child's evaluation results. (4) Accept vague goals. (5) Accept a placement without visiting the classroom first. (6) Sign the IEP if you disagree — you can request time to review. (7) Forget to coordinate IEP services with RC IPP services.

RISKS: ★ IEP RISKS: (1) School offers minimal services. (2) Goals not measurable or ambitious enough. (3) Placement too restrictive (segregated) OR not enough support (dumped in gen ed without services). (4) No coordination between IEP and RC IPP. (5) Parent overwhelmed in meeting. (6) ESY denied despite regression evidence. (7) BIP based on compliance rather than understanding function of behavior.

RESOURCES: IEP PREPARATION:
• CASE IEP training: caseadvocacy.org
• Wrightslaw IEP guide: wrightslaw.com/info/iep.index.htm
• Understood IEP guide: understood.org
• TASK IEP workshops: taskca.org
• DRC IEP rights: disabilityrightsca.org
• Matrix IEP training: matrixparents.org
BOOKS:
• 'From Emotions to Advocacy' by Pam Wright
• 'The Complete IEP Guide' by Lawrence Siegel

PRIMARY AGENCY: Local School District
OTHER AGENCIES: Regional Center
