insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$Autism Step 4.3: IEP Dispute Resolution (If Needed)

Autism — Step 4.3: IEP Dispute Resolution (If Needed)

WHAT TO DO: IF YOU DISAGREE WITH THE IEP: (1) First try informal resolution — request another IEP meeting. Put disagreements in writing. (2) If informal fails, you have 4 formal options: (a) CDE State Complaint: file at cde.ca.gov — CDE investigates within 60 days. Free, no attorney needed. (b) Mediation: voluntary, conducted by OAH mediator. Free. Often resolves disputes. (c) Due Process Hearing: formal legal proceeding through OAH. 45 days to hearing. Attorney strongly recommended. (d) ADR (Alternative Dispute Resolution): your district may offer this. (3) You may request IEE (Independent Educational Evaluation) at district expense if you disagree with their evaluation. (4) Consider contacting DRC for free legal assistance — especially for low-income families.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Put your disagreement in writing: 'I disagree with [specific aspect] of the IEP proposed on [date]. I request [specific remedy].'
STEP 2: Request another IEP meeting to try to resolve informally.
STEP 3: If no resolution, choose your path:
  • CDE Complaint: File at cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — free online form.
  • Mediation: Request through OAH at dgs.ca.gov/OAH — free, often effective.
  • Due Process: File at OAH — strongly recommend consulting DRC first: (800) 776-5746.
STEP 4: Throughout, DOCUMENT EVERYTHING — save emails, keep notes of conversations with dates.

CONTACT: CDE COMPLIANCE COMPLAINT: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp (online filing)
OAH (OFFICE OF ADMINISTRATIVE HEARINGS): dgs.ca.gov/OAH — for mediation and due process. Phone: (916) 263-0880.
DISABILITY RIGHTS CALIFORNIA: (800) 776-5746 — free legal help, priority for low-income families.
CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free advocacy support (Bay Area)
DREDF: dredf.org — free legal advocacy
SELPA: Contact your district's SELPA for local dispute resolution.
SPECIAL ED ATTORNEYS (if needed): CASE, DRC, or Education Law referral through your local bar association.

DOCUMENTS: DISPUTE DOCUMENTS:
• Written disagreement letter (dated)
• Prior Written Notice request
• CDE complaint form (if filing)
• OAH mediation/due process request
• IEE request letter (if applicable)
• Complete documentation binder (all IEP records, evaluations, correspondence)

TIMELINES:
• CDE complaint: 60 days to investigate
• Due process: hearing within 45 days of filing
• Statute of limitations: 2 years from known violation
• IEE: District must respond (pay or file due process)

TIMELINE: CDE complaint: 60 days to investigate. Mediation: scheduled within weeks. Due process: hearing within 45 days of filing. 2-YEAR statute of limitations on complaints. IEE: district must respond within reasonable time.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Give up because you're intimidated by the process. (2) File due process without trying other options first (it's adversarial and stressful). (3) Miss the 2-year statute of limitations for filing. (4) Accept retaliation — it's illegal for the district to retaliate against you for advocating. (5) Sign an IEP you disagree with — you lose leverage.

RISKS: ★ Parent doesn't know appeal rights. District pressures parent to sign. Attorney fees $300-600/hr prohibitive. Emotional toll of adversarial process. 2-year statute of limitations passes. District retaliates (illegal but happens).

RESOURCES: DISPUTE RESOLUTION TRAINING:
• Wrightslaw: wrightslaw.com/info/disres.index.htm
• DRC: disabilityrightsca.org
• CASE advocacy training: caseadvocacy.org
• CDE dispute resolution info: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/
• CADRE (Consortium for Dispute Resolution): directionservice.org/cadre

PRIMARY AGENCY: School District /
CDE / OAH
OTHER AGENCIES: DRC
Special Ed Attorney
