insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Journey Map — Overview

PDA (PATHOLOGICAL DEMAND AVOIDANCE) — Complete Family Navigation Journey (California)

19 steps across 7 phases:
* 1 Recognition & 'Lightbulb Moment': Parent Recognizes Atypical Demand Avoidance Patter, Parent Discovers PDA Framework
* 2 Seeking Diagnosis (Through ASD Pathway): Pursue Autism/ASD Evaluation (Primary Pathway), Comprehensive Evaluation — PDA-Informed, Navigate Diagnostic Language (No PDA Diagnosis Ava
* 3 Service Planning (PDA-Informed): IPP/Service Plan — RC (If ASD Eligible), Low-Demand Parenting & Therapeutic Approach
* 4 School Navigation (IEP/504): Request School District Assessment, School District Evaluation — PDA-Aware Approach, IEP Development — PDA-Specific Accommodations, 504 Plan Alternative (If IEP Denied), IEP/504 Dispute Resolution (If Needed)
* 5 Therapy & Intervention (PDA-Informed): Find PDA-Affirming Therapists & Providers, Insurance Authorization for PDA-Informed Therapy
* 6 School Avoidance & Crisis Management: Address School Avoidance / School Refusal, Manage PDA Crisis Episodes (Meltdowns/Shutdowns)
* 7 Long-Term Planning & Transition: Ongoing IEP/504 Review — PDA-Specific Monitoring, Transition Planning (Age 14+), Adult PDA — Ongoing Support & Self-Advocacy$kb$, $kb$journey_pda$kb$, $kb$jrn-pda-overview$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-pda-overview","title":"PDA Journey Map — Overview","category":"journey-pda","subcategory":"overview","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 1.1: Parent Recognizes Atypical Demand Avoidance Pattern

PDA — Step 1.1: Parent Recognizes Atypical Demand Avoidance Pattern

WHAT TO DO: TRACK AND DOCUMENT THE PDA BEHAVIORAL PATTERN: (1) PDA is NOT ordinary defiance — it's an anxiety-driven, pervasive avoidance of everyday demands that includes ANY expectation, even fun activities. Keep a DEMAND DIARY: for 2 weeks, log every demand that triggers avoidance, the child's specific response, and what worked/didn't. (2) Note PDA-specific markers: avoidance of routine demands (getting dressed, eating meals, leaving the house), social manipulation strategies (distraction, excuses, physical incapacitation claims, negotiation), surface sociability with difficulty sustaining friendships, extreme mood variability, comfort in role-play/fantasy/controlling play, equal avoidance of preferred AND non-preferred activities. (3) Document the ANXIETY underneath: your child is not choosing defiance — their nervous system perceives demands as threats. (4) Video-record episodes when safe to do so — clinicians rarely see PDA presentations in office settings because the novelty of the visit masks patterns. (5) Track what REDUCES avoidance: indirect language ('I wonder if...'), autonomy, novelty, humor, collaborative problem-solving. (6) Rate the demand avoidance on a 0-10 scale daily. (7) Prevalence: PDA affects an estimated 2-5% of all autistic individuals — it's uncommon but well-documented in research, particularly from the UK (Elizabeth Newson, Phil Christie).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Learn to recognize PDA patterns. Key signs: (a) Resists and avoids ordinary demands of daily life (getting dressed, eating, leaving the house), (b) Uses 'social strategies' to avoid — distraction, negotiation, excuses, withdrawal, (c) Appears comfortable with peers but driven by need for control, (d) Can be charming and socially aware when not in demand situations, (e) May mask all day at school then meltdown upon returning home, (f) Severe distress when faced with direct demands — even positive ones like 'Let's go to the park!'
STEP 2: Start a PDA-specific behavior journal. Record: date, demand or trigger, child's avoidance strategy (distraction, negotiation, refusal, meltdown), outcome, what helped reduce distress. Track patterns — what triggers are worst? What approaches reduce distress?
STEP 3: Take the PDA screening tool: Extreme Demand Avoidance Questionnaire (EDA-Q) — available free at pdanorthamerica.org. Note: this is a screening tool, not a diagnosis.
STEP 4: Join PDA parent communities immediately for peer support: PDA North America (pdanorthamerica.org), PDA Parents Facebook group, PDA Society UK (pdasociety.org.uk).
STEP 5: Begin reading about PDA: 'The Declarative Language Handbook' (Linda Murphy), 'Low Demand Parenting' (Amanda Diekman), PDA Society practice guides.

CONTACT: PDA NORTH AMERICA: pdanorthamerica.org — screening tools, provider directory, parent community, webinars
PDA SOCIETY UK: pdasociety.org.uk — comprehensive practice guidance (most established PDA resource worldwide)
LOW DEMAND PARENTING: lowdemandparenting.com — Amanda Diekman's resources, coaching
EDA-Q SCREENING: Available at pdanorthamerica.org
FINDING PDA-INFORMED PROFESSIONALS IN CA:
• PDANA provider directory: pdanorthamerica.org/find-help
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com — search 'PDA' or 'pathological demand avoidance'
• Ask PDA parent Facebook groups for CA-specific provider recommendations

DOCUMENTS: BEGIN CREATING:
• PDA behavior journal (triggers, strategies, outcomes)
• EDA-Q screening results
• Video recordings of demand avoidance episodes (CRITICAL evidence for future evaluations)
• School communication records
• Daily routine log showing where demands trigger distress
• List of strategies tried and their outcomes

DOCUMENTS TO COLLECT:
• Any prior behavioral evaluations
• School behavior reports
• Pediatrician notes on behavior concerns

TIMELINE: Ongoing — earlier recognition = earlier appropriate support. Many parents spend years on wrong interventions.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Assume your child is 'just being defiant' or 'manipulative' — PDA is driven by anxiety, not willful disobedience. Traditional behavioral approaches (reward charts, consequences, time-outs) typically INCREASE demand avoidance and distress. (2) Apply rigid behavioral frameworks — ABA, PBIS, or 'first-then' boards often backfire with PDA profiles by increasing perceived demands. (3) Compare to typical autism presentations — PDA children are often highly social, use masking, and may not 'look autistic' to untrained observers. (4) Blame yourself — PDA is a neurological profile, not a parenting failure. (5) Seek advice from professionals unfamiliar with PDA — they will likely recommend strategies that make things worse. (6) Wait for school to notice — PDA children often 'hold it toge

RISKS: ★ CRITICAL PDA TRAP: Child is misdiagnosed as ODD (Oppositional Defiant Disorder) or as having 'behavioral problems.' Traditional behavioral interventions (reward/consequence) escalate conflict. Parents blamed for 'poor parenting.' Child may mask at school and collapse at home ('after-school restraint collapse'). PDA not in DSM-5 — most U.S. clinicians unfamiliar with it.

