PDA — Step 1.2: Parent Discovers PDA Framework

WHAT TO DO: EDUCATE YOURSELF ON PDA BEFORE SEEKING EVALUATION: (1) PDA (Pathological Demand Avoidance) is a behavioral profile within the autism spectrum first described by Elizabeth Newson (1983, University of Nottingham). It's widely recognized in UK and increasingly in US clinical practice, but NOT a standalone DSM-5 diagnosis. (2) Read the foundational resources: PDA Society (pdasociety.org.uk) — the authoritative source; 'Understanding Pathological Demand Avoidance Syndrome in Children' by Phil Christie et al.; 'The PDA Paradox' by Harry Thompson (adult PDA). (3) Take the EDA-Q (Extreme Demand Avoidance Questionnaire) — a research-validated screening tool available at PDA Society website. Score 50+ suggests PDA profile. (4) Join PDA parent communities: PDA Society forum, PDA North America Facebook group (largest US group, 15K+ members), PDA Parents & Carers Facebook group. (5) CRITICAL: Learn LOW-DEMAND strategies NOW — don't wait for diagnosis. Traditional behavioral approaches (reward charts, consequences, ABA compliance training) typically WORSEN PDA. (6) Document your child's profile against the PDA criteria: demand avoidance, social strategies, lability of mood, role play/fantasy, surface sociability. (7) Prepare a '1-page PDA profile' summarizing your child's presentation — you'll use this with every provider.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Deep dive into PDA education. Priority resources: (a) PDA Society practice guidance: pdasociety.org.uk/what-is-pda/practice-guidance — the gold standard, (b) PDA North America: pdanorthamerica.org — U.S.-adapted information, (c) Laura Kerbey's PDA webinars (PDANA website).
STEP 2: Learn the LOW-DEMAND parenting approach — this is the cornerstone of PDA-informed parenting: reduce demands, use indirect language, offer choices, collaborate rather than direct, pick your battles ruthlessly.
STEP 3: Learn declarative language — replace commands ('Put on your shoes') with observations ('I notice it's time to go') and wonderings ('I wonder if we need shoes?'). Read 'The Declarative Language Handbook.'
STEP 4: Map the U.S. service pathway: PDA → Autism evaluation → ASD diagnosis (if meets criteria) → Regional Center IPP + School IEP. PDA characteristics documented in the evaluation support accommodation requests.
STEP 5: Begin preparing for evaluation by documenting PDA-specific characteristics (see Step 2.1).

CONTACT: PDA EDUCATION:
• PDA North America: pdanorthamerica.org — U.S.-focused resources, provider directory
• PDA Society UK: pdasociety.org.uk — practice guidance, professional courses
• Low Demand Parenting: lowdemandparenting.com
CA SERVICE PATHWAY:
• Regional Center: dds.ca.gov/rc — find your local RC for autism evaluation
• School District: your district's Special Education office
• DISABILITY RIGHTS CA: (800) 776-5746 — legal rights guidance
ONLINE COMMUNITIES:
• PDA Parents Facebook group (largest global PDA parent community)
• PDANA private community
• Reddit: r/PDA

DOCUMENTS: PDA EDUCATION PORTFOLIO:
• PDA Society practice guidance (downloaded/printed)
• EDA-Q results and interpretation
• Your PDA behavior journal (ongoing)
• List of PDA-specific characteristics your child shows
• Videos of demand avoidance episodes
• Notes on strategies tried (traditional vs low-demand) and outcomes

FOR FUTURE EVALUATORS:
• Prepare a 1-page 'PDA Profile Summary' for your child:
  - Demand avoidance patterns
  - Social strategies used to avoid
  - Anxiety presentations
  - Masking behaviors
  - What makes things worse
  - What reduces distress

TIMELINE: N/A — self-education phase

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Present PDA as a 'diagnosis' to schools or providers — PDA is NOT recognized in the DSM-5. In the U.S., there is no PDA diagnosis code. Instead, the pathway is through autism/ASD evaluation with PDA characteristics described. (2) Dismiss professionals who haven't heard of PDA — educate them gently. Most U.S. clinicians were not trained on PDA. (3) Use PDA as an excuse to avoid all intervention — PDA children still benefit from support, just through low-demand approaches. (4) Rely solely on UK-based guidance without adapting to U.S./CA systems — the service pathways are very different. (5) Overwhelm yourself trying to learn everything at once — PDA parenting is a marathon, not a sprint.

RISKS: Most U.S. clinicians don't know about PDA. Parents may encounter skepticism from professionals. UK research is more advanced than U.S. Parents blamed as 'internet paediatricians' for raising PDA. No standardized PDA assessment tool in widespread clinical use (EDA-Q under development). PDA not in DSM-5 or ICD-11.

