PDA — Step 2.1: Pursue Autism/ASD Evaluation (Primary Pathway)

WHAT TO DO: PURSUE ASD EVALUATION AS PRIMARY DIAGNOSTIC PATHWAY: (1) PDA is NOT a standalone DSM-5 diagnosis — your child's diagnostic pathway is through ASD (Autism Spectrum Disorder). (2) Your child may present atypically for autism: they may be socially motivated, make eye contact, use advanced language, and engage in imaginative play — all of which can MASK autism in traditional screening. (3) Request evaluation through TWO parallel pathways: (a) Regional Center — self-refer for developmental evaluation, no doctor referral needed, call your local RC intake line; (b) School district — written request for assessment in all areas of suspected disability. (4) When requesting evaluation, say: 'I'm concerned about autism spectrum features including pervasive demand avoidance, anxiety, social difficulties, and emotional dysregulation. My child may present atypically — they have surface sociability but struggle with genuine reciprocal friendship and demand tolerance.' (5) Ask specifically for evaluators experienced with: masking/camouflaging autism, atypical autism presentations, PDA profile, girls/women on the spectrum (PDA is often missed in girls). (6) Bring your Demand Diary, video recordings, EDA-Q results, and 1-page PDA profile to every evaluation. (7) CA Regional Centers: dds.ca.gov/rc/listing — find your local RC by county.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Contact your local Regional Center and request an autism evaluation. Say: 'I'd like to refer my child for a comprehensive developmental evaluation. I have concerns about autism with a demand avoidance profile.' RC evaluations are FREE regardless of income or insurance.
STEP 2: Simultaneously, pursue private evaluation if possible. Find a PDA-informed evaluator: check PDANA directory (pdanorthamerica.org/find-help), ask PDA parent groups for CA recommendations.
STEP 3: CRITICAL PREPARATION — write a detailed Parent Concerns Letter that specifically describes PDA characteristics: (a) pervasive demand avoidance across settings, (b) social strategies to avoid (negotiation, distraction, charm), (c) anxiety-driven behavior NOT willful defiance, (d) comfort with peers but need for control, (e) masking at school / meltdowns at home, (f) traditional behavioral approaches failed or worsened behavior.
STEP 4: Provide your PDA behavior journal and videos to the evaluator BEFORE the evaluation appointment.
STEP 5: Ask the evaluator: 'Are you familiar with the PDA profile? Will you use assessments that capture anxiety-driven demand avoidance rather than just social communication deficits?'
STEP 6: If RC evaluation doesn't capture PDA: request a second opinion through a private neuropsychologist. If you disagree with RC's findings, you have the right to appeal.

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER: Find yours at dds.ca.gov/rc — all 21 CA Regional Centers. Self-refer by phone. FREE evaluation regardless of income.
PDA-INFORMED EVALUATORS:
• PDANA directory: pdanorthamerica.org/find-help
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com — search autism, PDA, demand avoidance
• UC Davis MIND Institute: health.ucdavis.edu/mindinstitute — (916) 703-0280
• Ask PDA parent Facebook groups for CA-specific provider names
IF RC DENIES AUTISM:
• OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — clients' rights advocacy
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — appeal guidance
• Request a fair hearing through RC
PRIVATE EVALUATION:
• Neuropsychologists ($3K-$8K) — check insurance for 'neurodevelopmental testing' benefit

DOCUMENTS: PREPARE FOR EVALUATOR:
• Parent Concerns Letter — PDA-specific (see template in Step 6)
• PDA behavior journal with dates and examples
• Video recordings of demand avoidance episodes and meltdowns
• EDA-Q screening results
• Prior evaluations (behavioral, school, medical)
• School records (report cards, behavior reports, teacher comments)
• List of interventions tried and outcomes (esp. traditional vs low-demand)
• Daily routine challenges document

EVALUATOR WILL PRODUCE:
• Comprehensive evaluation report
• Autism/ASD diagnosis (if criteria met) — diagnostic code to use
• Recommendations for services and accommodations
• Assessment instruments used and scores

TIMELINE: RC: 120 days from referral to eligibility. Private dev ped: 6-12 month wait. Private neuropsych: 2-8 weeks.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Ask for a 'PDA evaluation' — ask for a 'comprehensive autism/ASD evaluation with specific attention to anxiety-driven demand avoidance.' (2) Accept a brief screener (M-CHAT, CARS) as sufficient — PDA children often PASS standard autism screens because they are socially engaged. ADOS-2 may also miss PDA children who mask effectively. (3) Accept evaluation from a provider unfamiliar with female autism presentations or PDA — masking is extremely common and can lead to false negatives. (4) Assume one evaluation attempt is final — if your child was not diagnosed but you believe PDA/autism is present, pursue a second opinion. (5) Let school-based evaluation replace a clinical autism evaluation — school evaluates for educational impact, not clinical diagnosis.

RISKS: ★ MAJOR RISK: Standard autism assessments (ADOS-2) may MISS PDA children. Children with PDA are often 'socially oriented' — they make eye contact, use social language, engage socially — which can mask autism on standardized tests. Evaluator unfamiliar with PDA may conclude 'not autistic.' Masking during evaluation means child appears more capable than they are. Female/AFAB children especially under-identified.

