PDA — Step 2.2: Comprehensive Evaluation — PDA-Informed

WHAT TO DO: ENSURE THE EVALUATION IS PDA-INFORMED: (1) Standard autism evaluations (ADOS-2) may MISS PDA — children with PDA often perform well in structured, novel clinical settings because the novelty reduces demand avoidance temporarily. (2) Request evaluators who understand: camouflaging/masking, PDA behavioral profile, atypical autism presentations, the role of anxiety in avoidance. (3) CRITICAL: Provide your Demand Diary, video recordings of home behavior, and parent-completed EDA-Q BEFORE the evaluation — these capture what the clinician won't see in their office. (4) For private evaluation: seek a neuropsychologist or developmental psychologist who specifically lists PDA, atypical autism, or demand avoidance in their expertise. Check: PDA North America provider recommendations, Psychology Today filtered by 'autism spectrum.' (5) If ADOS-2 results are borderline/negative but you believe PDA-autism is present: request the evaluator review your home documentation, administer the Diagnostic Interview for Social and Communication Disorders (DISCO — designed to capture PDA), or consider referral to a PDA-knowledgeable specialist. (6) Evaluation should include: cognitive testing, adaptive behavior (Vineland — PDA children often show significant adaptive skill deficits), social-emotional assessment, anxiety measures, sensory profile, and functional behavior analysis focused on demand avoidance. (7) In California, some Regional Centers will accept a 'provisional' ASD diagnosis for childre

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before evaluation, submit your complete PDA documentation package to the evaluator (letter, journal, videos, EDA-Q).
STEP 2: Request multi-session evaluation if possible. PDA children often mask in initial sessions but reveal their profile over time.
STEP 3: Ask evaluator to use assessments beyond ADOS-2 that capture anxiety and demand avoidance: Extreme Demand Avoidance Questionnaire (EDA-Q), Anxiety measures (SCARED, Spence), Sensory Profile, MIGDAS-2 (better for masking profiles), parent and teacher rating scales.
STEP 4: Provide context before testing: 'My child tends to mask in new settings. The demand avoidance and distress you're more likely to see in familiar, routine settings.'
STEP 5: If the evaluator concludes 'does not meet autism criteria,' ask: (a) 'What alternative diagnosis captures my child's demand avoidance and anxiety?' (b) 'Will you document the PDA characteristics observed?' (c) 'Can you provide recommendations for low-demand approaches regardless of diagnostic label?'
STEP 6: Obtain a copy of the full evaluation report. Review for accuracy — does it reflect the PDA profile you see at home?

CONTACT: YOUR EVALUATOR: Get their direct contact information (phone, email) before the evaluation.
RC EVALUATION TEAM: Your service coordinator can tell you who will evaluate.
PRIVATE EVALUATORS (if needed):
• PDANA provider directory: pdanorthamerica.org/find-help
• UC Davis MIND Institute: (916) 703-0280
• Stanford Autism Center: (650) 736-1235
• UCSF Autism & Neurodevelopment: (415) 353-2101
IF EVALUATION MISSES PDA:
• Request second opinion through private neuropsych
• OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — dispute RC evaluation
• DRC: (800) 776-5746

DOCUMENTS: SUBMIT TO EVALUATOR (BEFORE appointment):
• Parent Concerns Letter — PDA-specific
• PDA behavior journal
• Video recordings (CRITICAL for PDA)
• EDA-Q results
• Prior evaluations
• School records

EVALUATION SHOULD INCLUDE:
• Autism-specific: ADOS-2 and/or MIGDAS-2
• Demand avoidance: EDA-Q
• Anxiety: SCARED or Spence Child Anxiety Scale
• Adaptive: Vineland-3
• Cognitive: if indicated
• Sensory: Sensory Profile-2
• Multiple informant: parent + teacher rating scales
• Clinical observation (ideally multiple sessions)

YOU RECEIVE:
• Full evaluation report
• Diagnostic determination
• Recommendations

TIMELINE: 60 days from consent (school district). 120 days from referral (RC). Private: varies.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Rely solely on ADOS-2 — it was designed for 'classic' autism presentations and frequently MISSES PDA profiles, especially in females and children who mask. (2) Accept evaluation in an unfamiliar clinical setting as representative — PDA children may perform well in novel situations (novelty reduces demand anxiety). Request observation in natural settings. (3) Assume 'not meeting autism criteria' means no support — PDA children may qualify under anxiety disorder, or may meet autism criteria with a PDA-informed evaluation approach. (4) Accept a single assessment session — PDA children's presentation varies dramatically by context, demand level, and familiarity. (5) Withhold your PDA documentation from the evaluator — your detailed observations are more diagnostically valuable than

RISKS: Evaluator doesn't screen for PDA traits. EDA-Q not administered. ADOS-2 shows borderline/subclinical because PDA child is socially oriented. Evaluator attributes demand avoidance to ODD, anxiety, or 'behavioral issues.' Significant IQ-adaptive skill gap not explored as PDA indicator. Female/AFAB presentation missed. After-school restraint collapse dismissed because 'child is fine at school.'

