PDA — Step 2.3: Navigate Diagnostic Language (No PDA Diagnosis Available)

WHAT TO DO: NAVIGATE THE DIAGNOSTIC COMPLEXITY STRATEGICALLY: (1) Since PDA is not a DSM-5 diagnosis, your child's formal diagnosis will likely be: ASD (Autism Spectrum Disorder) — primary pathway that unlocks the most services; possibly with added specifiers like 'with associated anxiety' or clinician notes describing the PDA behavioral profile. (2) If ASD diagnosis is given: request the clinician DOCUMENT the PDA profile in their report — 'meets criteria for ASD with a behavioral profile consistent with Pathological Demand Avoidance (PDA), characterized by pervasive demand avoidance driven by anxiety, social manipulation strategies, mood lability, and surface sociability.' (3) If ASD is NOT given: push back with your documentation, request second opinion, or pursue private neuropsychological evaluation. Alternative diagnostic pathways: Anxiety Disorder (GAD, Social Anxiety), ODD (though PDA is NOT ODD — key distinction), or unspecified neurodevelopmental disorder. (4) CRITICAL: Do NOT accept ODD (Oppositional Defiant Disorder) as the sole diagnosis — ODD implies willful defiance and leads to punitive interventions that are harmful for PDA. If ODD is suggested, advocate: 'The avoidance is anxiety-driven, not defiance-driven. I'd like anxiety-based approaches rather than compliance-based ones.' (5) Whatever diagnosis is given, ensure the evaluation report includes detailed behavioral descriptions that will support service access: demand avoidance across settings, anxiety severity, adapti

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Understand the diagnostic landscape: In the U.S./CA, PDA is NOT a separate diagnosis. Options are: (a) ASD (Autism Spectrum Disorder) with notation of demand avoidance/anxiety profile, (b) Generalized Anxiety Disorder or Social Anxiety Disorder, (c) Unspecified Neurodevelopmental Disorder with PDA characteristics described. Each has different service implications.
STEP 2: FOR MAXIMUM SERVICE ACCESS in CA: ASD diagnosis unlocks the most services — Regional Center IPP (ABA, respite, parent training), insurance-funded behavioral therapy (SB 946), and school IEP under Autism eligibility category.
STEP 3: Ask evaluator to document PDA characteristics IN the report regardless of primary diagnosis: 'Please describe the demand avoidance profile, anxiety-driven behavior, masking, and need for low-demand approaches in your report.'
STEP 4: If diagnosed with ASD: proceed to RC and school pathways. If diagnosed with anxiety/other: still pursue school IEP (under OHI or Emotional Disturbance) and insurance-covered therapy.
STEP 5: Keep seeking PDA-informed providers even without ASD diagnosis — the PDA framework guides effective intervention regardless of diagnostic code.

CONTACT: DIAGNOSTIC GUIDANCE:
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — which diagnosis accesses which CA services
• OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — RC eligibility questions
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org — navigating U.S. diagnostic landscape
SERVICE ACCESS BY DIAGNOSIS:
• ASD diagnosis → RC IPP + SB 946 insurance + IEP (Autism)
• Anxiety diagnosis → Insurance therapy + IEP (OHI or ED)
• No diagnosis → 504 Plan (if functional limitation) + private services
SECOND OPINION:
• UC Davis MIND: (916) 703-0280
• Private neuropsychologist through PDANA directory

DOCUMENTS: DIAGNOSTIC DOCUMENTS TO ORGANIZE:
• Complete evaluation report
• Diagnostic code(s) assigned (ICD-10/DSM-5)
• Evaluator's description of PDA characteristics (CRITICAL — highlight these sections)
• Service recommendations from evaluator
• Your PDA Profile Summary (1-page parent document)

CREATE A 'SERVICE ACCESS' DOCUMENT:
• Diagnosis received → services it unlocks
• Regional Center eligibility (Y/N and basis)
• School eligibility category
• Insurance coverage basis
• List of providers to contact next

TIMELINE: Varies by evaluator — some do this readily, others resistant.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Get stuck on the label — the goal is accessing services, not getting a PDA diagnosis code (which doesn't exist in the U.S.). (2) Fight with evaluators who say 'PDA isn't a diagnosis' — they are technically correct in the U.S. system. Instead, ensure PDA CHARACTERISTICS are documented within whatever diagnosis IS given. (3) Accept a diagnosis without PDA characteristics documented — the diagnosis alone won't get PDA-appropriate accommodations. (4) Assume an anxiety diagnosis means you can't also pursue autism — they can co-occur. (5) Give up if the first evaluation doesn't result in a diagnosis — seek a second opinion.

RISKS: ★ DIAGNOSTIC GAP: Evaluator refuses to use PDA language. Child gets ASD diagnosis without PDA documentation, leading to inappropriate intervention recommendations (e.g., traditional ABA). OR child doesn't meet ASD criteria on standard tools and gets NO diagnosis, leaving family with no services. Some evaluators may diagnose ODD instead — leading to punitive interventions that worsen PDA. Insurance and school use diagnosis codes, not profile descriptions.

