PDA — Step 3.1: IPP/Service Plan — RC (If ASD Eligible)

WHAT TO DO: DEVELOP A PDA-APPROPRIATE IPP WITH YOUR REGIONAL CENTER: (1) If your child has an ASD diagnosis, they qualify for Regional Center services under the Lanterman Act — ASD is an automatic qualifying condition. (2) At the IPP (Individual Program Plan) meeting, request services that work FOR PDA, not against it: (a) PDA-informed behavioral consultation (NOT traditional ABA focused on compliance — request Collaborative & Proactive Solutions, low-demand approaches, or Declarative Language strategies), (b) Occupational therapy with sensory integration focus, (c) Parent training in low-demand strategies and PDA-specific approaches, (d) Respite care (PDA parenting is exhausting — respite is critical for family sustainability), (e) Social skills support that respects autonomy (not forced social compliance). (3) AVOID requesting traditional ABA that uses: discrete trial training, token economies, forced compliance, planned ignoring of demand avoidance, or extinction of avoidance behaviors — these increase anxiety and worsen PDA. (4) Request your IPP specifically state: 'PDA profile — services must use low-demand, autonomy-respecting, anxiety-reduction approaches.' (5) Know your POS (Purchase of Service) rights — RC must fund services; if they deny, request Prior Written Notice. (6) SSI, Medi-Cal, IHSS, and CalABLE may all apply — see benefits checklist.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: After ASD diagnosis, contact your Regional Center and request an IPP meeting. Say: 'My child has been diagnosed with ASD with a demand avoidance/PDA profile. I'd like to develop an IPP that includes PDA-informed services.'
STEP 2: Before the IPP meeting, write a 'Service Request Letter' listing: (a) PDA-informed behavioral support (NOT traditional ABA), (b) Parent training in low-demand strategies, (c) Respite care (specify hours/week), (d) Occupational therapy for sensory needs, (e) Social skills group (with PDA-aware facilitator), (f) Mental health services for anxiety.
STEP 3: At the IPP meeting, present your request letter. For each service, explain why PDA-specific approach is needed: 'Traditional ABA increases my child's anxiety and demand avoidance. I'm requesting a provider who uses naturalistic, play-based approaches.'
STEP 4: Request specific language in IPP: 'Services will use low-demand, PDA-informed approaches. Provider must be willing to adapt to child's anxiety-driven demand avoidance profile.'
STEP 5: Get names and contact info for all assigned providers. Interview them about PDA familiarity before starting.
STEP 6: If RC denies services, appeal: OCRA (800) 390-7032.

CONTACT: YOUR REGIONAL CENTER: Find at dds.ca.gov/rc — contact your service coordinator directly.
SERVICE COORDINATOR: Your main RC contact — get name, phone, email.
OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — if services denied or inadequate
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — RC rights guidance, fair hearing help
PDA-INFORMED PROVIDERS:
• PDANA directory: pdanorthamerica.org/find-help
• Ask RC for providers experienced with demand avoidance
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com — filter by PDA/autism/anxiety
PARENT TRAINING:
• Low Demand Parenting: lowdemandparenting.com — courses
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org — training resources
• Your RC should fund parent training as IPP service

DOCUMENTS: FOR IPP MEETING:
• ASD evaluation report (with PDA characteristics highlighted)
• Service Request Letter (detailed, specific)
• PDA Profile Summary (1-page)
• Your PDA behavior journal
• Research on PDA-informed approaches vs traditional ABA
• List of specific provider requests

IPP DOCUMENT SHOULD INCLUDE:
• PDA profile description in Present Level
• Specific services with PDA-informed approach noted
• Provider qualifications (familiarity with demand avoidance)
• Parent training services
• Respite hours
• Review schedule (every 6 months recommended for PDA)
• Emergency/crisis plan for severe demand avoidance episodes

TIMELINE: 60 days from eligibility to initial IPP.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept generic autism services that use high-demand approaches — standard ABA with discrete trial training is often counterproductive for PDA. Request PDA-informed or naturalistic ABA (PRT, ESDM, or play-based models). (2) Accept a 'one-size-fits-all' IPP — PDA children need individualized, low-demand approaches documented in the plan. (3) Let the service coordinator assign providers without your input — YOU choose providers. Ask: 'Is this provider familiar with demand avoidance profiles? Do they use low-demand approaches?' (4) Accept denial of parent training services — parent training in low-demand strategies is arguably the MOST important service for PDA families. (5) Forget to request respite care — PDA parenting is exhausting and burnout is common.

RISKS: ★ PDA SERVICE TRAP: RC recommends traditional ABA (40 hrs/week structured compliance training) — this approach typically escalates PDA behaviors, increases anxiety, and can cause trauma. Service coordinator unfamiliar with PDA. Available providers don't understand PDA profile. POS spending disparities affect PDA families too. Rural areas: virtually no PDA-informed providers.

