insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 3.2: Low-Demand Parenting & Therapeutic Approach

PDA — Step 3.2: Low-Demand Parenting & Therapeutic Approach

WHAT TO DO: IMPLEMENT LOW-DEMAND STRATEGIES IMMEDIATELY — DON'T WAIT FOR SERVICES: (1) Low-demand parenting is the EVIDENCE-BASED approach for PDA. Core principles: reduce demands to the minimum necessary, use indirect language ('I wonder if the shoes might want to go for a walk'), offer autonomy and choice ('Would you like to do X or Y, or something else entirely?'), use humor, novelty, and role-play to bypass the anxiety response, collaborate rather than direct. (2) Read NOW: 'Low Demand Parenting' by Amanda Diekman (the definitive parent guide), 'Declarative Language Handbook' by Linda Murphy, 'The Explosive Child' by Ross Greene (Collaborative & Proactive Solutions). (3) Strip demands to SURVIVAL LEVEL first (safety, food, basic hygiene) — let everything else go temporarily. You can add demands back gradually as your child's nervous system stabilizes. (4) Reframe ALL behavior through an anxiety lens: 'My child CAN'T, not WON'T.' (5) Create a DEMAND AUDIT: list every demand in your child's day (waking up, getting dressed, eating breakfast, school arrival, each class transition...) and rate each 0-10 for your child's tolerance. Eliminate or modify demands rated 7+. (6) Find parent support: PDA North America community, At Peace Parents (atpeaceparents.com), Declarative Language community. (7) Track what works and share with school and providers — your daily data is the treatment plan.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: IMPLEMENT LOW-DEMAND PARENTING: (a) Audit ALL demands in your child's day — written list. (b) Categorize: Essential (safety), Important (hygiene, nutrition), Nice-to-have (homework, chores). (c) Temporarily DROP all 'nice-to-have' demands and reduce 'important' demands. (d) Rebuild slowly as your child's nervous system regulation improves.
STEP 2: SWITCH TO DECLARATIVE LANGUAGE: Replace 'Do X' with observations and wonderings. Practice daily: 'I notice the toothbrush is on the counter' instead of 'Brush your teeth.' 'I wonder if it's a shoes day or a no-shoes day.'
STEP 3: BUILD COLLABORATION: Instead of 'You need to do X,' try 'We have a problem to solve — how should we handle [thing]?' Give genuine choices (not 'my way or highway').
STEP 4: REQUEST PARENT TRAINING through RC IPP (or privately): Low Demand Parenting coaching, Declarative Language training, SPACE treatment for accommodation-based approaches.
STEP 5: JOIN PDA PARENT SUPPORT GROUP — peer support is essential. PDANA, PDA Parents communities. Weekly/monthly.
STEP 6: Prioritize caregiver self-care: respite (through RC), therapy for yourself, regular breaks.

CONTACT: LOW-DEMAND PARENTING:
• Low Demand Parenting: lowdemandparenting.com — Amanda Diekman, courses + coaching
• PDA Society practice guidance: pdasociety.org.uk/what-is-pda/practice-guidance
DECLARATIVE LANGUAGE:
• 'The Declarative Language Handbook' by Linda Murphy
SPACE TREATMENT:
• Yale Child Study Center: spactreatment.net — evidence-based approach for anxiety/avoidance
• Find SPACE therapists through directory
PARENT SUPPORT:
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org — peer groups
• PDA Parents Facebook group
• Your Regional Center — request parent training + respite
CARGIVER SUPPORT:
• NAMI CA: namica.org — family support groups
• Your insurance: therapy for caregiver stress

DOCUMENTS: LOW-DEMAND PARENTING TOOLKIT:
• Demand audit worksheet (list all daily demands, categorize, reduce)
• Declarative language cheat sheet (command → declarative alternatives)
• Daily routine visual schedule (flexible, not rigid)
• Calm-down/regulation strategy list (co-created with child)
• Crisis plan for severe episodes
• Communication scripts for family/friends who don't understand PDA

TRACKING DOCUMENTS:
• Weekly demand reduction log
• Strategy effectiveness tracker
• Caregiver stress/burnout check-in
• Provider communication log
• RC respite hours used vs approved

TIMELINE: Ongoing — long-term approach, not quick fix.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Use traditional behavioral approaches: reward charts, sticker systems, token economies, 'first-then' boards, time-outs, consequences, removal of privileges. These INCREASE demand anxiety in PDA children. (2) Use direct commands: 'Put on your shoes,' 'Do your homework,' 'Sit down.' Instead use declarative language: 'I notice it's almost time to go,' 'I wonder if the shoes are ready.' (3) Engage in power struggles — PDA children cannot 'just comply' any more than an anxious person can 'just relax.' (4) Interpret masking at school as evidence that 'they can do it when they want to' — masking is exhausting and unsustainable. (5) Ignore your own burnout — PDA parenting requires ongoing support for caregivers.

RISKS: Traditional therapists unfamiliar with PDA use standard behavioral approaches that escalate. Parent exhaustion and burnout from constant negotiation. Other family members (grandparents, co-parent) don't understand low-demand approach — see it as 'giving in.' School uses opposite approach from home, creating inconsistency. Cost of PDA-informed private therapy.

