insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 4.1: Request School District Assessment

PDA — Step 4.1: Request School District Assessment

WHAT TO DO: REQUEST SCHOOL EVALUATION WITH PDA-SPECIFIC FRAMING: (1) Submit written request for comprehensive evaluation 'in all areas of suspected disability including autism spectrum, anxiety, emotional disturbance, social-emotional functioning, adaptive behavior, and functional behavior.' (2) CRITICAL FRAMING: Do NOT describe your child as 'defiant' or 'oppositional' — use: 'pervasive anxiety-driven demand avoidance,' 'difficulty transitioning between activities,' 'significant anxiety in response to everyday expectations,' 'need for autonomy and control as anxiety management.' (3) If your child has school avoidance/refusal, document every missed day and the anxiety-driven reasons. (4) Request: psychoeducational evaluation, Functional Behavior Assessment (FBA) focused on DEMAND AVOIDANCE as the behavior (not 'non-compliance'), speech-language pragmatics evaluation, OT with sensory profile, social-emotional assessment. (5) CA law: 15 calendar days for assessment plan after written request. (6) Bring your Demand Diary, PDA profile, any RC evaluation, private evaluation, and video documentation. (7) If school refuses evaluation: file CDE compliance complaint or contact Disability Rights California (DRC).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Write your evaluation request letter: 'I am writing to request a comprehensive special education evaluation for my child. My child has been diagnosed with [ASD/anxiety/other] with a demand avoidance (PDA) profile. I request evaluation in: academic achievement, cognitive ability, social-emotional functioning, behavioral assessment, functional behavior, adaptive behavior, and any other areas of suspected disability.'
STEP 2: ATTACH your PDA documentation: evaluation report, PDA Profile Summary, behavior journal excerpts, and a cover letter explaining PDA for the school team.
STEP 3: Hand-deliver AND email the letter. Get a date-stamped copy. This triggers the 15-day assessment plan deadline per CA Education Code §56321.
STEP 4: Include a 1-page PDA education brief for the school: 'What is PDA? Key points for educators.' Available at pdasociety.org.uk/education.
STEP 5: Follow up in 10 days if no response. If 15 days pass with no assessment plan, send: 'My evaluation request submitted [date] triggered a 15-day assessment plan deadline that has now passed. Please respond immediately or I will file a compliance complaint with CDE.'

CONTACT: YOUR SCHOOL DISTRICT SpEd DEPARTMENT: Find contact at your district website under 'Special Education' or call the main district office.
CA DEPT OF EDUCATION: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/ — SpEd guidance and complaint process
CDE COMPLIANCE COMPLAINT: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — free legal advocacy if district refuses evaluation
CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free IEP advocacy
TASK: taskca.org — parent training on school rights
MATRIX: matrixparents.org — parent support
PDA SCHOOL GUIDANCE:
• PDA Society education guidance: pdasociety.org.uk/education
• PDANA school resources: pdanorthamerica.org

DOCUMENTS: PARENT SUBMITS:
• Written evaluation request letter (dated, keep copy with receipt stamp)
• PDA documentation packet (diagnosis report, PDA Profile Summary, behavior journal)
• PDA education brief for school team
• Prior evaluations
• School records showing impact (attendance, behavior reports, grades)

DISTRICT MUST PROVIDE (within 15 days):
• Assessment plan listing all areas to be evaluated
• Parent rights notification (Procedural Safeguards)
• OR: Prior Written Notice if refusing evaluation

PARENT REVIEWS assessment plan for:
• All requested areas included?
• Social-emotional and behavioral areas included?
• FBA (Functional Behavior Assessment) included?

TIMELINE: District: 15 days to respond with assessment plan. 60 days from consent to completed assessment.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Assume school will understand PDA — most teachers and school psychologists have never heard of it. YOU must educate them. (2) Request a 'PDA evaluation' from the school — request a comprehensive special education evaluation covering academic, behavioral, social-emotional, and functional areas. (3) Rely on verbal request only — PUT IT IN WRITING. (4) Accept 'your child is doing fine academically' as reason not to evaluate — PDA affects functional performance, behavior, attendance, and emotional wellbeing, not just grades. A child masking all day is NOT 'doing fine.' (5) Let RTI/MTSS delay your evaluation request. (6) Accept school's behavioral assessments as complete — they rarely capture the demand avoidance profile.

RISKS: ★ SCHOOL PDA TRAP: Child masks at school — appears 'fine' to teachers. School sees no disability because behavior only shows at home ('after-school restraint collapse'). School frames behaviors as willful noncompliance or ODD. District psychologist unfamiliar with PDA. Standard FBA (Functional Behavior Assessment) may miss anxiety-driven avoidance because it focuses on visible behaviors, not internal states. Child labeled as 'oppositional' rather than anxious.

