insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 4.2: School District Evaluation — PDA-Aware Approach

PDA — Step 4.2: School District Evaluation — PDA-Aware Approach

WHAT TO DO: ENSURE THE SCHOOL EVALUATION CAPTURES PDA ACCURATELY: (1) School psychologists typically do NOT know about PDA — you will need to educate them. Bring: PDA Society overview (pdasociety.org.uk), your 1-page PDA profile, EDA-Q results, and demand diary. (2) Request that the school psychologist review PDA research before evaluating: 'I'd like to share some research on Pathological Demand Avoidance, a profile within autism, so the evaluation can capture my child's presentation accurately.' (3) The FBA (Functional Behavior Assessment) is CRITICAL — ensure it identifies DEMAND AVOIDANCE as the function of behavior, driven by ANXIETY, not attention-seeking or escape from non-preferred tasks (the typical school interpretation). (4) Push back if the FBA labels behavior as 'non-compliance' or 'defiance' — reframe: 'The behavior is anxiety-driven avoidance of perceived demands, including demands the child wants to meet but cannot due to their neurological profile.' (5) If the school psychologist isn't familiar with PDA, request a specialist consultant or request the district fund an outside PDA-knowledgeable evaluator. (6) Ensure adaptive behavior assessment (Vineland) is included — PDA children often have significant gaps between cognitive ability and adaptive functioning. (7) Request observation across multiple settings including unstructured time (recess, lunch, transitions) — PDA often presents differently than in structured classroom time.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Provide the school psychologist with your complete PDA documentation BEFORE testing begins. Include: clinical evaluation, PDA Profile Summary, behavior journal, and PDA education brief.
STEP 2: Request a FUNCTIONAL BEHAVIOR ASSESSMENT (FBA) as part of the evaluation: 'I request an FBA that specifically examines the function of my child's avoidance behavior. The function is anxiety reduction, not attention-seeking or escape from non-preferred tasks.'
STEP 3: Complete Parent Input forms THOROUGHLY — describe the PDA profile in detail. Describe the HOME vs SCHOOL contrast: 'At school, my child masks and appears compliant. At home, after a day of masking, they experience severe meltdowns, demand avoidance, and emotional dysregulation.'
STEP 4: Request observation during STRUCTURED/DEMANDING activities, not just free play or preferred activities.
STEP 5: After evaluation, request reports 5+ days before the eligibility meeting. Review for: (a) Does it accurately describe PDA? (b) Is demand avoidance framed as anxiety-driven? (c) Are recommendations PDA-appropriate?

CONTACT: SCHOOL PSYCHOLOGIST: Get name, email, phone from SpEd coordinator — request a pre-evaluation conversation.
SPECIAL EDUCATION COORDINATOR: Your district contact for scheduling and process.
FBA SPECIALIST: If school's FBA doesn't capture PDA function, request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at district expense.
IEE RIGHTS: 34 CFR §300.502 — if you disagree with school evaluation
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — IEE guidance
CASE: caseadvocacy.org — advocate for evaluation
PDA SCHOOL RESOURCES:
• PDA Society education: pdasociety.org.uk/education
• Share with school psychologist: pdanorthamerica.org professional resources

DOCUMENTS: SUBMIT TO SCHOOL (before evaluation):
• Clinical evaluation report (with PDA characteristics highlighted)
• PDA Profile Summary (1-page)
• PDA education brief for school team
• Behavior journal excerpts (home vs school contrast)
• Video recordings (if available and appropriate)
• Parent Input Form (detailed, PDA-specific)

SCHOOL PRODUCES:
• Psychoeducational evaluation report
• FBA report (request separately if not included)
• Classroom observation data
• Teacher Input results
• Social-emotional assessment results

REVIEW CHECKLIST:
• PDA accurately described? Or mischaracterized as ODD?
• Demand avoidance framed as anxiety-driven?
• Recommendations include low-demand approaches?

TIMELINE: 60 calendar days from consent to completed assessment.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Let the school interpret demand avoidance as 'oppositional defiant disorder' (ODD) — PDA is anxiety-driven, not defiance-driven. If the school is heading toward ODD, provide PDA literature and your clinical evaluation. (2) Accept evaluation only during 'good' periods — request observation during demanding activities. (3) Let the school evaluate only academics — PDA impacts behavior, social-emotional functioning, attendance, and self-regulation. (4) Withhold your home data — the contrast between home and school behavior IS the evidence of masking. (5) Accept a brief behavioral checklist as a complete behavioral assessment — request FBA (Functional Behavior Assessment) that examines function of demand avoidance behavior.

RISKS: Assessment only captures school behavior (where child masks). No home data collected. Evaluator uses standard autism tools that miss PDA social presentation. Demand avoidance attributed to ODD/defiance. Executive function deficits missed. Sensory needs underestimated. Child performs well on standardized tests (high IQ masks difficulties).

