insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 4.3: IEP Development — PDA-Specific Accommodations

PDA — Step 4.3: IEP Development — PDA-Specific Accommodations

WHAT TO DO: If eligible, IEP team develops plan. CRITICAL PDA ACCOMMODATIONS to request: (1) Low-demand task entry — offer choice and novelty. (2) Predictable routines with opt-in breaks and sensory regulation spaces. (3) Assessment flexibility — oral, visual, chunked work options. (4) Collaborative language scripts ('How could we make this easier?'). (5) Reduced direct demands — use declarative language. (6) Safe space/regulation room access. (7) 1:1 aide trained in PDA-informed strategies (if needed). (8) Modified behavior plan — NO traditional reward/consequence charts. (9) Transition warnings and preparation. (10) Flexible attendance for high-anxiety days. IEP GOALS should be neurodiversity-affirming, using declarative language, supporting executive functioning, sensory needs, and emotional regulation.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before IEP meeting, prepare a written 'IEP Request' document with PDA-specific accommodations. ESSENTIAL PDA ACCOMMODATIONS: (a) Reduced direct demands — use declarative language, indirect prompts, choices, (b) Flexible schedule — permission to leave/take breaks without asking, (c) Designated safe space for regulation, (d) Modified work demands (reduced volume, not reduced expectations), (e) No public call-outs, (f) Advance notice of transitions and changes, (g) Adult uses collaborative/negotiation approach rather than directives.
STEP 2: Propose PDA-appropriate IEP GOALS: 'Student will use self-regulation strategies to manage anxiety in demanding situations, demonstrating [specific behavior] in [X] of [Y] opportunities.' NOT: 'Student will comply with teacher directives.'
STEP 3: Bring an advocate if possible (CASE, DRC).
STEP 4: Request BIP uses Collaborative & Proactive Solutions (CPS/Ross Greene) approach, NOT traditional ABA/behaviorist approach.
STEP 5: Request staff training on PDA — the school team must understand WHY they're using these approaches.
STEP 6: Request 6-month IEP review (not 12-month) to monitor PDA accommodation effectiveness.

CONTACT: IEP MEETING TEAM: You, SpEd teacher, gen ed teacher, school psychologist, administrator. Bring advocate.
ADVOCACY:
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free IEP advocates in CA
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• TASK: taskca.org — parent training
• Matrix: matrixparents.org
PDA IEP RESOURCES:
• PDA Society education guidance: pdasociety.org.uk/education — accommodation lists
• PDANA school toolkit: pdanorthamerica.org
• 'The Explosive Child' by Ross Greene — CPS approach for BIP
• Lives in the Balance: livesinthebalance.org — free CPS resources
IF IEP DENIED:
• Request 504 Plan (see Step 4.4)
• CDE complaint: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp

DOCUMENTS: PARENT BRINGS TO IEP MEETING:
• Written IEP Request document (PDA-specific accommodations, goals, BIP approach)
• PDA Profile Summary (1-page)
• Evaluation reports (clinical + school)
• PDA education brief for team members who are new
• Research on PDA-informed school approaches (PDA Society, PDANA)
• Ross Greene's CPS overview for BIP discussion

IEP DOCUMENT SHOULD INCLUDE:
• Present Levels: PDA profile description, demand avoidance pattern, masking, anxiety
• Goals: regulation, flexibility, self-advocacy (NOT compliance)
• BIP: CPS-based, NOT behaviorist
• Accommodations: comprehensive PDA accommodation list
• Staff training: PDA awareness for all staff who interact with child
• Review schedule: 6 months (not 12)
• Crisis plan: for severe demand avoidance/meltdown episodes

TIMELINE: 30 days from completed assessment to IEP meeting.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) based on traditional behaviorist methods — no reward/consequence systems, no token economies, no 'earning' breaks. PDA children need low-demand, collaborative approaches. (2) Accept rigid schedules without flexibility built in — PDA children need escape valves and choice. (3) Accept goals requiring direct compliance ('Student will follow 3-step directions 80% of time') — reframe as regulation goals. (4) Let the school remove your child from preferred activities as 'consequence' for avoidance — this increases anxiety. (5) Accept 'they need to learn to comply' as educational philosophy — compliance-based approaches cause harm in PDA children. (6) Let placement default to behavioral/ED classroom — PDA children often do best in general educ

RISKS: ★ PDA IEP PITFALLS: School uses traditional behavior plan (sticker charts, earned privileges) that triggers PDA escalation. IEP goals focus on 'compliance' rather than regulation and self-advocacy. 1:1 aide uses directive approach that increases demand avoidance. School removes child from preferred activities as consequence — increases trauma. Staff see accommodations as 'giving in' to behavior. IEP doesn't address masking/after-school collapse.

