insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 4.4: 504 Plan Alternative (If IEP Denied)

PDA — Step 4.4: 504 Plan Alternative (If IEP Denied)

WHAT TO DO: PURSUE 504 PLAN IF IEP IS DENIED OR AS SUPPLEMENT: (1) 504 Plan has BROADER eligibility than IEP — any disability (including anxiety, ASD-related) that 'substantially limits a major life activity' (learning, concentrating, social interaction). (2) For PDA, a 504 Plan can provide: reduced demand accommodations, flexible scheduling, movement breaks, alternative assignments, sensory accommodations, testing modifications, reduced homework, communication modifications (indirect language), safe space access. (3) 504 does NOT provide specialized instruction or related services — only accommodations. If your child needs a modified curriculum or therapeutic services, push for IEP. (4) CRITICAL: 504 can include a demand reduction plan: explicit list of demands that are modified or eliminated, alternative ways to demonstrate learning, flexible attendance for school avoidance, adult check-ins for anxiety management. (5) If 504 is denied: file OCR complaint with Office for Civil Rights — ed.gov/ocr, (800) 421-3481. (6) 504 is enforced under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act and ADA — stronger anti-discrimination protection than IDEA in some areas. (7) Document accommodation implementation — 504 has no progress monitoring like IEP, so YOU must track.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: If IEP denied, immediately request 504 evaluation: 'My child has [diagnosis] with a demand avoidance profile that substantially limits major life activities including learning, concentrating, and self-care. I request a 504 evaluation.'
STEP 2: Provide documentation: clinical evaluation, school evaluation, PDA Profile Summary.
STEP 3: At 504 meeting, present PDA-specific accommodation list (same as IEP accommodations from Step 4.3).
STEP 4: Get the 504 plan IN WRITING with specific PDA accommodations.
STEP 5: Monitor implementation — email teachers monthly: 'Is [child] receiving all 504 accommodations? Any challenges?'
STEP 6: If accommodations not implemented: file OCR complaint (ed.gov/ocr) — (800) 421-3481.

CONTACT: SCHOOL 504 COORDINATOR: Usually principal or school counselor — ask front office.
DISTRICT 504 CONTACT: Through your district's special education or student services office.
IF 504 DENIED:
• OCR (Office for Civil Rights): ed.gov/ocr — (800) 421-3481
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org
• DREDF: dredf.org (disability rights)
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• Your school district Special Education department
• CA Dept of Education: cde.ca.gov/sp/se
• Parent Training & Information Centers: parentcenterhub.org — find your CA PTI by county

DOCUMENTS: 504 DOCUMENTS:
• Written request for 504 evaluation
• Medical documentation (ASD/anxiety diagnosis, evaluation reports)
• Private evaluation with PDA profile documentation
• 504 Plan document (accommodations listed)
• Teacher acknowledgment form
• Annual review notes
• Implementation tracking records (parent-maintained)

FOR FUTURE TRANSITIONS:
• Complete 504 accommodation history
• All supporting evaluations
• Documentation of accommodation effectiveness
• SAT/ACT/college accommodation applications will need this history

TIMELINE: No specific federal timeline — 'reasonable' time.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept 504 if your child needs specialized instruction or a BIP — 504 only provides accommodations, not specially designed instruction. (2) Accept generic accommodations (just 'extra time') — PDA needs very specific accommodations around demand reduction, flexibility, and safe space access. (3) Let 504 go unmonitored — there's no progress monitoring like IEP, so YOU must track implementation. (4) Forget that 504 accommodations are legally enforceable under Section 504 — file OCR complaint if school ignores them.

RISKS: District doesn't mention 504 when denying IEP. 504 plan is generic (extra time only). PDA-specific accommodations not included. No enforcement mechanism as strong as IEP. Staff don't implement 504 accommodations consistently.

