insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 4.5: IEP/504 Dispute Resolution (If Needed)

PDA — Step 4.5: IEP/504 Dispute Resolution (If Needed)

WHAT TO DO: KNOW YOUR DISPUTE RESOLUTION OPTIONS AND USE THEM: (1) If the school denies evaluation, denies eligibility, offers inadequate IEP/504, or fails to implement accommodations: you have legal rights. (2) California dispute resolution options in escalating order: (a) Informal resolution — meet with principal/SpEd director, bring advocate; (b) Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR) — facilitated IEP or mediation through CDE; (c) CDE Compliance Complaint — file within 1 year at cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — CDE investigates and can order corrective action; (d) Mediation — free, through OAH (Office of Administrative Hearings), voluntary; (e) Due Process Hearing — formal legal proceeding through OAH, 2-year statute of limitations. (3) For PDA families, common disputes include: school uses punitive behavioral approach (restraint, seclusion, suspension) instead of anxiety-based, school attributes avoidance to 'choice' rather than disability, IEP goals focused on compliance rather than anxiety reduction, school refuses to implement low-demand accommodations, school attendance policies penalize disability-related absences. (4) RESTRAINT AND SECLUSION: California law limits use — if your child is restrained or secluded, demand incident reports and file complaint. CA Ed Code §49005-49006.4. (5) FREE advocacy support available — use it.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Document the dispute clearly in writing. Email to SpEd director: 'I disagree with [specific decision]. My child requires [specific accommodation/service] because [PDA-specific reason].'
STEP 2: Request another IEP meeting to resolve informally.
STEP 3: If informal resolution fails, choose escalation path:
  (a) CDE Compliance Complaint: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — for procedural violations. Free, CDE investigates. Decision within 60 days.
  (b) Mediation through OAH: dgs.ca.gov/OAH — free, voluntary, confidential.
  (c) Due Process Hearing: OAH — (916) 263-0880 — formal hearing, decision binding.
STEP 4: Contact DRC BEFORE filing anything: (800) 776-5746 — free consultation on best strategy.
STEP 5: Consider hiring a SpEd attorney for due process. Many work on contingency for strong cases.
STEP 6: 2-YEAR statute of limitations from the date you knew or should have known about the violation.

CONTACT: DRC: (800) 776-5746 — free legal consultation and representation for some cases
CDE COMPLAINT: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — file online or by mail
OAH (mediation/due process): dgs.ca.gov/OAH — (916) 263-0880
CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free advocacy support
SPECIAL EDUCATION ATTORNEYS (CA):
• Disability Rights Education & Defense Fund (DREDF): dredf.org
• Ask DRC for attorney referrals
• CA State Bar lawyer referral: calbar.ca.gov
OCR (for 504 complaints): ed.gov/ocr — (800) 421-3481

DOCUMENTS: DISPUTE DOCUMENTS:
• Written description of dispute (dated)
• All relevant IEP/504 documents
• Evaluation reports
• Communication log (all emails with school)
• Evidence of PDA profile and needed supports
• Prior Written Notice from district
• Timeline of events

FILING DOCUMENTS:
• CDE Complaint: complaint form + supporting docs
• OAH Mediation: request form
• OAH Due Process: complaint filing
• OCR Complaint (504): complaint form + evidence of discrimination

TIMELINE: CDE complaint: 60 days. Due process: 45 days from filing.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept verbal promises — get everything in writing. (2) Skip the informal resolution step — many disputes resolve through a well-written letter or advocate call. (3) File due process without legal guidance — consult DRC first (free). (4) Let the 2-year statute of limitations pass. (5) Accept mediation if the school has already shown bad faith — go to due process. (6) Represent yourself at due process hearing without at least consulting an attorney.

RISKS: Parent doesn't know appeal rights. District claims 'PDA isn't a real diagnosis.' Attorney unfamiliar with PDA. Hearing officer unfamiliar with PDA research. Cost of private advocate/attorney.

