insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 5.1: Find PDA-Affirming Therapists & Providers

PDA — Step 5.1: Find PDA-Affirming Therapists & Providers

WHAT TO DO: FIND PROVIDERS WHO UNDERSTAND PDA — THIS IS CRITICAL: (1) Traditional therapeutic approaches often FAIL or HARM PDA children: standard ABA (compliance-based), traditional CBT (demand-heavy worksheets/homework), behavioral reward/punishment systems, social skills groups focused on compliance. (2) PDA-AFFIRMING approaches: Collaborative & Proactive Solutions (Ross Greene model — cpsconnection.com), Declarative Language strategies (Linda Murphy), low-demand ABA or naturalistic ABA (if using ABA at all — play-based, child-led), DIR/Floortime (child-led, relationship-based), occupational therapy with sensory integration, drama therapy/art therapy (leverages PDA strength in role-play). (3) When interviewing providers, ask: 'Are you familiar with PDA/Pathological Demand Avoidance? How do you modify your approach for children who experience anxiety-driven demand avoidance? Do you use compliance-based or autonomy-based approaches?' (4) RED FLAGS: provider says 'they just need firmer boundaries,' uses token economies/sticker charts as primary strategy, insists on ABA compliance training, dismisses PDA as 'just ODD.' (5) CA-specific: Regional Center can fund PDA-informed behavioral consultation — request 'behavioral services using collaborative, low-demand methodology.' (6) Many PDA-knowledgeable providers offer telehealth — don't limit search to your city. (7) PDA North America maintains a growing provider directory.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Search for PDA-informed therapists: PDANA directory (pdanorthamerica.org/find-help), Psychology Today (filter: autism, anxiety, demand avoidance), ask PDA parent groups for CA recommendations.
STEP 2: Interview every potential therapist: 'Are you familiar with PDA/pathological demand avoidance? What approaches do you use with children who have anxiety-driven demand avoidance? Do you use reward/consequence systems? How do you handle a child who refuses to participate in a session?'
STEP 3: RED FLAGS in therapist responses: mentions token economy, mentions 'earning' privileges, describes compliance goals, says 'they just need consistent boundaries,' unfamiliar with PDA.
STEP 4: GREEN FLAGS: mentions collaborative approaches, talks about reducing demands, discusses anxiety management, flexible about session structure, willing to follow child's lead.
STEP 5: Therapy types most compatible with PDA: Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT), Play therapy (child-led), SPACE treatment (parent-focused), Modified/adapted CBT (low-demand), Art/music therapy.
STEP 6: Request RC fund PDA-informed therapy through IPP. Request insurance authorization citing autism diagnosis (SB 946).

CONTACT: FIND PDA-INFORMED THERAPISTS:
• PDANA directory: pdanorthamerica.org/find-help
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com — search PDA, demand avoidance, autism anxiety
• SPACE therapist directory: spacetreatment.net
• Ask PDA parent Facebook groups for CA provider names
INSURANCE AUTHORIZATION:
• SB 946 (CA): requires insurance to cover behavioral therapy for autism
• DMHC: (888) 466-2219 — HMO complaints
• CDI: (800) 927-4357 — PPO complaints
• Health Consumer Alliance: healthconsumer.org
REGIONAL CENTER:
• Request PDA-informed therapy through IPP
• If RC assigns non-PDA-informed provider, request change
• OCRA: (800) 390-7032

DOCUMENTS: THERAPY DOCUMENTS:
• Insurance pre-authorization (if required)
• Therapist credentials and approach description
• Treatment plan from therapist (should reflect PDA-informed approach)
• Session notes and progress reports
• Your tracking log: is therapy helping or increasing demand avoidance?

INSURANCE DOCUMENTS:
• Referral from pediatrician/diagnosing clinician
• Pre-authorization approval
• EOB after sessions
• Appeal materials if denied (DRC can help)

TIMELINE: Waitlists vary. PDA-informed providers are scarce — may need telehealth.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Send your child to a therapist who uses compliance-based approaches — this includes traditional CBT that requires homework compliance, exposure therapy without child-led pacing, or any therapy that uses reward/consequence systems. (2) Assume all autism therapists understand PDA — most don't. (3) Continue with a therapist who makes your child worse — if demand avoidance increases after therapy starts, the approach may be wrong. (4) Pursue ABA without ensuring it's PDA-informed and naturalistic — discrete trial ABA is harmful for PDA profiles. (5) Skip therapy for parents — parent coaching in low-demand strategies is often MORE impactful than direct child therapy.

RISKS: ★ PROVIDER DESERT: Very few U.S. therapists understand PDA. Traditional ABA providers dominate autism therapy landscape. Insurance may only cover 'ABA' for autism, not PDA-informed alternatives. RC vendor list may not include PDA-affirming providers. Rural areas: virtually no options. Telehealth may be only option for PDA-informed care.

