insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 5.2: Insurance Authorization for PDA-Informed Therapy

PDA — Step 5.2: Insurance Authorization for PDA-Informed Therapy

WHAT TO DO: NAVIGATE INSURANCE FOR PDA-INFORMED THERAPY: (1) CA SB 946 (Autism insurance mandate) requires insurance to cover behavioral health treatment (BHT) for ASD — including ABA. For PDA, request 'modified ABA using naturalistic, play-based methodology' or 'behavioral consultation using low-demand approaches.' (2) Insurance will NOT cover 'PDA therapy' — you must use covered diagnostic codes: ASD (F84.0), Generalized Anxiety (F41.1), Social Anxiety (F40.10), Adjustment Disorder. (3) Mental health parity laws require coverage for anxiety treatment: individual therapy (CBT modified for PDA), family therapy, psychiatric evaluation for medication (anxiety medication can help PDA). (4) If insurance denies ABA or therapy: CA DMHC complaint (HMO): (888) 466-2219; CA CDI complaint (PPO): (800) 927-4357. (5) Regional Center fills gaps insurance won't cover — respite, parent training, behavioral consultation. (6) Medi-Cal covers behavioral health through managed care plans. (7) School-based services (IEP/504) are FREE regardless of insurance. (8) For PDA medication evaluation: SSRIs (anxiety) may help reduce baseline anxiety, making demands more tolerable — discuss with child psychiatrist, not just pediatrician.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Call insurance: 'My child has an ASD diagnosis. I'm requesting authorization for behavioral health treatment under CA SB 946. The treatment plan calls for [therapy type] with a PDA-informed provider.'
STEP 2: If insurance denies: file internal appeal first, then external appeal through DMHC (HMO) or CDI (PPO).
STEP 3: If using Medi-Cal: your managed care plan covers behavioral therapy. Call the plan directly.
STEP 4: If insurance won't cover a PDA-informed provider: request a 'network inadequacy' exception — if no in-network provider has PDA expertise, insurance may be required to cover an out-of-network provider.
STEP 5: Track all authorization dates, session limits, and renewal deadlines.
STEP 6: For services not covered by insurance, request through RC IPP.

CONTACT: INSURANCE HELP:
• DMHC (HMO complaints/appeals): (888) 466-2219
• CDI (PPO complaints/appeals): (800) 927-4357
• Health Consumer Alliance: healthconsumer.org — free insurance navigation help
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — insurance rights
MEDI-CAL:
• Managed care plan: call number on Medi-Cal card
• Medi-Cal Ombudsman: (888) 452-8609
SB 946 INFORMATION:
• CA Health & Safety Code §1374.73 — autism behavioral therapy mandate
• Autism Speaks insurance guide: autismspeaks.org/insurance

DOCUMENTS: INSURANCE DOCUMENTS:
• Pre-authorization request and approval
• Treatment plan from provider
• Authorization dates and session limits
• Denial letter (if denied)
• Appeal documentation
• EOBs (Explanation of Benefits)
• Provider credentials

TRACK:
• Authorization start/end dates
• Sessions used vs approved
• Renewal deadlines
• Co-pay/deductible status

TIMELINE: Prior auth: 5-15 business days. Denial appeal: 30 days internal, 45 days DMHC IMR.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept insurance denial without appealing — CA SB 946 requires insurance to cover behavioral health treatment for autism. (2) Let insurance dictate the therapy type — you have the right to request specific PDA-informed approaches. (3) Accept a provider from insurance's list without verifying PDA familiarity. (4) Forget Medi-Cal as an option — Medi-Cal covers behavioral therapy for autism with no co-pay. (5) Let prior authorization expire without renewal — track authorization end dates.

RISKS: Insurance denies non-ABA autism therapy. Authorization limited to traditional ABA hours. PDA-informed therapy coded differently — doesn't match ABA billing codes. Medi-Cal managed care plan unfamiliar with PDA. Prior auth requires 'medical necessity' documentation that specifically addresses PDA approach.

