insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 6.1: Address School Avoidance / School Refusal

PDA — Step 6.1: Address School Avoidance / School Refusal

WHAT TO DO: ADDRESS SCHOOL AVOIDANCE AS A DISABILITY SYMPTOM, NOT A BEHAVIOR PROBLEM: (1) School avoidance/refusal is one of the most common and devastating PDA impacts — up to 70% of PDA children experience significant school avoidance. (2) CRITICAL REFRAME: Your child is not 'truant' or 'school-refusing' — they are experiencing a disability-related inability to tolerate the demand-saturated school environment. Every aspect of school is a demand: arrival time, sitting still, following instructions, social navigation, academic work, transitions. (3) DOCUMENT EVERYTHING: every absence, every morning struggle (video if possible), anxiety symptoms (nausea, headaches, meltdowns, shutdowns), and what you've tried. (4) Demand a TEAM MEETING immediately — bring your documentation and advocate. (5) Explore alternatives within the IEP/504: part-time schedule (gradual increase), homebound instruction (CA Ed Code §48206.3), independent study program, online/virtual school within your district, alternative school placement, hybrid schedule. (6) California chronic absence threshold: 10% of school days. Schools must provide intervention, NOT punishment. (7) If district threatens truancy: respond with disability documentation. Truancy proceedings against a child whose absences are disability-related violate Section 504 and ADA. Contact DRC immediately. (8) SARB (School Attendance Review Board) cannot override IEP accommodations for attendance.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: IMMEDIATELY notify the school IN WRITING: 'My child's attendance difficulties are directly related to their disability ([diagnosis]). I am requesting an IEP meeting to address school avoidance as a manifestation of their disability. I am also requesting that any attendance-related interventions be trauma-informed and PDA-appropriate.'
STEP 2: Request IEP amendment for school avoidance accommodations: (a) Modified schedule (gradual re-entry, part-time, late start), (b) Designated safe person and safe space, (c) Alternative arrival/departure (avoid crowded times), (d) Home-hospital instruction if needed (CA Ed Code §48206.3), (e) Independent study option (CA Ed Code §51745).
STEP 3: Document all absences as disability-related — this protects against truancy consequences.
STEP 4: Consider whether current placement is appropriate — may need transfer, reduced setting, or alternative program.
STEP 5: Address underlying anxiety: increase therapy, consider SPACE treatment (parent-focused anxiety intervention), reduce non-school demands at home.
STEP 6: If SARB threatened: contact DRC immediately (800) 776-5746. PDA school avoidance is disability-related, not truancy.

CONTACT: SCHOOL DISTRICT: Principal + SpEd coordinator — request emergency IEP meeting.
HOME-HOSPITAL INSTRUCTION: Request through school district. CA Ed Code §48206.3.
INDEPENDENT STUDY: CA Ed Code §51745 — request through district.
LEGAL PROTECTION:
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — school avoidance and disability rights
• CA Ed Code §48900.5 — cannot suspend for attendance without considering disability
• AB 2040 — attendance reforms protecting disabled students
PDA SCHOOL REFUSAL RESOURCES:
• PDA Society: pdasociety.org.uk/education — school avoidance guidance
• Not Fine in School: notfineinschool.co.uk — parent support for school avoidance
• SPACE treatment: spacetreatment.net

DOCUMENTS: SCHOOL AVOIDANCE DOCUMENTS:
• Written notification to school (disability-related absences)
• IEP amendment request
• Attendance records showing pattern
• Clinical documentation linking avoidance to disability
• Home-hospital instruction request (if needed)
• Independent study request (if applicable)
• Communication log with school re: attendance

IF SARB THREATENED:
• Written response: 'Absences are disability-related under [diagnosis]. SARB proceedings would constitute disability discrimination.'
• DRC contact information
• All IEP/504 documentation showing disability
• Medical/clinical letters supporting disability-related avoidance

TIMELINE: Home/hospital: 5 school days for temporary certification. Modified schedule: IEP team decision.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Force school attendance through punishment — this increases trauma and may trigger school refusal crisis. (2) Assume school refusal is 'truancy' — PDA-related school avoidance is anxiety-driven and disability-related. CA Education Code §48900.5 and AB 2040 protect against punitive attendance responses for disability-related absences. (3) Accept the school's 'tough love' approach or threats of SARB (School Attendance Review Board). (4) Blame your child — they are not choosing to avoid school; their nervous system is in survival mode. (5) Wait for it to 'resolve on its own' — school avoidance typically escalates without intervention. (6) Remove all support because 'they need to learn to cope' — this is not a coping skills issue.

RISKS: ★ CRISIS RISK: School threatens truancy/SARB (Student Attendance Review Board) proceedings against PDA family. Parent blamed for not 'making' child attend. Child forced back to school prematurely — retraumatized. Home/hospital instruction limited to 60-day certification. Independent study requires specific agreement. District resists modified schedule.

