insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 6.2: Manage PDA Crisis Episodes (Meltdowns/Shutdowns)

PDA — Step 6.2: Manage PDA Crisis Episodes (Meltdowns/Shutdowns)

WHAT TO DO: DEVELOP A PDA CRISIS SAFETY PLAN FOR HOME AND SCHOOL: (1) PDA meltdowns are NOT tantrums — they are neurological overwhelm (fight/flight/freeze response) triggered by demand overload. The child has LOST voluntary control. (2) DURING CRISIS: ensure physical safety (yours and theirs), REMOVE ALL DEMANDS (stop talking, stop directing, step back), reduce sensory input (lights, noise), offer SPACE not solutions, wait. Say nothing or use only soothing sounds. Do NOT: restrain (unless imminent danger), lecture, give consequences, ask questions, or make demands during the crisis. (3) RECOVERY PERIOD: PDA meltdown recovery can take hours to DAYS. Demand tolerance is dramatically reduced after crisis. Drop demands to absolute minimum for 24-48 hours. (4) PREVENTION: maintain demand audit, monitor early warning signs (increased rigidity, refusal of previously tolerated activities, withdrawal, physical complaints), proactively reduce demands before threshold is reached. (5) SCHOOL CRISIS PLAN: Write it into the IEP/504/BIP. Include: designated safe space, named trusted adult, no demand/no consequence during crisis, recovery time protocol, parent notification, NO restraint or seclusion (CA Ed Code §49005). (6) IF SCHOOL USES RESTRAINT/SECLUSION: document, demand incident report within 24 hours, file CDE complaint, contact DRC. (7) Emergency: if child is in danger, call 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline or mobile crisis team.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: DURING MELTDOWN: (a) Reduce all demands to zero — say nothing or use minimal, calm reassurance, (b) Ensure safety — remove dangerous objects, keep distance if child is physical, (c) Reduce sensory input — dim lights, reduce noise, remove other people, (d) Offer regulation tools without demand: place weighted blanket nearby (don't put it on them), open the door to their safe space, (e) Wait — meltdowns have a physiological arc that must complete.
STEP 2: AFTER MELTDOWN (when child is regulated — may be hours later): (a) Reconnect with warmth, no judgment, (b) If child wants to discuss, use 'I noticed' language: 'I noticed that was really hard.' (c) Do NOT require apology or processing.
STEP 3: CREATE A CRISIS PLAN with your child (when calm): safe space, regulation tools, signal words, what helps, what doesn't.
STEP 4: Include crisis plan in IEP/504 — school staff must know the plan.
STEP 5: Debrief with partner/support system — caregiver emotional processing is essential.
STEP 6: If meltdowns are escalating in frequency or severity: increase therapy, request RC crisis services, reassess demand load.

CONTACT: CRISIS SUPPORT:
• Your child's therapist (if available for urgent consultation)
• Regional Center: request crisis services through service coordinator
• 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline: call/text 988 (if safety concern)
• Crisis Text Line: text HOME to 741741
• CA Behavioral Health Crisis Line: your county's crisis line
PDA CRISIS RESOURCES:
• PDA Society crisis guidance: pdasociety.org.uk
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org
SCHOOL CRISIS:
• Ensure IEP/504 includes crisis plan
• School should NOT call police for disability-related meltdowns
• If school uses restraint/seclusion: document and report to DRC (800) 776-5746

DOCUMENTS: CRISIS PLAN DOCUMENTS:
• Written crisis plan (co-created with child when calm)
• Safe space identification (home and school)
• Regulation tools list
• Signal words/phrases
• De-escalation steps for caregivers and staff
• Emergency contacts
• Medical information

IEP/504 CRISIS ADDENDUM:
• Crisis plan incorporated into BIP
• Staff trained on plan
• Documentation of any restraint/seclusion incidents (school must report to parent)
• Incident reports from school (request copies)

TIMELINE: Immediate response needed during crisis. Prevention is ongoing.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Restrain your child during a meltdown unless there is immediate danger — restraint escalates PDA crisis. (2) Use verbal demands during a meltdown: 'Stop crying,' 'Calm down,' 'Go to your room.' (3) Punish after a meltdown — PDA meltdowns are involuntary nervous system responses, not behavioral choices. (4) Call police during a meltdown unless there is genuine safety risk — police involvement can be traumatic and dangerous for neurodivergent individuals. (5) Discuss the incident immediately after — wait until your child is fully regulated (may be hours). (6) Use physical interventions taught in schools (CPI, restraint) at home.

RISKS: School staff physically restrain child during meltdown (common, traumatic for PDA children). School calls police for 'violent behavior.' Seclusion room used. Child suspended or expelled for crisis behavior. Parent called to pick up child repeatedly — job loss risk. Crisis misidentified as manipulative behavior rather than dysregulation.

