insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 7.1: Ongoing IEP/504 Review — PDA-Specific Monitoring

PDA — Step 7.1: Ongoing IEP/504 Review — PDA-Specific Monitoring

WHAT TO DO: MONITOR AND ADAPT THE IEP/504 FOR PDA'S CHANGING PRESENTATION: (1) PDA demands change as your child grows — what worked at age 6 may fail at age 10. Puberty often dramatically increases demand sensitivity. (2) Review IEP/504 PROACTIVELY every 6 months (you can request a meeting anytime). Focus on: Are accommodations actually being implemented? Is the behavioral approach still anxiety-based (not compliance-based)? Has demand tolerance changed? Are new demands emerging (academic complexity, social pressure, puberty)? (3) Track quarterly: absences, grades, behavioral incidents, your child's self-reported anxiety level, demand avoidance severity. (4) Watch for MASKING COLLAPSE: PDA children often hold it together at school and collapse at home. If your child is 'fine at school' but melting down every evening, they are NOT fine — they are masking at unsustainable cost. Document the home impact and present to the IEP team. (5) Triennial re-evaluation every 3 years — do NOT waive. Request updated FBA focused on demand avoidance. (6) If approaches aren't working, request: change in behavioral methodology, updated FBA, IEE at district expense, change in placement (more flexible environment). (7) Connect with other PDA parents for strategy sharing — PDA Society forum, PDA North America.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Create a PDA IEP monitoring calendar: annual IEP date, triennial date, quarterly progress checks.
STEP 2: 30 days before annual IEP: write updated Parent Concerns Letter describing current PDA presentation, what's working, what's not, new concerns.
STEP 3: At each review, assess: (a) Are accommodations being implemented? (b) Is the BIP approach still PDA-appropriate? (c) Has school avoidance improved or worsened? (d) Is masking being addressed? (e) Are staff trained on PDA? (f) Is the crisis plan current?
STEP 4: Request data: attendance records, behavioral incident reports, staff implementation logs.
STEP 5: If not progressing: request IEP meeting to revise approach, consider FBA update, consider change of placement.
STEP 6: Every 3 years (triennial): participate in re-evaluation. Request updated assessment of PDA profile, anxiety, and functional impact.

CONTACT: IEP CASE MANAGER: Your child's SpEd teacher — primary contact.
ADVOCACY:
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free IEP advocacy
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• TASK: taskca.org
• Matrix: matrixparents.org
IF ACCOMMODATIONS NOT IMPLEMENTED:
• Document in writing to SpEd coordinator
• Request IEP meeting
• If unresolved: CDE complaint (cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp)
PDA MONITORING:
• PDA Society education: pdasociety.org.uk/education
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org

DOCUMENTS: ONGOING DOCUMENTS:
• Annual IEP documents (keep every version)
• Parent Concerns Letters (annual)
• Quarterly progress reports
• Attendance records
• Behavioral incident reports
• Staff training documentation
• Crisis plan (updated annually)
• Communication log with school
• Triennial evaluation reports (every 3 years)

PARENT MONITORING BINDER:
• Section 1: Current IEP + BIP
• Section 2: Evaluations
• Section 3: Progress reports
• Section 4: Correspondence
• Section 5: PDA resources shared with school
• Section 6: Crisis plan

TIMELINE: Annual IEP review. Triennial reassessment every 3 years. Parent can request review anytime.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Skip annual IEP reviews or go unprepared. (2) Accept the same accommodations year after year without checking effectiveness. (3) Waive triennial re-evaluation — PDA presentation changes as children develop, updated data is critical. (4) Let services decrease without evidence that PDA accommodation needs have decreased. (5) Assume PDA 'goes away' — it's a lifelong neurological profile that requires ongoing support. (6) Stop monitoring implementation — PDA accommodations are often the first dropped by new teachers who 'don't believe in special treatment.'

RISKS: Goals changed to compliance-based without parent agreement. PDA-trained staff leave — replacements use traditional approaches. Success at school (masking) used to justify reducing supports. After-school collapse worsens but school claims child is 'doing great.'

RESOURCES: PDA NORTH AMERICA: pdanorthamerica.org — free PDA guide, webinars, peer support groups
PDA SOCIETY UK: pdasociety.org.uk — online courses, practice guidance
REFRAMING AUTISM: reframingautism.org.au — neurodiversity-affirming resources
