insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 7.2: Transition Planning (Age 14+)

PDA — Step 7.2: Transition Planning (Age 14+)

WHAT TO DO: PLAN TRANSITION WITH PDA-SPECIFIC CONSIDERATIONS: (1) Transition for PDA is uniquely complex because traditional transition goals (employment, independent living, post-secondary education) involve HIGH-DEMAND environments. (2) ITP (Individual Transition Plan) by age 16 — include: flexible post-secondary pathway (gap year, part-time college, online learning, vocational with accommodations), career exploration focused on autonomy and interest-driven work (many PDA adults thrive in self-employment, creative fields, or roles with high autonomy and low external demand). (3) Self-advocacy is CRITICAL: teach your child to understand their PDA profile, recognize demand overload, communicate needs ('I need a break,' 'I can do this differently'), and request accommodations. (4) College disability services: student must self-identify. IEP doesn't transfer but documentation does. Request accommodations: flexible attendance, extended deadlines, alternative assignments, reduced course load, testing modifications. (5) DOR (Dept of Rehabilitation) may provide vocational support: dor.ca.gov. (6) If ASD diagnosis: IHSS, SSI may continue into adulthood. Regional Center services continue through adult life. (7) CalABLE account: calable.ca.gov — available for disability-related savings. (8) Housing: independent living may require supported living services through RC. Explore gradual independence with support.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 14: request ITP (Individual Transition Plan) development within IEP. Include self-advocacy, career exploration, and independence goals.
STEP 2: Career exploration: help your child identify interests and strengths. PDA-friendly career characteristics: autonomy, flexibility, interest-driven, limited rigid hierarchy. Many PDA adults succeed in creative, entrepreneurial, and self-employed roles.
STEP 3: Self-advocacy: teach your child to understand and explain their PDA profile. Role-play: 'I work best when I can manage my own schedule. I need flexibility and autonomy. I have anxiety around rigid demands.'
STEP 4: By age 15-16: prepare for SAT/ACT accommodations (if college-bound). Current evaluation + accommodation history needed. College Board SSD: collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities.
STEP 5: College prep: identify colleges with strong disability services. Visit offices during tours. Community college as low-demand entry point.
STEP 6: DOR referral at age 16: dor.ca.gov — vocational support, job coaching, workplace accommodations.

CONTACT: IEP TEAM: Transition planning at IEP meetings starting age 14.
DOR: dor.ca.gov — vocational rehabilitation (referral at age 16)
SAT/ACT:
• College Board SSD: collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities — (844) 255-7728
• ACT: act.org/the-act/accommodations — (319) 337-1332
COLLEGE DISABILITY SERVICES:
• AHEAD: ahead.org — college disability resource
• Each college's disability office
PDA ADULT RESOURCES:
• PDA Society adult resources: pdasociety.org.uk/life-with-pda/adults
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org
• Harry Thompson's 'The PDA Paradox' (adult perspective)
CalABLE: calable.ca.gov — if applicable

DOCUMENTS: TRANSITION DOCUMENTS:
• ITP in IEP (starting age 14+)
• Transition assessments (interests, strengths, needs)
• Self-advocacy plan
• Career exploration results
• SAT/ACT accommodation application (6+ months before test)
• College disability documentation packet
• DOR referral and IPE (Individual Plan for Employment)
• Summary of Performance (from school at graduation)

COLLEGE PREP PACKET:
• Most recent evaluation (within 3 years)
• IEP/504 history (accommodation documentation)
• PDA Profile Summary (adapted for college disability office)
• Clinician letter describing PDA and needed accommodations

TIMELINE: Transition plan required by age 16 (14 recommended).

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Wait until senior year — start transition planning at age 14+. (2) Assume PDA means your child can't work or attend college — many PDA adults are highly successful in self-directed, flexible environments. (3) Push your child toward a career path that involves high demand compliance (rigid schedules, strict hierarchies) — PDA adults thrive in autonomous roles. (4) Skip self-advocacy skill development — understanding their own PDA profile is the most important transition skill. (5) Forget to build accommodation documentation history for college SAT/ACT requests. (6) Ignore DOR (Department of Rehabilitation) services — available at age 16.

RISKS: 'Failure to launch' — PDA youth may struggle with post-secondary demands (college, employment, independent living). Traditional transition programs are demand-heavy. Self-advocacy training uses directive approach that triggers PDA. College disability office unfamiliar with PDA. Employment environments are inherently demand-heavy.

RESOURCES: PDA NORTH AMERICA: pdanorthamerica.org — free PDA guide, webinars, peer support groups
PDA SOCIETY UK: pdasociety.org.uk — online courses, practice guidance
REFRAMING AUTISM: reframingautism.org.au — neurodiversity-affirming resources
