insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$PDA Step 7.3: Adult PDA — Ongoing Support & Self-Advocacy

PDA — Step 7.3: Adult PDA — Ongoing Support & Self-Advocacy

WHAT TO DO: SUPPORT ADULT PDA — LIFELONG MANAGEMENT AND SELF-ADVOCACY: (1) PDA does not resolve in adulthood — it evolves. Many PDA adults develop sophisticated coping strategies but remain fundamentally demand-avoidant. (2) Key adult PDA resources: 'The PDA Paradox' by Harry Thompson (adult PDA lived experience), PDA Society adult resources, PDA North America adult community. (3) Adult PDA challenges: employment (traditional 9-5 with boss demands is extremely difficult), relationships (partner demands trigger avoidance), healthcare (appointment demands, medication compliance demands), self-care (basic demands of daily living). (4) Successful PDA adults often: work for themselves or in high-autonomy roles, structure their environment to minimize external demands, use technology to manage demands (reminders, automation), have partners/supports who understand PDA. (5) Mental health: anxiety, depression, burnout, and autistic burnout are very common in PDA adults. Ensure ongoing mental health support with a PDA-aware therapist. (6) Benefits continuation: if receiving SSI/IHSS/RC services, navigate adult re-determination (SSI redetermination at 18, ongoing IHSS annual reviews). (7) Self-advocacy resources: ASAN (Autistic Self Advocacy Network), PDA Society, local self-advocacy organizations. (8) Legal planning: SDM (Supported Decision-Making) preferred over conservatorship — PDA adults can make their own decisions with appropriate support.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 18: ensure continuity of services. If RC-eligible: continue IPP with adult services. If employed: request workplace accommodations under ADA.
STEP 2: RC Self-Determination Program: perfect for PDA — YOU direct your own services and budget. Apply through your service coordinator.
STEP 3: Workplace accommodations (ADA): flexible schedule, remote work options, reduced meetings, written instructions instead of verbal, autonomy in task management. File ADA accommodation request with employer.
STEP 4: Ongoing mental health: continue PDA-informed therapy. Monitor for burnout, anxiety, depression.
STEP 5: Build PDA-affirming social network: PDANA community, autistic adult groups, online PDA communities.
STEP 6: Financial planning: CalABLE account (calable.ca.gov), SSI (if applicable), Special Needs Trust for assets above SSI limits.

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER: Lifelong services if ASD-eligible. Self-Determination Program.
WORKPLACE ACCOMMODATIONS:
• ADA: ada.gov — accommodation information
• JAN (Job Accommodation Network): askjan.org — (800) 526-7234 — free workplace accommodation consultation
• DOR: dor.ca.gov — ongoing vocational support
MENTAL HEALTH:
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com — find PDA-informed therapist
• NAMI CA: namica.org — support groups
FINANCIAL:
• CalABLE: calable.ca.gov
• SSI: ssa.gov — (800) 772-1213
• SNT attorneys: CA Special Needs Trust attorneys through DRC referral
PDA ADULT COMMUNITY:
• PDA Society (adults): pdasociety.org.uk/life-with-pda/adults
• PDANA: pdanorthamerica.org

DOCUMENTS: ADULT DOCUMENTS:
• RC IPP (ongoing, with Self-Determination if applicable)
• ADA accommodation request (employer)
• DOR IPE (if using vocational services)
• SSI application/records (if applicable)
• CalABLE account setup
• Special Needs Trust (if applicable)
• Mental health treatment records
• PDA Profile Summary (adult version — for employers, providers, colleges)

TIMELINE: SSI re-evaluation at age 18. RC transition to adult services.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Assume adult services will be as structured as school-based services — adults must self-advocate. (2) Drop all professional support at age 18/22 — PDA is lifelong. (3) Ignore mental health — PDA adults have high rates of anxiety, depression, and burnout. (4) Force traditional employment model — PDA adults may need accommodations, flexible schedules, or self-employment pathways. (5) Forget about Regional Center — if ASD-eligible, RC provides lifelong services (Self-Determination Program is particularly PDA-compatible).

RISKS: Adult PDA under-recognized. Workplace demands cause burnout and job loss. SSI evaluators unfamiliar with PDA. Mental health providers treat anxiety/depression without understanding PDA root. Relationship difficulties from demand sensitivity. Burnout from years of masking.

RESOURCES: PDA NORTH AMERICA: pdanorthamerica.org — free PDA guide, webinars, peer support groups
PDA SOCIETY UK: pdasociety.org.uk — online courses, practice guidance
REFRAMING AUTISM: reframingautism.org.au — neurodiversity-affirming resources
