ADHD — Complete Family Navigation Journey (California)

24 steps across 8 phases:
* 1 Recognition & Concern: Parent/Teacher Notices Attention, Behavior, or Lea, Well-Child Visit / Pediatrician Discussion
* 2 Evaluation & Diagnosis: Comprehensive ADHD Evaluation, Receive ADHD Diagnosis & Understand Presentation
* 3 Treatment Planning: Develop Multimodal Treatment Plan, Navigate ADHD Medication (If Applicable), Behavioral Parent Training & Family Strategies
* 4 School Supports (IEP/504): Request School District Evaluation, School District Evaluation — ADHD-Focused, IEP Development — ADHD-Specific Accommodations & G, 504 Plan Alternative (If IEP Denied or Preferred), IEP/504 Dispute Resolution
* 5 Insurance & Therapy: Insurance Authorization for ADHD Treatment, Access Behavioral & Organizational Therapy
* 6 Home & Daily Life Management: Build ADHD-Friendly Home Environment, Executive Function Skill Building
* 7 Co-occurring Conditions & Complex ADHD: Screen & Address Anxiety (Co-occurs ~40%), Screen & Address Learning Disabilities (Co-occurs , Screen & Address Behavioral/Conduct Problems (Co-o, Address Emotional Dysregulation
* 8 Long-Term Planning & Transition: Ongoing Medication Management & Monitoring, Annual IEP/504 Review — ADHD-Specific Monitoring, Transition Planning (Age 14+), Adult ADHD — Ongoing Support$kb$, $kb$journey_adhd$kb$, $kb$jrn-adhd-overview$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-adhd-overview","title":"ADHD Journey Map — Overview","category":"journey-adhd","subcategory":"overview","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 1.1: Parent/Teacher Notices Attention, Behavior, or Learning Concerns

ADHD — Step 1.1: Parent/Teacher Notices Attention, Behavior, or Learning Concerns

WHAT TO DO: TRACK AND DOCUMENT ADHD-RELATED CONCERNS SYSTEMATICALLY: (1) ADHD affects ~9.8% of children ages 3-17 in the US — it is the most commonly diagnosed neurodevelopmental disorder. (2) Three presentations: INATTENTIVE (difficulty focusing, disorganized, forgetful, loses things, doesn't follow through — often missed, especially in girls), HYPERACTIVE-IMPULSIVE (fidgety, can't sit still, talks excessively, interrupts, acts without thinking), COMBINED (most common, both features). (3) Start a BEHAVIOR LOG: for 2 weeks, document daily observations across settings — school behavior (from teacher), homework battles, social interactions, emotional reactions, sleep quality, appetite. (4) Request TEACHER INPUT: email the teacher: 'I have concerns about [child's] attention/focus/behavior. Can you share your observations about how they're performing compared to peers? I'm particularly interested in: staying on task, following multi-step directions, organization, completing work, social interactions, and impulsivity.' (5) Note: ADHD symptoms must be present in 2+ settings and cause functional impairment. Track both home AND school. (6) Video-record examples if possible — homework sessions, morning routines, emotional outbursts. (7) Girls with ADHD are significantly under-diagnosed — they more often present inattentive (daydreamy, quiet, disorganized) rather than hyperactive.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Create a dated Behavior Log with columns: Date, Setting (home/school), What Happened, Duration, Your Response, What Helped/Didn't. Track for minimum 2 weeks.
STEP 2: Email your child's teacher: 'I've been observing some attention/focus challenges with [child] at home. Can we schedule a brief meeting to compare notes? I'd like to understand how [child] is doing with: sustained attention, task completion, following directions, staying organized, peer interactions, and self-regulation compared to same-age peers.'
STEP 3: At the teacher meeting, ask: 'Would you be willing to complete a behavioral rating scale if I provide one? My pediatrician may request a Vanderbilt or Conners teacher form.'
STEP 4: Check developmental milestones: Is your child significantly behind peers in executive function tasks (organization, planning, time management, emotional regulation)? ADHD is essentially a developmental delay in executive function.
STEP 5: Complete the Vanderbilt Parent Assessment Scale yourself (available free at nichq.org/adhd-toolkit). This is the #1 tool pediatricians use.
STEP 6: Schedule a well-child visit or dedicated ADHD concern appointment with your pediatrician. Bring: your behavior log, completed Vanderbilt parent form, teacher observations, report cards, and any prior standardized test scores.

CONTACT: YOUR CHILD'S TEACHER: First point of contact — request meeting and ask for behavioral observations.
PEDIATRICIAN: Will likely conduct initial screening using Vanderbilt Assessment Scales.
SCHOOL COUNSELOR: Can provide observations across settings.
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• Your school district — request SST (Student Study Team) meeting if school concerns exist
• CHADD (Children and Adults with ADHD): chadd.org — parent education, local chapters throughout CA
• ADDitude Magazine: additudemag.com — free resources, expert webinars
• NICHQ ADHD Toolkit: nichq.org/adhd-toolkit — free Vanderbilt forms
• Understood.org: understood.org — screeners, explanations, tech tools
• CA PTI: parentcenterhub.org — find your local Parent Training & Information center by county

DOCUMENTS: BEGIN COLLECTING:
• Behavior log (dated daily observations, 2+ weeks)
• Report cards (last 2-3 years — look for comments about focus/behavior)
• Standardized test scores (SBAC/STAR)
• Teacher comments and progress notes
• Teacher observation notes (from meeting)
• Completed Vanderbilt Parent Assessment Scale
• Your child's work samples showing pattern (incomplete work, careless errors, disorganization)
• Homework time log (expected vs actual time)
• Sleep log (ADHD often disrupts sleep)
• Video recordings of concerning behavior (if available)
• Any prior evaluations or school intervention records

TIMELINE: Ongoing — earlier identification = better outcomes. Average age of diagnosis is 7, but inattentive type often not identified until middle school.

AVOID: DO NOT: Parent or teacher observes patterns: difficulty sustaining attention, excessive fidgeting/movement, impulsive behavior (blurting out, not waiting turns), trouble organizing tasks, frequently losing things, difficulty following multi-step instructions, emotional dysregulation (big reactions, low frustration tolerance) | May also notice: daydreaming (inattentive presentation), hyperfocus on preferred activities but inability to engage with non-preferred tasks, social difficulties, homework battles lasting hours

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Girls/AFAB children with inattentive-type ADHD overlooked because they're quiet and 'well-behaved.' Behaviors attributed to laziness, lack of motivation, or poor parenting. Gifted children with ADHD ('twice exceptional'/2e) mask through high IQ. C

RISKS: Girls/AFAB children with inattentive-type ADHD overlooked because they're quiet and 'well-behaved.' Behaviors attributed to laziness, lack of motivation, or poor parenting. Gifted children with ADHD ('twice exceptional'/2e) mask through high IQ. Cultural differences in how attention and behavior are perceived. Teachers may resist raising ADHD possibility due to fear of 'labeling.'

RESOURCES: CHADD: chadd.org — Parent-to-Parent training (FREE), local chapters, webinars, Attention Magazine
ADDITUDE: additudemag.com — free expert webinars, articles, checklists
UNDERSTOOD: understood.org — ADHD parent guides, tech tools, community
CDC ADHD RESOURCES: cdc.gov/adhd — treatment information, data
BOOKS: 'Taking Charge of ADHD' (Barkley), 'The Explosive Child' (Greene), 'Smart but Scattered' (Dawson/Guare), 'What Your ADHD Child Wishes You Knew' (Saline)
CA PTI NETWORK: parentcenterhub.org
MATRIX: matrixparents.org
TASK: taskca.org

PRIMARY AGENCY: None (Parent/Teacher-initiated)
OTHER AGENCIES: Pediatrician
