insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 1.2: Well-Child Visit / Pediatrician Discussion

ADHD — Step 1.2: Well-Child Visit / Pediatrician Discussion

WHAT TO DO: DISCUSS ADHD CONCERNS WITH YOUR PEDIATRICIAN: (1) AAP (American Academy of Pediatrics) guidelines recommend ADHD evaluation for any child age 4-18 with academic or behavioral problems and symptoms of inattention, hyperactivity, or impulsivity. (2) Bring ALL your documentation: behavior log, completed Vanderbilt parent form, teacher observations, report cards. (3) Ask the pediatrician: 'I'd like to discuss an ADHD evaluation for [child]. I have concerns about [specific symptoms]. Can you initiate the Vanderbilt Assessment process?' (4) The pediatrician should: provide Vanderbilt forms for parent AND teacher (BOTH required), review your concerns, conduct a physical exam to rule out other causes (thyroid, sleep disorders, vision/hearing), and potentially refer for comprehensive evaluation. (5) CRITICAL: Pediatrician screening alone is often insufficient for complex cases — if there are concerns about co-occurring conditions (anxiety, LD, ASD), request referral to a developmental-behavioral pediatrician, child psychologist, or neuropsychologist. (6) Ask about AAP ADHD guidelines: for ages 4-5, behavioral therapy is first-line (before medication); for ages 6+, FDA-approved medication AND behavioral therapy together is recommended. (7) Request vision AND hearing screening — these can mimic ADHD symptoms.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Schedule a dedicated appointment (not a quick add-on to a sick visit). Tell scheduling: 'I need a 30-minute appointment to discuss behavioral/attention concerns.'
STEP 2: Bring to appointment: your behavior log, completed Vanderbilt Parent form, teacher observations, report cards, standardized test scores, any videos.
STEP 3: Say to pediatrician: 'I have concerns about ADHD. I've been tracking [child's] behavior for [X weeks] and have completed the Vanderbilt Parent Assessment. I've also gathered teacher input. Can we initiate a formal ADHD evaluation?'
STEP 4: Pediatrician provides Vanderbilt Teacher Assessment Scale — give to teacher (ideally 2+ teachers for accuracy). Ask teacher to return within 2 weeks.
STEP 5: Ask pediatrician: 'Based on the severity of symptoms, should we also pursue (a) a comprehensive neuropsychological evaluation, (b) school district evaluation, or (c) both?'
STEP 6: If co-occurring concerns exist (anxiety, learning difficulties, ASD features): request referral to developmental-behavioral pediatrician or neuropsychologist for comprehensive assessment. In CA, wait times for dev-behavioral pediatricians can be 3-6 months — get on the list NOW.
STEP 7: Return for follow-up once Vanderbilt teacher forms are completed (typically 2-4 weeks).

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN: Your child's primary care provider — initiates Vanderbilt screening process.
DEVELOPMENTAL-BEHAVIORAL PEDIATRICIAN: For complex cases. Ask pediatrician for referral. In CA:
• UCSF Developmental-Behavioral Pediatrics: (415) 353-2101
• Stanford ADHD Clinic: (650) 723-5511
• UC Davis MIND Institute: (916) 703-0280
• UCLA Semel Institute: (310) 825-9989
• Children's Hospital Los Angeles: (323) 361-2471
• Children's Hospital of Orange County: (714) 509-3939
CHILD PSYCHOLOGIST: For comprehensive testing — ask pediatrician for referral or search Psychology Today (psychologytoday.com) filtered by 'ADHD' and your city.
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• CHADD local chapters: chadd.org/affiliate-locator
• NICHQ ADHD Toolkit (free Vanderbilt forms): nichq.org
• CA Academy of Pediatrics: calaap.org

DOCUMENTS: BRING TO PEDIATRICIAN:
• Behavior log (2+ weeks)
• Completed Vanderbilt Parent Assessment Scale
• Report cards (last 2-3 years)
• Standardized test scores
• Teacher observation notes
• Work samples
• Sleep log
• Family history of ADHD (genetic component is strong — 75% heritability)

PEDIATRICIAN PROVIDES:
• Vanderbilt Teacher Assessment Scale forms (provide to teacher)
• Physical exam results
• Vision/hearing screening referral
• Referral to specialist (if needed)
• AAP ADHD guidelines discussion

AFTER VISIT:
• Track teacher form completion (2-week turnaround)

TIMELINE: Can be initiated at any well-child visit. Evaluation may take 1-3 visits depending on complexity.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Pediatrician dismisses concerns ('just a boy thing,' 'they'll grow out of it'). Only parent rating collected, no teacher input. Brief office visit insufficient for thorough evaluation. Pediatrician jumps to medication without full evaluation. Co-occurring conditions (anxiety, learning disabilities) not screened. Parent pressured to medicate or pressured NOT to medicate by conflicting family opinions.

RISKS: Pediatrician dismisses concerns ('just a boy thing,' 'they'll grow out of it'). Only parent rating collected, no teacher input. Brief office visit insufficient for thorough evaluation. Pediatrician jumps to medication without full evaluation. Co-occurring conditions (anxiety, learning disabilities) not screened. Parent pressured to medicate or pressured NOT to medicate by conflicting family opinions.

RESOURCES: CHADD: chadd.org — Parent-to-Parent training (FREE), local chapters, webinars, Attention Magazine
ADDITUDE: additudemag.com — free expert webinars, articles, checklists
UNDERSTOOD: understood.org — ADHD parent guides, tech tools, community
