insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 2.1: Comprehensive ADHD Evaluation

ADHD — Step 2.1: Comprehensive ADHD Evaluation

WHAT TO DO: Full evaluation per AAP guidelines should include: (1) DSM-5 criteria assessment — 6+ symptoms of inattention and/or 6+ hyperactivity-impulsivity symptoms, present before age 12, in 2+ settings, for 6+ months. (2) Validated rating scales from multiple informants (parent, teacher, child if older). (3) Developmental and medical history. (4) Assessment of functional impairment. (5) Screening for co-occurring conditions (78% of children with ADHD have at least one: anxiety ~40%, behavior/conduct problems ~50%, learning disabilities ~30-50%, depression, autism, Tourette syndrome). (6) Rule out other causes. Can be conducted by: pediatrician, child psychiatrist, psychologist, neuropsychologist, developmental pediatrician.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: If pursuing comprehensive evaluation (recommended for complex presentations): call insurance for referral requirements: 'Does my plan cover neuropsychological or psychological testing? Do I need a referral?'
STEP 2: If using Vanderbilt-only pathway (appropriate for straightforward ADHD): ensure BOTH parent AND teacher Vanderbilt forms are completed and scored. Child must meet criteria in both settings.
STEP 3: For comprehensive evaluation, provide evaluator with ALL documentation: behavior log, Vanderbilt scores, report cards, teacher input, work samples, any school intervention data.
STEP 4: Comprehensive evaluation should include: clinical interview (parent and child), behavioral observation, rating scales from multiple informants (parent, 2+ teachers), cognitive testing (WISC-V — important to identify co-occurring LD), achievement testing (to rule out LD as primary cause), executive function assessment (BRIEF-2), continuous performance test (CPT — measures sustained attention), social-emotional assessment, adaptive behavior.
STEP 5: Ask evaluator: 'Will you screen for co-occurring conditions? Up to 60% of ADHD children have at least one co-occurring condition (anxiety, LD, ODD, ASD).'
STEP 6: Request full written report with DSM-5 diagnosis, specific recommendations for school and home, and medication recommendation (if applicable).

CONTACT: EVALUATORS:
• Neuropsychologist: Most comprehensive ($3K-$6K, insurance may cover)
• Child psychologist: Thorough ($1.5K-$3K)
• Developmental-behavioral pediatrician: Medical-focused
• Pediatrician: Vanderbilt screening (quickest, least comprehensive)
FIND CA EVALUATORS:
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com — filter by ADHD, Testing/Evaluation, your CA city
• CHADD professional directory: chadd.org
• Ask pediatrician for referral network
• UC system clinics: often shorter waits than private (UCSF, UCLA, Stanford, UC Davis)
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• Insurance: call number on back of card — ask for 'psychological testing' benefits
• Medi-Cal: covers through managed care — request referral from PCP
• Regional Center: generally does NOT serve ADHD-only (ADHD is not a Lanterman Act qualifying condi

DOCUMENTS: FOR THE EVALUATION:
• All documentation collected in Steps 1.1-1.2
• Completed Vanderbilt Parent AND Teacher forms
• Insurance pre-authorization (if required)
• Referral from pediatrician (if required)

EVALUATOR PRODUCES:
• Comprehensive evaluation report including:
  - Clinical interview summary
  - Behavioral observation
  - Cognitive testing results (IQ, processing speed, working memory)
  - Achievement testing results
  - Executive function assessment (BRIEF-2)
  - Rating scale results (Vanderbilt, Conners, BASC-3)
  - DSM-5 diagnosis with presentation type
  - Co-occurring condition screening results
  - Specific recommendations for school, home, therapy, medication
• Summary letter for school (if requested)

TIMELINE: Pediatrician: 1-3 visits. Specialist wait: 3-12 months for dev ped, 1-4 months for psychologist. Private neuropsych: 2-6 weeks but $$.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: EVALUATION GAPS: Diagnosis based solely on brief office visit without teacher input. 3 DSM-5 presentations (Predominantly Inattentive, Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive, Combined) not differentiated — affects treatment. Co-occurring conditions missed (especially anxiety, which mimics and co-occurs with ADHD in ~40% of cases). Learning disabilities not screened — child gets ADHD diagnosis but underlying LD unaddressed. IQ testing not done — 2e children missed. Evaluation focuses on deficits rather than also documenting strengths.

RISKS: ★ EVALUATION GAPS: Diagnosis based solely on brief office visit without teacher input. 3 DSM-5 presentations (Predominantly Inattentive, Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive, Combined) not differentiated — affects treatment. Co-occurring conditions missed (especially anxiety, which mimics and co-occurs with ADHD in ~40% of cases). Learning disabilities not screened — child gets ADHD diagnosis but underlying LD unaddressed. IQ testing not done — 2e children missed. Evaluation focuses on deficits rather than also documenting strengths.

