ADHD — Step 2.2: Receive ADHD Diagnosis & Understand Presentation

WHAT TO DO: DSM-5 recognizes three ADHD presentations: (1) Predominantly Inattentive (formerly ADD) — difficulty sustaining attention, organizing, following through, often overlooked in girls. (2) Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive — fidgeting, excessive talking, difficulty waiting, acting without thinking. (3) Combined Presentation — meets criteria for both. ADHD severity: Mild, Moderate, Severe. 2022 NSCH data: among 6.5M children with current ADHD, 36% mild, 52% moderate, 12% severe. Key: ADHD is a RECOGNIZED DSM-5 diagnosis (314.00/314.01) with established diagnostic codes — unlike PDA, this is widely accepted by clinicians, insurance, and schools.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Review the evaluation report carefully. Note: ADHD presentation type (Inattentive, Hyperactive-Impulsive, Combined), severity level (Mild, Moderate, Severe), co-occurring conditions identified, specific recommendations.
STEP 2: Ask the evaluator: 'What does this mean for my child's daily life? What interventions have the strongest evidence? Do you recommend medication, behavioral therapy, or both?'
STEP 3: AAP RECOMMENDS by age group: Ages 4-5: Behavioral parent training FIRST (evidence-based programs: Triple P, Incredible Years, Parent-Child Interaction Therapy). Ages 6-11: FDA-approved ADHD medication AND behavioral therapy (combined is most effective). Ages 12+: Medication with adolescent's input, plus skills training.
STEP 4: Prepare to share the diagnosis with: (a) your child (age-appropriate explanation: 'Your brain works differently — it's very creative and energetic, AND it needs some extra help with focus and organization'), (b) school (evaluation report supports IEP/504 request), (c) family members (education reduces stigma).
STEP 5: Join CHADD (chadd.org) for ongoing parent education. Attend a local Parent-to-Parent support group.
STEP 6: Request a copy of the evaluation report for your records — you'll need it for school, insurance, and future providers.

CONTACT: EVALUATOR/DIAGNOSING CLINICIAN: Review results and ask questions.
PEDIATRICIAN: Share results — they'll coordinate medication if recommended.
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• CHADD Local Chapters: chadd.org/affiliate-locator — find your nearest chapter for parent education and support groups (chapters throughout CA: SF Bay Area, LA, San Diego, Sacramento, Orange County)
• ADDitude Magazine: additudemag.com — free expert webinars, downloadable guides
• Understood.org: understood.org — ADHD-specific resources
• LDA of CA: ldaca.org — if co-occurring LD identified
• NAMI CA: namica.org — mental health support if co-occurring anxiety/depression
FOR YOUR CHILD:
• CHADD's 'What is ADHD?' youth resources
• Understood.org child-friendly explanations
• Books: 'The Survival Guide for Kids with ADHD' by John Taylo

DOCUMENTS: DIAGNOSIS DOCUMENTS:
• Complete evaluation report (keep original + copies)
• DSM-5 diagnostic summary (presentation type, severity)
• Recommendations section (extract for school and providers)
• Summary letter (request if not included)

SHARE WITH:
• Pediatrician (original or copy of full report)
• School (copy for IEP/504 request)
• Insurance (may need for pre-authorization of services)
• Future providers (therapists, tutors)

PARENT CREATES:
• 1-page 'ADHD Profile' summary (diagnosis, presentation type, key recommendations, what helps/hinders)
• Service coordination binder (start organizing all ADHD-related documents)

TIMELINE: Diagnosis typically within 1-3 visits after initial evaluation.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Diagnosis documentation insufficient for school/insurance needs. Presentation type not specified. Co-occurring conditions not documented. Parent receives diagnosis without adequate explanation of what ADHD is and isn't. Grief, guilt, relief — complex emotional response not addressed. Misinformation from social media about ADHD.

RISKS: Diagnosis documentation insufficient for school/insurance needs. Presentation type not specified. Co-occurring conditions not documented. Parent receives diagnosis without adequate explanation of what ADHD is and isn't. Grief, guilt, relief — complex emotional response not addressed. Misinformation from social media about ADHD.

