ADHD — Step 3.1: Develop Multimodal Treatment Plan

WHAT TO DO: AAP recommends multimodal approach: (1) For ages 4-5: behavioral therapy FIRST, medication only if therapy insufficient. (2) For ages 6-11: BOTH medication AND behavioral therapy (gold standard). (3) For ages 12+: medication with consent of adolescent, behavioral therapy recommended. Treatment components: ADHD medication, behavioral parent training, school accommodations/interventions, organizational skills training, social skills support, executive function coaching. Evidence base: MTA study (largest ADHD treatment study) showed combination of medication + behavioral therapy produces best outcomes.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: With your pediatrician or evaluator, create a written treatment plan that includes: behavioral interventions (parent training, school accommodations, organizational strategies), medication (if recommended and consented), therapy (CBT, social skills, organizational coaching), school supports (IEP or 504). This is MULTIMODAL — no single intervention is sufficient for most children.
STEP 2: Prioritize based on age and severity: (a) Ages 4-5: Behavioral parent training first — enroll in evidence-based program (Triple P, Incredible Years, PCIT). (b) Ages 6+: Behavioral + medication consultation simultaneously. (c) All ages: School accommodations immediately.
STEP 3: Create a treatment timeline: Week 1-2: school evaluation request + medication consultation. Month 1: behavioral parent training enrollment + 504/IEP development. Month 2-3: therapist/coach identified. Month 4+: monitor, adjust, maintain.
STEP 4: For each provider, ensure they have the full evaluation report and your 1-page ADHD Profile.
STEP 5: Designate yourself as the CARE COORDINATOR — you connect the pediatrician, school, therapist, and any other providers. Create a shared communication plan.
STEP 6: Set review milestones: 30 days (initial medication adjustment), 90 days (comprehensive check-in), 6 months (full review of all interventions).

CONTACT: TREATMENT TEAM:
• Pediatrician: medication management, overall coordination
• Child psychiatrist: complex medication needs (stimulants + additional medications)
• Behavioral therapist/psychologist: CBT, parent training, skills coaching
• School IEP/504 team: academic accommodations
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• CHADD: chadd.org — parent education courses (virtual and in-person)
• Triple P (Positive Parenting Program): triplep.net — evidence-based parent training, CA locations
• Incredible Years: incredibleyears.com — evidence-based parent training
• PCIT (Parent-Child Interaction Therapy): pcit.org — for ages 2-7, CA providers listed
• ADHD Coaches Organization: adhdcoaches.org — find ADHD coach for teens+
• Medi-Cal Behavioral Health: through your managed care plan
• Regional Center: only if ADHD co

DOCUMENTS: TREATMENT PLAN DOCUMENTS:
• Written multimodal treatment plan (create with pediatrician)
• Medication consent form (if applicable)
• Behavioral parent training enrollment
• Insurance pre-authorizations for therapy
• School evaluation request letter (copy)
• Provider contact sheet (all providers, phone, email)
• Treatment timeline with milestones
• Communication plan for providers

ONGOING TRACKING:
• Medication log (dose, time, effects, side effects)
• Behavior tracking (weekly rating 1-10: focus, impulsivity, emotional regulation, homework, sleep)
• School performance (grades, teacher feedback)
• Therapy progress notes

TIMELINE: Treatment plan should be developed within weeks of diagnosis.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: TREATMENT TRAPS: Medication prescribed without behavioral component. Behavioral therapy recommended without medication for moderate-severe ADHD in school-age children (undertreated). Only medication OR only therapy when both are needed. Family opposed to medication based on stigma/misinformation — child undertreated. Medication prescribed without monitoring plan. No school accommodations discussed alongside clinical treatment.

RISKS: ★ TREATMENT TRAPS: Medication prescribed without behavioral component. Behavioral therapy recommended without medication for moderate-severe ADHD in school-age children (undertreated). Only medication OR only therapy when both are needed. Family opposed to medication based on stigma/misinformation — child undertreated. Medication prescribed without monitoring plan. No school accommodations discussed alongside clinical treatment.

