ADHD — Step 3.2: Navigate ADHD Medication (If Applicable)

WHAT TO DO: ADHD medications: STIMULANTS (first-line, 70-80% response rate): methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta, Focalin) and amphetamine (Adderall, Vyvanse, Dexedrine). Short-acting (4 hrs), intermediate (6-8 hrs), long-acting (10-14 hrs). NON-STIMULANTS (second-line): atomoxetine (Strattera), guanfacine (Intuniv), clonidine (Kapvay), viloxazine (Qelbree). Process: start low, titrate slowly, monitor for efficacy and side effects. Common side effects: appetite suppression, sleep difficulties, mood changes. Medication shortage has been ongoing issue (FDA Adderall shortage 2022-2024).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: ADHD medications: STIMULANTS (first-line, 70-80% response rate): methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta, Focalin) and amphetamine (Adderall, Vyvanse, Dexedrine).
STEP 2: Short-acting (4 hrs), intermediate (6-8 hrs), long-acting (10-14 hrs).
STEP 3: NON-STIMULANTS (second-line): atomoxetine (Strattera), guanfacine (Intuniv), clonidine (Kapvay), viloxazine (Qelbree).
STEP 4: Process: start low, titrate slowly, monitor for efficacy and side effects.
STEP 5: Common side effects: appetite suppression, sleep difficulties, mood changes.

CONTACT: CHADD PROFESSIONAL DIRECTORY: chadd.org — find ADHD specialists
CBT THERAPISTS: psychologytoday.com — filter by ADHD, CBT, children
PARENT TRAINING PROGRAMS: CHADD Parent-to-Parent (free), Triple P, PCIT (ages 2-7)
INSURANCE: Call number on card. ADHD medication + behavioral therapy covered under mental health parity (MHPAEA).
MEDI-CAL: Covers ADHD evaluation, medication, and behavioral therapy.
DMHC: (888) 466-2219 | CDI: (800) 927-4357

DOCUMENTS: MEDICATION DOCUMENTS:
• Medication consent form (signed)
• Medication log: date, medication name, dose, time taken, effects observed, side effects
• Height/weight tracking (stimulants can affect growth)
• Blood pressure/heart rate (if monitored)
• Teacher feedback forms (before and after medication starts)
• Side effect checklist (appetite, sleep, mood, irritability, tics)
• Insurance formulary information
• Prior authorization documentation (if needed)

KEEP FOR RECORDS:
• All medication changes with dates and reasons
• Any adverse reaction documentation
• Annual medication review notes from prescriber

TIMELINE: Titration: 4-8 weeks to find optimal dose. Follow-up: monthly initially, then every 3-6 months.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: MEDICATION CHALLENGES: Medication shortage — 71.5% of adults on stimulants had difficulty filling prescriptions (2024 CDC data). Prior authorization delays for specific medications. Generic vs brand-name differences. Side effects cause parents to discontinue without physician guidance. Medication doesn't cover full school day (wearing off). Diversion/misuse concerns at school. Medication stigma. Growth suppression concerns require monitoring.

RISKS: ★ MEDICATION CHALLENGES: Medication shortage — 71.5% of adults on stimulants had difficulty filling prescriptions (2024 CDC data). Prior authorization delays for specific medications. Generic vs brand-name differences. Side effects cause parents to discontinue without physician guidance. Medication doesn't cover full school day (wearing off). Diversion/misuse concerns at school. Medication stigma. Growth suppression concerns require monitoring.

