insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 3.3: Behavioral Parent Training & Family Strategies

ADHD — Step 3.3: Behavioral Parent Training & Family Strategies

WHAT TO DO: Evidence-based parent training programs: CHADD Parent-to-Parent (free, peer-led), Triple P (Positive Parenting Program), Parent-Child Interaction Therapy (PCIT, ages 2-7), Incredible Years. Key strategies: (1) Positive reinforcement systems (specific praise, token economies). (2) Clear, consistent expectations with visual supports. (3) Environmental modifications (reduce distractions, organize spaces). (4) Routine/structure with flexibility. (5) Break tasks into smaller steps. (6) Movement breaks. (7) Emotional coaching for dysregulation. (8) Collaborative problem-solving (Ross Greene's 'The Explosive Child' approach). Russell Barkley's 'Taking Charge of ADHD' widely recommended.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Find an evidence-based parent training program. AAP recommends for ADHD: Triple P (Positive Parenting Program), Incredible Years, PCIT (Parent-Child Interaction Therapy, ages 2-7), Barkley's Defiant Children program.
STEP 2: Check coverage: Insurance covers parent training when billed as 'family therapy' or 'behavioral therapy.' Medi-Cal covers through managed behavioral health. Some school districts offer free parent workshops.
STEP 3: Enroll in a program — most are 8-12 weekly sessions. Can be group or individual format.
STEP 4: Key strategies you'll learn: clear/specific commands (not 'behave' but 'please put your backpack on the hook by the door'), immediate positive reinforcement, structured routines with visual supports, planned ignoring of minor misbehavior, time-out procedures, daily report cards between school and home.
STEP 5: Practice consistency — ADHD children need MORE consistent structure, not less. Create visual schedules, use timers, establish predictable routines.
STEP 6: Involve all caregivers (both parents, grandparents, babysitters) — inconsistency across caregivers undermines progress.
STEP 7: If your child is age 8+, also consider organizational coaching or ADHD-specific CBT for the child directly.

CONTACT: EVIDENCE-BASED PARENT TRAINING IN CA:
• Triple P: triplep.net — multiple CA locations, also online
• Incredible Years: incredibleyears.com — group format, check local listings
• PCIT International: pcit.org — find CA providers (ages 2-7)
• CHADD Parent-to-Parent: chadd.org — free peer support and education
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• Medi-Cal covers parent training through managed behavioral health plans
• County mental health departments often offer free parenting classes
• Family Resource Centers: frcnca.org — peer support and parent workshops
• SELPA (Special Education Local Plan Area): may offer parent workshops on behavioral strategies
• 211 (dial 2-1-1): United Way resource line — connects to local parenting programs
BOOKS:
• 'Taking Charge of ADHD' by Russell Barkley (gold standard parent gu

DOCUMENTS: PARENT TRAINING DOCUMENTS:
• Program enrollment confirmation
• Session notes/handouts (keep from each session)
• Practice worksheets (daily behavior charts, reward systems)
• Home behavior tracking sheets (from program)
• Insurance/Medi-Cal billing documentation

IMPLEMENTATION TRACKING:
• Daily behavior chart (from parent training program)
• School-home daily report card
• Visual schedule (posted in home)
• Routine checklists (morning, homework, bedtime)
• Reward system tracker
• Weekly parent self-assessment (am I implementing consistently?)

TIMELINE: Parent training: typically 8-16 sessions. Ongoing at home.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Insurance doesn't cover parent training programs. CHADD chapters not available in all areas. Waitlists for behavioral therapists. Parent burnout from implementing strategies consistently. Co-parent/family disagreement on approach. Strategies that work for typical ADHD may not work for ADHD + co-occurring conditions.

RISKS: Insurance doesn't cover parent training programs. CHADD chapters not available in all areas. Waitlists for behavioral therapists. Parent burnout from implementing strategies consistently. Co-parent/family disagreement on approach. Strategies that work for typical ADHD may not work for ADHD + co-occurring conditions.

