ADHD — Step 4.1: Request School District Evaluation

WHAT TO DO: Parent sends written request for special education evaluation. ADHD qualifies under IEP category 'Other Health Impairment' (OHI) — specifically, ADHD causes 'limited alertness with respect to the educational environment' or 'heightened alertness to environmental stimuli' that results in limited alertness to educational tasks. IMPORTANT: ADHD diagnosis alone does NOT guarantee IEP eligibility — must demonstrate that ADHD adversely affects educational performance. Educational performance includes academics AND non-academic domains (social, behavioral, emotional regulation, organizational). OSEP has clarified 'educational performance' is NOT limited to grades.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Parent sends written request for special education evaluation.
STEP 2: ADHD qualifies under IEP category 'Other Health Impairment' (OHI) — specifically, ADHD causes 'limited alertness with respect to the educational environment' or 'heightened alertness to environmental stimuli' that results in limited alertness to educational tasks.
STEP 3: IMPORTANT: ADHD diagnosis alone does NOT guarantee IEP eligibility — must demonstrate that ADHD adversely affects educational performance.
STEP 4: Educational performance includes academics AND non-academic domains (social, behavioral, emotional regulation, organizational).
STEP 5: OSEP has clarified 'educational performance' is NOT limited to grades.

CONTACT: DELIVER YOUR LETTER TO:
• School office (hand-deliver, get date stamp)
• Email to: Principal AND Special Education Director
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• Your school district Special Education department
• CA Dept of Education: cde.ca.gov/sp/se — SpEd information
• CDE Complaint: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp
IF SCHOOL WON'T ACCEPT OR RESPOND:
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — free legal advocacy
• CDE compliance complaint: cde.ca.gov
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org
• DREDF: dredf.org
• Your local SELPA (Special Education Local Plan Area)
• CA PTI: parentcenterhub.org — find your local center
KEY LAW:
• CA Ed Code §56321: 15 calendar days to provide assessment plan after written request

DOCUMENTS: PARENT CREATES:
• Written evaluation request letter (CRITICAL — dated, keep copy with receipt stamp)
• Email copy with read receipt
• List of all areas to be evaluated (attention, executive function, academic achievement, social-emotional, behavioral)

DISTRICT MUST PROVIDE (within 15 days):
• Assessment plan listing all areas to be evaluated
• Parent rights notification (Procedural Safeguards)
• OR: Prior Written Notice if refusing (with reasons)

ALSO PREPARE:
• Copy of ADHD evaluation/diagnosis report
• Behavior log and teacher observations
• Report cards and test scores
• Any prior private evaluations

TIMELINE: District: 15 days to respond with assessment plan. 60 days from consent to completed assessment.

AVOID: DO NOT: IMPORTANT: ADHD diagnosis alone does NOT guarantee IEP eligibility — must demonstrate that ADHD adversely affects educational performance | OSEP has clarified 'educational performance' is NOT limited to grades

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: SCHOOL TRAP: District says child's grades are too good for IEP ('A/B student doesn't need services'). OSEP has clarified that grades alone don't determine eligibility — but many districts still use this gatekeeping. District claims ADHD is 'medical, not educational.' District refuses OHI eligibility because child doesn't have physical health impairment (wrong — ADHD is specifically listed under OHI). Assessment only tests academics, misses behavioral/social/organizational impact. School psychologist doesn't assess executive functioning.

RISKS: ★ SCHOOL TRAP: District says child's grades are too good for IEP ('A/B student doesn't need services'). OSEP has clarified that grades alone don't determine eligibility — but many districts still use this gatekeeping. District claims ADHD is 'medical, not educational.' District refuses OHI eligibility because child doesn't have physical health impairment (wrong — ADHD is specifically listed under OHI). Assessment only tests academics, misses behavioral/social/organizational impact. School psychologist doesn't assess executive functioning.

