ADHD — Step 4.3: IEP Development — ADHD-Specific Accommodations & Goals

WHAT TO DO: If eligible under OHI, IEP team develops plan. KEY ADHD ACCOMMODATIONS: (1) Preferential seating (reduced distractions). (2) Extended time on tests/assignments. (3) Breaks for movement/sensory regulation. (4) Chunked assignments with check-ins. (5) Written/visual instructions (not just verbal). (6) Organizational system support (binder checks, assignment tracking). (7) Reduced homework load. (8) Testing in separate quiet setting. (9) Daily report card/home-school communication. (10) Executive function coaching. (11) Medication administration plan. (12) Teacher training on ADHD. (13) Transition support between activities. IEP GOALS should target: executive functioning, self-regulation, organizational skills, self-advocacy, social skills — NOT just 'paying attention' or 'staying in seat.'

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before IEP meeting, prepare a written 'IEP Request' document: (a) Specific goals for executive function, organization, task completion, behavioral regulation. (b) Accommodations: extended time, preferential seating, movement breaks, chunked assignments, visual schedules, check-ins, modified homework, fidget tools, testing accommodations. (c) Services: behavioral support, counseling, social skills (if applicable). (d) Assistive technology: timers, graphic organizers, text-to-speech, organizational apps.
STEP 2: ADHD eligibility category: 'Other Health Impairment' (OHI) under IDEA — 34 CFR §300.8(c)(9). OHI covers conditions that result in 'limited alertness' affecting educational performance. ADHD directly qualifies.
STEP 3: At IEP meeting: 'My child qualifies under OHI for ADHD. I'm requesting goals in executive function and self-regulation, accommodations for attention and impulsivity, and [specific services].'
STEP 4: Push for SPECIFIC, MEASURABLE goals: not 'improve attention' but 'Given a visual checklist, [child] will independently complete 4 of 5 steps of a classroom routine with no more than 1 verbal prompt, in 4 of 5 trials, as measured by teacher data collection.'
STEP 5: Request a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) if behavioral issues exist — the BIP should use POSITIVE behavioral supports, not punitive consequences.
STEP 6: Consider Extended School Year (ESY) if regression is documented.
STEP 7: If you disagree with the offer, do NOT sign: 'I'd like to revie

CONTACT: IEP TEAM: You, SpEd teacher, gen ed teacher, school psychologist, administrator. Bring advocate if possible.
