insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 6.2: Executive Function Skill Building

ADHD — Step 6.2: Executive Function Skill Building

WHAT TO DO: Executive function deficits are the CORE challenge of ADHD. Key EF skills to build: (1) Working memory — external memory aids (written lists, phone reminders, visual cues). (2) Time management — timers, visual time tools, 'time estimation' practice. (3) Organization — systems that are simple enough to maintain (the '2-minute rule'). (4) Planning/prioritizing — break projects into steps, use planners/apps. (5) Task initiation — 'just do the first step,' body doubling, accountability partners. (6) Emotional regulation — identify triggers, use coping strategies, self-advocacy for breaks. (7) Flexible thinking — practice plan B thinking. (8) Self-monitoring — learn to recognize when attention has drifted. Programs: Unstuck and on Target, Understood.org EF guides, CHADD EF resources.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Understand executive function (EF) as ADHD's core challenge. EF skills develop 3-5 years behind peers. Key EF skills: working memory, flexible thinking, self-monitoring, planning/prioritization, task initiation, organization, time management, emotional regulation.
STEP 2: Teach EXPLICIT EF strategies by age:
• Ages 5-8: Visual checklists, 'first/then' boards, timer use, single-step instructions, color-coded supplies
• Ages 9-12: Planner use (teach HOW to use it, not just give one), assignment tracking system, test preparation checklists, self-monitoring cards ('Am I on track?'), break tasks into sub-tasks
• Ages 13-18: Digital planner (Google Calendar), time estimation practice, project planning templates, self-advocacy skills, study strategies (spaced retrieval, active recall)
STEP 3: Practice EF skills EXTERNALLY first (visual supports, adult scaffolding), then gradually internalize.
STEP 4: Consider an ADHD coach for teens — they specialize in EF skill building. ADHD Coaches Organization: adhdcoaches.org.
STEP 5: Request EF goals in IEP/504 — these are legitimate, measurable goals.
STEP 6: Build in MOVEMENT — physical activity directly improves EF. Exercise before homework, movement breaks during study.

CONTACT: EXECUTIVE FUNCTION RESOURCES:
• Smart but Scattered (book/website): smartbutscatteredkids.com
• ADHD Coaches Organization: adhdcoaches.org — find ADHD coach
• Understood.org: understood.org — EF strategies by age
• ADDitude: additudemag.com — EF articles and webinars
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• CHADD chapters: chadd.org/affiliate-locator — workshops on EF
• School OT: request OT evaluation if fine motor/organizational issues
• School counselor: may provide EF groups
• Local tutoring centers with EF focus (many Kumon/Sylvan locations offer organizational support)
PROGRAMS:
• Cogmed Working Memory Training (computer-based)
• Brain Balance (locations throughout CA)
• Neurofeedback (some evidence, not first-line — discuss with clinician)

DOCUMENTS: EF SKILL BUILDING DOCUMENTS:
• Executive function assessment (BRIEF-2 results from evaluation)
• EF goal tracking sheet (by skill area)
• Visual supports created (checklists, schedules, planners)
• Teacher EF feedback forms
• ADHD coach notes (if applicable)
• IEP/504 EF goals and progress

TRACK PROGRESS:
• Task initiation (how long to start homework?)
• Organization (backpack/desk rating weekly)
• Time management (assignment time estimates vs actual)
• Planning (multi-step project completion)
• Emotional regulation (meltdown frequency/intensity)

TIMELINE: Ongoing — EF skills develop through mid-20s in ADHD (delayed maturation).

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: EF skills taught in isolated context — don't generalize to daily life. School and home not coordinating EF approach. Child resistant to 'more strategies.' Parent doing EF work FOR child instead of coaching. Developmental expectations don't account for ADHD EF delay (30% maturity gap).

RISKS: EF skills taught in isolated context — don't generalize to daily life. School and home not coordinating EF approach. Child resistant to 'more strategies.' Parent doing EF work FOR child instead of coaching. Developmental expectations don't account for ADHD EF delay (30% maturity gap).

