insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 7.1: Screen & Address Anxiety (Co-occurs ~40%)

ADHD — Step 7.1: Screen & Address Anxiety (Co-occurs ~40%)

WHAT TO DO: Anxiety co-occurs with ADHD in approximately 40% of children. Can be: (1) Primary anxiety + ADHD (both independent conditions). (2) Anxiety CAUSED by ADHD experiences (academic failure, social rejection, constant criticism). (3) ADHD symptoms that LOOK like anxiety (restlessness, difficulty concentrating, avoidance). Treatment: anxiety may need to be treated first or alongside ADHD. SSRIs for anxiety can be combined with stimulants for ADHD. CBT for anxiety is first-line behavioral treatment. School accommodations should address BOTH conditions.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Anxiety co-occurs with ADHD in approximately 40% of children.
STEP 2: Can be: (1) Primary anxiety + ADHD (both independent conditions).
STEP 3: (2) Anxiety CAUSED by ADHD experiences (academic failure, social rejection, constant criticism).
STEP 4: (3) ADHD symptoms that LOOK like anxiety (restlessness, difficulty concentrating, avoidance).
STEP 5: Treatment: anxiety may need to be treated first or alongside ADHD.

CONTACT: ANXIETY SCREENING:
• Pediatrician: request anxiety screening (Screen for Child Anxiety Related Disorders — SCARED)
• Child psychologist/psychiatrist: comprehensive anxiety evaluation
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• NAMI CA: namica.org — mental health support, local affiliates
• CHADD: chadd.org — co-occurring conditions resources
• ADDitude: additudemag.com — ADHD + anxiety articles
• Medi-Cal behavioral health: through managed care plan
• County mental health: check your county website for children's services
• School ERMHS: request through IEP (Educationally Related Mental Health Services)
• Crisis support: 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline (call or text 988)

DOCUMENTS: ANXIETY DOCUMENTS:
• Anxiety screening results (SCARED, GAD-7)
• Anxiety evaluation report (if comprehensive eval done)
• Medication management notes (if SSRI prescribed)
• Therapy progress notes
• School-based ERMHS assessment (if requested)
• IEP/504 updated to include anxiety accommodations
• Crisis safety plan (if anxiety is severe)

TIMELINE: Screening at initial ADHD evaluation and ongoing. Treatment concurrent.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Anxiety masked by ADHD diagnosis — provider treats only ADHD. Stimulant medication worsens anxiety in some children. Anxiety causes school avoidance that is attributed to ADHD avoidance. CBT for anxiety conflicts with ADHD behavioral strategies. Child on multiple medications — complex interactions.

RISKS: ★ Anxiety masked by ADHD diagnosis — provider treats only ADHD. Stimulant medication worsens anxiety in some children. Anxiety causes school avoidance that is attributed to ADHD avoidance. CBT for anxiety conflicts with ADHD behavioral strategies. Child on multiple medications — complex interactions.

