insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 7.2: Screen & Address Learning Disabilities (Co-occurs ~30-50%)

ADHD — Step 7.2: Screen & Address Learning Disabilities (Co-occurs ~30-50%)

WHAT TO DO: Learning disabilities co-occur with ADHD in 30-50% of children. Most common: dyslexia, dyscalculia, dysgraphia, processing disorders. CRITICAL: ADHD medication improves attention but does NOT remediate LD — both must be addressed separately. Child may have IEP for LD and accommodate for ADHD, or IEP covering both. Evaluation should test academic skills AT CURRENT GRADE LEVEL. Watch for: child whose reading/math/writing doesn't improve with ADHD treatment alone, significant discrepancy between ability and achievement, specific academic areas of persistent difficulty.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Learning disabilities co-occur with ADHD in 30-50% of children.
STEP 2: Most common: dyslexia, dyscalculia, dysgraphia, processing disorders.
STEP 3: CRITICAL: ADHD medication improves attention but does NOT remediate LD — both must be addressed separately.
STEP 4: Child may have IEP for LD and accommodate for ADHD, or IEP covering both.
STEP 5: Evaluation should test academic skills AT CURRENT GRADE LEVEL.

CONTACT: LD SCREENING:
• School psychologist: request academic achievement testing if not already done
• Private neuropsychologist: most comprehensive LD evaluation
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• LDA of CA: ldaca.org — learning disability resources
• IDA (International Dyslexia Association): dyslexiaida.org — dyslexia screening
• Understood.org: understood.org — LD screening tools
• REFER TO SLD JOURNEY MAP IN THIS WORKBOOK for complete navigation guidance
KEY STAT: Up to 50% of children with ADHD also have a co-occurring learning disability. If academic struggles persist despite ADHD treatment, screen for LD.

DOCUMENTS: LD SCREENING DOCUMENTS:
• Academic achievement testing results
• Reading fluency/comprehension assessments
• Math calculation/reasoning assessments
• Written expression samples
• IEP updated with dual eligibility (OHI + SLD) if applicable
• Private LD evaluation (if obtained)
• See SLD Journey Map for complete document guidance

TIMELINE: School: within IEP timeline. Private: 2-8 weeks.

AVOID: DO NOT: CRITICAL: ADHD medication improves attention but does NOT remediate LD — both must be addressed separately

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: LD MISSED: ADHD medication helps attention — everyone assumes academics will improve. They don't. Underlying LD never identified. Child labeled 'not trying hard enough' despite medication. LD evaluation not requested because 'we already know it's ADHD.' School attributes academic difficulties to ADHD alone. Gifted + ADHD + LD (triple exceptional) — all three mask each other.

RISKS: ★ LD MISSED: ADHD medication helps attention — everyone assumes academics will improve. They don't. Underlying LD never identified. Child labeled 'not trying hard enough' despite medication. LD evaluation not requested because 'we already know it's ADHD.' School attributes academic difficulties to ADHD alone. Gifted + ADHD + LD (triple exceptional) — all three mask each other.

