insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 7.3: Screen & Address Behavioral/Conduct Problems (Co-occurs ~50%)

ADHD — Step 7.3: Screen & Address Behavioral/Conduct Problems (Co-occurs ~50%)

WHAT TO DO: Behavioral or conduct problems co-occur with ADHD in approximately 50% of children. Includes: ODD (Oppositional Defiant Disorder) — approximately 40% of ADHD children. Conduct Disorder — approximately 15-20% of ADHD children (more common in adolescence). ADHD + ODD/CD significantly increases risk for: school discipline, substance use, justice system involvement. Key: distinguish ADHD impulsivity from intentional defiance. ADHD impulsivity = acting without thinking. ODD defiance = deliberate opposition. Treatment: behavioral parent training effective for both. Some medications help both ADHD and aggression (guanfacine, risperidone for severe cases).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Behavioral or conduct problems co-occur with ADHD in approximately 50% of children.
STEP 2: Includes: ODD (Oppositional Defiant Disorder) — approximately 40% of ADHD children.
STEP 3: Conduct Disorder — approximately 15-20% of ADHD children (more common in adolescence).
STEP 4: ADHD + ODD/CD significantly increases risk for: school discipline, substance use, justice system involvement.
STEP 5: Key: distinguish ADHD impulsivity from intentional defiance.

CONTACT: BEHAVIORAL SUPPORT:
• Pediatrician/psychiatrist: behavioral assessment
• Child psychologist: behavioral therapy, parent training
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• CHADD: chadd.org — behavioral strategies
• Medi-Cal behavioral health: through managed care plan
• County mental health children's services
• School counselor: school-based behavioral support
• BIP (Behavior Intervention Plan): request through IEP if behavioral issues at school
CRITICAL: Distinguish ADHD impulsivity from conduct disorder. ADHD behavior is not intentionally malicious — it's impulsive. Punitive approaches worsen ADHD behavior.

DOCUMENTS: BEHAVIORAL DOCUMENTS:
• FBA (Functional Behavior Assessment) results
• BIP (Behavior Intervention Plan)
• Behavioral incident reports from school
• Suspension records (track all — MDR required at 10+ days)
• Manifestation Determination Review documentation
• Behavioral therapy progress notes
• Daily behavior charts (home and school)

TIMELINE: Screening at ADHD evaluation and ongoing.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: ADHD impulsivity mislabeled as ODD/defiance. Child disciplined/suspended for ADHD-driven behaviors. Zero tolerance policies disproportionately affect ADHD children. School-to-prison pipeline risk for ADHD + conduct issues. Medication alone insufficient for combined ADHD + ODD.

RISKS: ADHD impulsivity mislabeled as ODD/defiance. Child disciplined/suspended for ADHD-driven behaviors. Zero tolerance policies disproportionately affect ADHD children. School-to-prison pipeline risk for ADHD + conduct issues. Medication alone insufficient for combined ADHD + ODD.

