insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 7.4: Address Emotional Dysregulation

ADHD — Step 7.4: Address Emotional Dysregulation

WHAT TO DO: Emotional dysregulation is increasingly recognized as a CORE feature of ADHD, not just a co-occurring condition. Includes: intense emotional reactions disproportionate to situation, difficulty calming down once upset, low frustration tolerance, rejection sensitivity ('Rejection Sensitive Dysphoria' — not a formal diagnosis but widely discussed), rapid mood shifts, emotional impulsivity (saying hurtful things in the moment). Treatment: emotional regulation skills training, CBT, medication (some children respond to guanfacine or atomoxetine for emotional regulation), dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) skills for adolescents.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Emotional dysregulation is increasingly recognized as a CORE feature of ADHD, not just a co-occurring condition.
STEP 2: Includes: intense emotional reactions disproportionate to situation, difficulty calming down once upset, low frustration tolerance, rejection sensitivity ('Rejection Sensitive Dysphoria' — not a formal diagnosis but widely discussed), rapid mood shifts, emotional impulsivity (saying hurtful things in the moment).
STEP 3: Treatment: emotional regulation skills training, CBT, medication (some children respond to guanfacine or atomoxetine for emotional regulation), dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) skills for adolescents.

CONTACT: EMOTIONAL DYSREGULATION SUPPORT:
• Child psychologist: CBT for emotional regulation
• Occupational therapist: sensory regulation strategies
FOR CA FAMILIES:
• NAMI CA: namica.org
• DBT for adolescents: dialecticalbehaviortherapy.com — skills training
• Medi-Cal behavioral health
• School ERMHS: request through IEP
• Crisis: 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline
KEY: Emotional dysregulation is a CORE feature of ADHD, not a separate condition. It should be addressed in the treatment plan.

DOCUMENTS: EMOTIONAL REGULATION DOCUMENTS:
• Emotional dysregulation assessment
• Therapy progress notes
• Coping strategy list (written, visual)
• School de-escalation plan
• BIP with emotional regulation strategies
• Crisis safety plan (if needed)
• Medication adjustment notes (if dysregulation is medication side effect)

TIMELINE: Ongoing — skill building is long-term.

AVOID: DO NOT: Emotional dysregulation is increasingly recognized as a CORE feature of ADHD, not just a co-occurring condition | Includes: intense emotional reactions disproportionate to situation, difficulty calming down once upset, low frustration tolerance, rejection sensitivity ('Rejection Sensitive Dysphoria' — not a formal diagnosis but widely discussed), rapid mood shifts, emotional impulsivity (saying hurtful things in the moment)

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Emotional dysregulation misdiagnosed as bipolar disorder or mood disorder. Medication changes made for 'mood instability' when issue is ADHD emotional dysregulation. RSD causes social withdrawal and avoidance. Emotional outbursts at school lead to suspension/restraint. Parent doesn't recognize emotional component as part of ADHD.

RISKS: Emotional dysregulation misdiagnosed as bipolar disorder or mood disorder. Medication changes made for 'mood instability' when issue is ADHD emotional dysregulation. RSD causes social withdrawal and avoidance. Emotional outbursts at school lead to suspension/restraint. Parent doesn't recognize emotional component as part of ADHD.

