insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 8.1: Ongoing Medication Management & Monitoring

ADHD — Step 8.1: Ongoing Medication Management & Monitoring

WHAT TO DO: ADHD medication requires ongoing management: (1) Growth monitoring every 3-6 months (height, weight, BMI). (2) Medication efficacy review — is current dose/medication still working? (3) Dosage adjustments as child grows. (4) Annual medication 'holiday' consideration (controversial — AAP does not recommend routine drug holidays). (5) Transition from pediatric to adult prescriber. (6) Medication diversion prevention for stimulants. (7) Manage ongoing shortage issues. (8) Re-evaluation of medication need as child matures. 30.1% of children with current ADHD receive neither medication nor behavioral treatment — significant treatment gap.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: ADHD medication requires ongoing management: (1) Growth monitoring every 3-6 months (height, weight, BMI).
STEP 2: (2) Medication efficacy review — is current dose/medication still working? (3) Dosage adjustments as child grows.
STEP 3: (4) Annual medication 'holiday' consideration (controversial — AAP does not recommend routine drug holidays).
STEP 4: (5) Transition from pediatric to adult prescriber.
STEP 5: (6) Medication diversion prevention for stimulants.

CONTACT: CHADD PROFESSIONAL DIRECTORY: chadd.org — find ADHD specialists
CBT THERAPISTS: psychologytoday.com — filter by ADHD, CBT, children
PARENT TRAINING PROGRAMS: CHADD Parent-to-Parent (free), Triple P, PCIT (ages 2-7)
INSURANCE: Call number on card. ADHD medication + behavioral therapy covered under mental health parity (MHPAEA).
MEDI-CAL: Covers ADHD evaluation, medication, and behavioral therapy.
DMHC: (888) 466-2219 | CDI: (800) 927-4357

DOCUMENTS: MEDICATION MANAGEMENT DOCUMENTS:
• Medication log (ongoing): dose, time, effects, side effects
• Prescriber visit notes (every 1-3 months)
• Height/weight growth chart (annual)
• Teacher feedback forms (each school year start + quarterly)
• Annual medication review summary
• Pharmacy records
• Insurance formulary updates
• Any genetic testing results (pharmacogenomics)

TIMELINE: Every 3-6 months for medication review. Annually for comprehensive re-assessment.

AVOID: DO NOT: (4) Annual medication 'holiday' consideration (controversial — AAP does not recommend routine drug holidays)

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Medication complacency — 'set it and forget it.' Growth suppression not monitored. Medication stops working (tolerance or dose needs adjustment) — parent assumes ADHD is untreatable. Adolescent medication non-compliance. Provider shortage for stimulant prescribers. Pharmacy switches generic manufacturers — different response.

RISKS: Medication complacency — 'set it and forget it.' Growth suppression not monitored. Medication stops working (tolerance or dose needs adjustment) — parent assumes ADHD is untreatable. Adolescent medication non-compliance. Provider shortage for stimulant prescribers. Pharmacy switches generic manufacturers — different response.

