insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 8.3: Transition Planning (Age 14+)

ADHD — Step 8.3: Transition Planning (Age 14+)

WHAT TO DO: By age 14-16, IEP must include transition goals. For ADHD: (1) Self-advocacy — understanding ADHD and communicating needs. (2) Self-management — medication management, using organizational tools independently. (3) College preparation — SAT/ACT accommodations (apply 6+ months early), college disability services registration, 504/IEP documentation transfer. (4) Career exploration — careers matching ADHD strengths (entrepreneurship, creative fields, emergency services, sales). (5) Driving — ADHD increases accident risk 2-4x; medication timing for driving important. (6) DOR services if vocational support needed. (7) Financial literacy — ADHD impulsivity affects spending.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 14-15: begin transition discussions at IEP meetings. Include: self-advocacy goals (understanding ADHD, requesting accommodations), executive function independence goals, medication self-management goals.
STEP 2: By age 15-16: begin SAT/ACT accommodation application. Gather: current evaluation (within 3 years), 504/IEP history, documentation of accommodation use. Apply through College Board SSD (collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities) or ACT (act.org) — submit 6+ months before test.
STEP 3: College preparation: (a) Visit college disability offices during tours — ask about ADHD-specific support. (b) Many colleges have ADHD support programs (coaching, study skills, extended testing). (c) Student must self-identify and register with disability office BEFORE classes begin. IEP does NOT transfer.
STEP 4: Teach medication self-management: by age 16-17, teen should understand their medication, set their own reminders, and communicate with prescriber.
STEP 5: DOR (Dept of Rehabilitation): dor.ca.gov — may provide vocational support for significant ADHD. Refer through IEP by age 16.
STEP 6: Driver's license: ADHD increases accident risk — consider graduated driving, extra practice hours, medication timing for driving.
STEP 7: CalABLE: calable.ca.gov — if ADHD significantly limits functioning.

CONTACT: SAT/ACT ACCOMMODATIONS:
• College Board SSD: collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities — (844) 255-7728
• ACT accommodations: act.org/the-act/accommodations — (319) 337-1332
