insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ADHD Step 8.4: Adult ADHD — Ongoing Support

ADHD — Step 8.4: Adult ADHD — Ongoing Support

WHAT TO DO: ADHD persists into adulthood in 60-75% of cases. Adult needs: (1) Transition to adult prescriber — many pediatric patients lose continuity. (2) Workplace accommodations under ADA. (3) Ongoing medication management. (4) Relationship skills — ADHD affects partnerships. (5) Financial management strategies. (6) Executive function support systems. (7) Mental health monitoring — ADHD adults at higher risk for depression, anxiety, substance use. (8) If co-occurring LD: workplace and continuing education accommodations. 15.5 million U.S. adults had ADHD diagnosis by late 2023 — growing recognition of lifelong nature.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Adult ADHD: diagnosis continues — ~60% of children with ADHD continue to meet criteria as adults. Updated adult evaluation may be needed (use DSM-5 adult criteria).
STEP 2: Medication: continue or adjust as needed. Find an adult psychiatrist or primary care provider experienced with adult ADHD. In CA, psychiatrists are accessed through insurance (private) or county mental health (Medi-Cal).
STEP 3: Workplace accommodations: ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) requires reasonable accommodations — flexible scheduling, written instructions, noise reduction, organizational support. JAN (Job Accommodation Network): askjan.org.
STEP 4: Adult ADHD coaching: ADHD Coaches Organization (adhdcoaches.org) — find a certified coach for career, relationship, and life management.
STEP 5: Relationships: ADHD significantly impacts relationships. Resources: 'The ADHD Effect on Marriage' by Melissa Orlov, couples therapy with ADHD-aware therapist.
STEP 6: Benefits: SSI (if ADHD significantly limits functioning), Medi-Cal, CalABLE. Most adults with ADHD do not qualify for SSI, but severe cases may.
STEP 7: Self-advocacy organizations: CHADD (chadd.org), ADDA (Attention Deficit Disorder Association: add.org).

CONTACT: ADULT ADHD SUPPORT:
• CHADD: chadd.org — adult ADHD resources, local chapters throughout CA
• ADDA (Attention Deficit Disorder Association): add.org — adult-focused
• ADDitude: additudemag.com — adult ADHD articles and webinars
