insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 1.1: Parent Notices Speech or Language Concerns

SLI — Step 1.1: Parent Notices Speech or Language Concerns

WHAT TO DO: Parent observes developmental concerns: SPEECH concerns — child difficult to understand, substitutes sounds, omits sounds, stutters/repeats sounds or words, voice sounds hoarse/nasal. LANGUAGE concerns — late talker (no words by 18 months, no 2-word phrases by 24 months), difficulty following directions, limited vocabulary for age, short/simple sentences, trouble telling stories or answering questions, difficulty with reading/writing in school-age children. ASHA milestones: by 12 mo = 1-2 words; by 24 mo = 50+ words, 2-word combos; by 36 mo = 3-word sentences, understood by familiar adults. Red flags: not babbling by 9 months, no words by 15-18 months, regression in any speech/language skills.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Parent observes developmental concerns: SPEECH concerns — child difficult to understand, substitutes sounds, omits sounds, stutters/repeats sounds or words, voice sounds hoarse/nasal.
STEP 2: LANGUAGE concerns — late talker (no words by 18 months, no 2-word phrases by 24 months), difficulty following directions, limited vocabulary for age, short/simple sentences, trouble telling stories or answering questions, difficulty with reading/writing in school-age children.
STEP 3: ASHA milestones: by 12 mo = 1-2 words; by 24 mo = 50+ words, 2-word combos; by 36 mo = 3-word sentences, understood by familiar adults.
STEP 4: Red flags: not babbling by 9 months, no words by 15-18 months, regression in any speech/language skills.

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN: Request hearing test and SLP referral at well-child visit.
REGIONAL CENTER (ages 0-3): Find yours at dds.ca.gov/rc/listing — 21 Regional Centers statewide. Self-referral accepted, no doctor referral needed.
ASHA PARENT RESOURCES: asha.org/public — 'How Does Your Child Hear and Talk?' milestones, parent handouts in English/Spanish.
FIRST 5 CALIFORNIA: ccfc.ca.gov — local First 5 county commissions fund early childhood development. Find your county at first5association.org.
HELP ME GROW CA: helpmegrowca.org — statewide developmental screening and referral, call 211.
CA FAMILY RESOURCE CENTERS (FRCNCA): frcnca.org — peer support and information/referral in your area.

DOCUMENTS: BEGIN COLLECTING:
• Dated observations journal: 'Oct 5: Child said 'wabbit' for 'rabbit,' drops final consonants, 3-word sentences at age 3 (expect 4-5 words)'
• Video recordings of child's speech in natural settings (play, mealtime, with peers)
• ASHA milestone checklist: asha.org/public/speech/development — completed and dated
• CDC Milestone Tracker app results
• Well-child visit notes mentioning speech/language
• Daycare/preschool teacher observations (request in writing)

DO NOT NEED YET:
• No medical referral needed for RC self-referral
• No documentation needed to call RC intake

TIMELINE: Ongoing — earlier is better. 'Wait and see' advice delays intervention during critical period. Late talkers: some catch up, but persistent delay by age 3 = higher risk for DLD.

AVOID: DO NOT: Red flags: not babbling by 9 months, no words by 15-18 months, regression in any speech/language skills

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: CRITICAL DELAY: Pediatrician says 'wait and see, they'll catch up' — delays critical early intervention window. Boys are late talkers more often AND more likely to be referred, while girls' language issues are overlooked. Bilingual children's normal language development misidentified as disorder, OR true disorder attributed to bilingualism. Family members normalize delay ('dad didn't talk until 3'). Hearing loss not ruled out as cause.

RISKS: ★ CRITICAL DELAY: Pediatrician says 'wait and see, they'll catch up' — delays critical early intervention window. Boys are late talkers more often AND more likely to be referred, while girls' language issues are overlooked. Bilingual children's normal language development misidentified as disorder, OR true disorder attributed to bilingualism. Family members normalize delay ('dad didn't talk until 3'). Hearing loss not ruled out as cause.

