insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 2.1: Early Start Referral & IFSP (Ages 0-3)

SLI — Step 2.1: Early Start Referral & IFSP (Ages 0-3)

WHAT TO DO: California's Early Start program (IDEA Part C) provides early intervention for infants/toddlers with developmental delays. Self-referral accepted — no doctor referral required. Regional Center coordinates evaluation and services. IFSP (Individualized Family Service Plan) developed — family-centered, addresses child AND family needs. Speech-language services may include: direct SLP therapy, parent coaching (teaching parents strategies for daily routines), and assistive technology. Hanen Centre 'It Takes Two to Talk' program widely used — teaches parents to be their child's primary language facilitator. Services delivered in 'natural environments' (home, daycare).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: California's Early Start program (IDEA Part C) provides early intervention for infants/toddlers with developmental delays.
STEP 2: Self-referral accepted — no doctor referral required.
STEP 3: Regional Center coordinates evaluation and services.
STEP 4: IFSP (Individualized Family Service Plan) developed — family-centered, addresses child AND family needs.
STEP 5: Speech-language services may include: direct SLP therapy, parent coaching (teaching parents strategies for daily routines), and assistive technology.

CONTACT: YOUR LOCAL REGIONAL CENTER: dds.ca.gov/rc/listing — 21 RCs statewide. Call intake line, say: 'I'd like to refer my child for Early Start services for speech/language concerns.'
EARLY START PROGRAM: CA DDS administers Early Start (IDEA Part C). Info: dds.ca.gov/services/early-start
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS (FEC): CA Education Code requires FECs in each SELPA — free advocacy and parent training. Find yours: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — Family Resource Centers provide peer support during Early Start process.
DRC (Disability Rights California): (800) 776-5746 — if RC delays intake beyond 45 days or denies services.
OCRA (Office of Clients' Rights Advocacy): (800) 390-7032 — free advocacy for RC disputes.

DOCUMENTS: TO BRING TO RC INTAKE:
• Child's birth certificate (copy)
• Proof of CA residency (utility bill, lease)
• Medi-Cal card or insurance card
• Hearing test results
• Pediatrician referral letter (if available, NOT required)
• Your observations journal and videos

RC WILL PROVIDE:
• Referral acknowledgment
• Rights booklet (Lanterman Act rights)
• Consent for evaluation

AFTER EVALUATION:
• Multidisciplinary evaluation report
• IFSP document (service plan)
• Service authorization(s)
• Service coordinator contact info
• Annual IFSP review schedule

TIMELINE: 45 days from referral to IFSP and start of services. Services continue until age 3.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: EARLY START GAPS: Regional Center evaluates but may use narrow eligibility criteria — child must show 33% delay in one area or 25% in two areas. 'Mild' delay may not qualify despite parent concern. Service frequency may be insufficient (1x/month instead of weekly). Transition from Early Start to school district at age 3 is a critical vulnerability — services can stop abruptly. SLP shortage means waitlists even within Early Start.

RISKS: ★ EARLY START GAPS: Regional Center evaluates but may use narrow eligibility criteria — child must show 33% delay in one area or 25% in two areas. 'Mild' delay may not qualify despite parent concern. Service frequency may be insufficient (1x/month instead of weekly). Transition from Early Start to school district at age 3 is a critical vulnerability — services can stop abruptly. SLP shortage means waitlists even within Early Start.

