SLI — Step 2.2: Transition from Early Start to School District (Age 3)

WHAT TO DO: At age 3, children transition from Early Start (Part C) to school district (Part B). This is one of the MOST CRITICAL transitions — services can change dramatically. School district must evaluate and develop IEP by child's 3rd birthday. Parent should: (1) Start transition planning at 2.5 years. (2) Attend transition meeting with RC and school district. (3) Request comprehensive speech-language evaluation. (4) Understand that service delivery model changes: from home-based/natural environment to school-based/pull-out or push-in. IFSP goals ≠ IEP goals — IEP must address 'educational performance.'

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 3, children transition from Early Start (Part C) to school district (Part B).
STEP 2: This is one of the MOST CRITICAL transitions — services can change dramatically.
STEP 3: School district must evaluate and develop IEP by child's 3rd birthday.
STEP 4: Parent should: (1) Start transition planning at 2.
STEP 5: (2) Attend transition meeting with RC and school district.

CONTACT: YOUR SCHOOL DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION: Contact the SpEd department 6 months before child turns 3. Find your district: cde.ca.gov/ds/si/ds/redist.asp
YOUR REGIONAL CENTER SERVICE COORDINATOR: Must facilitate transition meeting with school district per Early Start requirements.
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS (FEC): cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — free transition support and IEP advocacy.
CA SELPA ADMINISTRATORS: your SELPA manages special education for your region. Find yours: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/as/caselpas.asp
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — if transition is delayed or services reduced at age 3.
TASK (Team of Advocates for Special Kids): taskca.org — (714) 533-8275 — free parent training statewide.

DOCUMENTS: TRANSITION DOCUMENTS:
• Transition meeting invitation (RC must initiate 6+ months before age 3)
• Transition plan document
• All Early Start/IFSP records (request complete copy from RC)
• All evaluation reports from RC services
• Progress reports from SLP and other Early Start providers

SCHOOL DISTRICT WILL REQUIRE:
• Written parental consent for evaluation
• Assessment plan (district provides within 15 days of request)
• Your signed consent on assessment plan (starts 60-day clock)

PARENT CREATES:
• Written evaluation request letter to school district
• Updated Parent Concerns Letter describing current abilities and needs
• 'What Works' document: strategies that help your child communicate

TIMELINE: Transition conference: at least 90 days before 3rd birthday. IEP in place by 3rd birthday.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: TRANSITION CLIFF: Services stop at age 3 if school district doesn't complete evaluation on time. School district provides less therapy than Early Start (30 min/week vs weekly home visits). Service model change (home → school) disrupts child's progress. Child doesn't qualify for IEP because educational impact not yet documented (they haven't been in school yet). 'Speech-only' IEP limits access to broader special education supports.

RISKS: ★ TRANSITION CLIFF: Services stop at age 3 if school district doesn't complete evaluation on time. School district provides less therapy than Early Start (30 min/week vs weekly home visits). Service model change (home → school) disrupts child's progress. Child doesn't qualify for IEP because educational impact not yet documented (they haven't been in school yet). 'Speech-only' IEP limits access to broader special education supports.

