SLI — Step 3.1: School District Speech-Language Evaluation

WHAT TO DO: Parent requests evaluation (written request). Under IDEA, SLI is defined as 'a communication disorder, such as stuttering, impaired articulation, a language impairment, or a voice impairment, that adversely affects a child's educational performance' (34 CFR §300.8(c)(11)). Evaluation by school SLP should include: (1) Parent interview and history. (2) Hearing screening (REQUIRED). (3) Oral mechanism exam. (4) Standardized speech/language tests (articulation, receptive language, expressive language). (5) Language sample analysis. (6) Classroom observation. (7) Teacher input on educational impact. (8) Review of academic performance. SLI covers FOUR domains: articulation/speech sounds, language (receptive + expressive), fluency (stuttering), and voice.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Parent requests evaluation (written request).
STEP 2: Under IDEA, SLI is defined as 'a communication disorder, such as stuttering, impaired articulation, a language impairment, or a voice impairment, that adversely affects a child's educational performance' (34 CFR §300.
STEP 3: Evaluation by school SLP should include: (1) Parent interview and history.
STEP 4: (2) Hearing screening (REQUIRED).
STEP 5: (3) Oral mechanism exam.

CONTACT: SCHOOL DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION: Submit written evaluation request to SpEd Director. Find your district contact: cde.ca.gov/ds/si/ds/redist.asp
SCHOOL SLP: Will conduct the speech-language evaluation. Ask for their name and direct contact after submitting request.
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — help with evaluation request letters.
CDE COMPLAINT (if school delays): cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — file if 15-day assessment plan timeline missed.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — evaluation rights guidance.
ASHA: asha.org/public — parent guides on speech-language evaluations.

DOCUMENTS: PARENT CREATES:
• Written evaluation request letter (CRITICAL — hand-deliver AND email, keep date-stamped copy)
• Parent Input Form (detailed, with specific examples of communication at home)
• Updated concerns journal with recent examples
• Video recordings of communication in natural settings

SCHOOL DISTRICT MUST PROVIDE:
• Assessment plan within 15 calendar days of request
• Signed assessment plan triggers 60-day evaluation clock
• Evaluation report including: standardized speech-language testing, language sampling, classroom observation, teacher input
• Eligibility determination document
• Parent rights notification (Procedural Safeguards)

KEEP ALL:
• Your original request letter with date stamp
• Email confirmations
• All evaluation reports
• Calendar with 15-day and 60-day deadline

TIMELINE: 15 days: district presents assessment plan. 60 days from consent: evaluation completed.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: EVALUATION RISKS: Hearing not screened — hearing loss causes speech/language delays that are treatable but misidentified as SLI. Standardized tests biased against bilingual/bidialectal children — language DIFFERENCE misidentified as DISORDER. Cognitive referencing used (comparing IQ to language scores) — ASHA does NOT support this practice. Only expressive language tested — receptive language deficits missed. Language sample not collected — standardized tests alone miss real-world communication difficulties. Educational impact narrowly defined as academic grades only.

RISKS: ★ EVALUATION RISKS: Hearing not screened — hearing loss causes speech/language delays that are treatable but misidentified as SLI. Standardized tests biased against bilingual/bidialectal children — language DIFFERENCE misidentified as DISORDER. Cognitive referencing used (comparing IQ to language scores) — ASHA does NOT support this practice. Only expressive language tested — receptive language deficits missed. Language sample not collected — standardized tests alone miss real-world communication difficulties. Educational impact narrowly defined as academic grades only.

