SLI — Step 3.2: Private SLP Evaluation (Optional/Supplemental)

WHAT TO DO: Private SLP evaluation may be pursued: (1) If school evaluation is delayed or incomplete. (2) For second opinion. (3) For more comprehensive assessment than school provides. (4) If parent suspects school will not qualify child. Private SLPs can diagnose speech/language disorders clinically — but clinical diagnosis ≠ school eligibility. IMPORTANT: a child can have a clinical speech/language diagnosis but NOT qualify for school services if educational impact isn't demonstrated. Conversely, school can identify SLI without clinical diagnosis. Insurance may cover private SLP evaluation and therapy (check plan). Medi-Cal covers speech therapy.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Private SLP evaluation may be pursued: (1) If school evaluation is delayed or incomplete.
STEP 2: (2) For second opinion.
STEP 3: (3) For more comprehensive assessment than school provides.
STEP 4: (4) If parent suspects school will not qualify child.
STEP 5: Private SLPs can diagnose speech/language disorders clinically — but clinical diagnosis ≠ school eligibility.

CONTACT: ASHA PROFIND: asha.org/profind — find certified SLPs by ZIP code, specialty (articulation, language, stuttering, voice), and language spoken.
INSURANCE: Call number on back of card. Ask: 'Does my plan cover speech-language evaluation for my child? Do I need a referral? What is my copay/coinsurance?'
UNIVERSITY SPEECH CLINICS (reduced cost):
• UC Davis Speech-Language-Hearing Center: (530) 752-0583
• San Jose State Univ Communicative Disorders: (408) 924-3688
• SDSU Speech-Language Clinic: (619) 594-7746
• CSU Sacramento: (916) 278-6601
• Cal State LA: (323) 343-4690
IEE RIGHTS: If you disagree with school eval, request IEE at district expense — DRC: (800) 776-5746.

DOCUMENTS: INSURANCE DOCUMENTS:
• Physician referral letter (some plans require)
• Insurance pre-authorization (if required)
• EOB (Explanation of Benefits) after evaluation

PRIVATE SLP PROVIDES:
• Comprehensive evaluation report with standardized scores
• Diagnosis with type and severity
• Detailed recommendations for treatment and school accommodations
• Prognosis statement

IEE DOCUMENTS (if requesting at district expense):
• Written IEE request letter citing 34 CFR §300.502
• District response (agreement or due process filing)
• IEE evaluation report

SHARE with school: Private evaluation supports IEP — school MUST consider it.

TIMELINE: Private evaluation: 1-4 weeks. Insurance auth: 5-15 business days.

AVOID: DO NOT: (4) If parent suspects school will not qualify child | IMPORTANT: a child can have a clinical speech/language diagnosis but NOT qualify for school services if educational impact isn't demonstrated

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Private evaluation contradicts school evaluation — confusing for parents. Insurance limits evaluation to brief screening, not comprehensive assessment. Private SLP diagnoses disorder but school says 'no educational impact.' Medi-Cal reimbursement rates low — fewer SLPs accept it. SLP shortage — waitlists for private evaluation, especially in rural areas.

RISKS: Private evaluation contradicts school evaluation — confusing for parents. Insurance limits evaluation to brief screening, not comprehensive assessment. Private SLP diagnoses disorder but school says 'no educational impact.' Medi-Cal reimbursement rates low — fewer SLPs accept it. SLP shortage — waitlists for private evaluation, especially in rural areas.

