insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 3.3: Understand Diagnosis: Speech Sound Disorder vs DLD vs Stuttering v

SLI — Step 3.3: Understand Diagnosis: Speech Sound Disorder vs DLD vs Stuttering vs Voice

WHAT TO DO: SLI encompasses four distinct types, each with different prognosis and treatment: (1) SPEECH SOUND DISORDER (articulation/phonological) — difficulty producing sounds correctly. Most common type. ~8-9% of young children. Many resolve by school age with therapy. (2) DEVELOPMENTAL LANGUAGE DISORDER (DLD) — persistent difficulty understanding and/or using spoken language. 1 in 14 children (~7%). Does NOT resolve on its own. Impacts reading, writing, social skills, academic performance long-term. Previously called 'Specific Language Impairment.' (3) STUTTERING (fluency disorder) — affects ~1% of children. 75% recover naturally, 25% persist. (4) VOICE DISORDER — hoarseness, breathiness, abnormal pitch. Less common in children. Rarest SLI type.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: SLI encompasses four distinct types, each with different prognosis and treatment: (1) SPEECH SOUND DISORDER (articulation/phonological) — difficulty producing sounds correctly.
STEP 2: ~8-9% of young children.
STEP 3: Many resolve by school age with therapy.
STEP 4: (2) DEVELOPMENTAL LANGUAGE DISORDER (DLD) — persistent difficulty understanding and/or using spoken language.
STEP 5: 1 in 14 children (~7%).

CONTACT: YOUR CHILD'S SLP: Primary resource for explaining diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment plan.
ASHA: asha.org/public — diagnosis-specific parent handouts:
• Speech Sound Disorders: asha.org/public/speech/disorders/speech-sound-disorders
• Language Disorders: asha.org/public/speech/disorders/language-disorders-in-children
• Stuttering: asha.org/public/speech/disorders/stuttering
• Voice Disorders: asha.org/public/speech/disorders/voice-disorders
RAISING AWARENESS OF DLD: radld.org — Developmental Language Disorder resources, awareness campaign.
NATIONAL STUTTERING ASSOCIATION: westutter.org — (800) 937-8888 — peer support for stuttering.
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — emotional support and peer connections after diagnosis.

DOCUMENTS: EVALUATION REPORTS SHOULD INCLUDE:
• Specific diagnosis TYPE: Speech Sound Disorder (articulation vs phonological), Developmental Language Disorder (receptive and/or expressive), Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder (stuttering), Voice Disorder
• Severity rating (mild/moderate/severe/profound)
• Standardized test scores with percentiles
• Functional impact statement (how it affects daily communication and academics)
• Prognosis (expected trajectory with/without treatment)
• Treatment recommendations

PARENT SHOULD HAVE:
• Your copy of all evaluation reports
• Written explanation of diagnosis from SLP
• Questions list for follow-up: 'What is the prognosis? How long will therapy be needed? What type of therapy is evidence-based for this diagnosis?'

TIMELINE: At evaluation completion.

AVOID: DO NOT: Does NOT resolve on its own

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: DIAGNOSIS CONFUSION: Parents told child has 'speech delay' without distinguishing type — treatment differs dramatically for articulation vs DLD. DLD underdiagnosed — it's 7x more common than autism but gets far less research attention and public awareness. Stuttering assessed too early (normal disfluency in 2-5 year olds) or too late (early intervention most effective). Voice disorder not evaluated — hoarseness normalized. DLD misattributed to 'laziness,' ADHD, or behavior problems.

RISKS: ★ DIAGNOSIS CONFUSION: Parents told child has 'speech delay' without distinguishing type — treatment differs dramatically for articulation vs DLD. DLD underdiagnosed — it's 7x more common than autism but gets far less research attention and public awareness. Stuttering assessed too early (normal disfluency in 2-5 year olds) or too late (early intervention most effective). Voice disorder not evaluated — hoarseness normalized. DLD misattributed to 'laziness,' ADHD, or behavior problems.

