insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 4.1: IEP Development — SLI-Specific Services & Goals

SLI — Step 4.1: IEP Development — SLI-Specific Services & Goals

WHAT TO DO: SLI is UNIQUE under IDEA: speech-language services can be BOTH special education AND a related service. 'Speech-only' IEP: child's sole disability is SLI, and speech-language is the primary service. IEP SERVICES may include: (1) Direct SLP therapy (individual or small group). (2) Classroom-based/push-in services. (3) Consultation model (SLP advises teacher). (4) AAC (Augmentative and Alternative Communication) devices/training if needed. IEP GOALS should be: specific, measurable, tied to educational impact (not just 'improve articulation' but 'produce /r/ sound in 80% of opportunities in classroom reading'). Common goal areas: articulation accuracy, sentence length/complexity, vocabulary, comprehension of directions, narrative skills, social communication, fluency management.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: SLI is UNIQUE under IDEA: speech-language services can be BOTH special education AND a related service.
STEP 2: 'Speech-only' IEP: child's sole disability is SLI, and speech-language is the primary service.
STEP 3: IEP SERVICES may include: (1) Direct SLP therapy (individual or small group).
STEP 4: (2) Classroom-based/push-in services.
STEP 5: (3) Consultation model (SLP advises teacher).

CONTACT: IEP TEAM: You, school SLP, gen ed teacher, SpEd teacher (if applicable), administrator.
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — free IEP advocacy and meeting support.
TASK: taskca.org — (714) 533-8275 — parent training on IEP development.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — IEP rights guidance.
DRC SERR MANUAL: serr.disabilityrightsca.org — comprehensive special education rights reference.
ASHA SCHOOL-BASED SERVICES: asha.org/public/speech/development — understand what school SLP should provide.
CA SELPA ADMINISTRATORS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/as/caselpas.asp — your local SELPA for policy questions.

DOCUMENTS: IEP DOCUMENT INCLUDES:
• PLAAFP (Present Levels) with current speech-language functioning
• Annual goals: measurable, time-bound, specific to diagnosis type
• Service page: SLP minutes/week, individual vs group, push-in vs pull-out, provider qualifications
• Accommodations: preferential seating, visual supports, reduced verbal demands, extended response time
• AT consideration (AAC if applicable)
• ESY determination (if regression risk)
• Progress reporting schedule (quarterly)

PARENT BRINGS:
• All evaluation reports (school and private)
• Parent Concerns Letter (updated)
• Your goal priorities document
• Research on evidence-based SLI interventions
• Private SLP input/report if applicable
• Work samples showing academic impact of SLI

TIMELINE: 30 days from completed assessment to IEP meeting.

AVOID: DO NOT: IEP GOALS should be: specific, measurable, tied to educational impact (not just 'improve articulation' but 'produce /r/ sound in 80% of opportunities in classroom reading')

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: IEP PITFALLS: Therapy frequency insufficient (20-30 min 1x/week for severe disorder). Group therapy used when individual is needed. 'Articulation only' goals when child also has language disorder. Goals not connected to curriculum/classroom performance. Consultation-only model when direct service is needed. School SLP has excessive caseload (national average 48 students, many have 60+) — reduces service quality. 'Speech-only' IEP limits access to other supports child may need.

RISKS: ★ IEP PITFALLS: Therapy frequency insufficient (20-30 min 1x/week for severe disorder). Group therapy used when individual is needed. 'Articulation only' goals when child also has language disorder. Goals not connected to curriculum/classroom performance. Consultation-only model when direct service is needed. School SLP has excessive caseload (national average 48 students, many have 60+) — reduces service quality. 'Speech-only' IEP limits access to other supports child may need.

