insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 4.2: Advocate for Appropriate Service Delivery Model

SLI — Step 4.2: Advocate for Appropriate Service Delivery Model

WHAT TO DO: Service delivery models for SLI: (1) PULL-OUT: child removed from classroom for individual/small group therapy. Most traditional. (2) PUSH-IN/CLASSROOM-BASED: SLP works with child within classroom context. Better for generalization. (3) CONSULTATION: SLP trains teacher, who implements strategies. Least direct. (4) COMBINED: mix of models based on goals. Best practice is often a combination. For DLD specifically: classroom-based services help language skills transfer to academic contexts. For articulation: pull-out may be efficient for drill-based practice. Research shows collaboration between SLP and teacher produces better outcomes than isolated pull-out therapy.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Service delivery models for SLI: (1) PULL-OUT: child removed from classroom for individual/small group therapy.
STEP 2: (2) PUSH-IN/CLASSROOM-BASED: SLP works with child within classroom context.
STEP 3: Better for generalization.
STEP 4: (3) CONSULTATION: SLP trains teacher, who implements strategies.
STEP 5: (4) COMBINED: mix of models based on goals.

CONTACT: SCHOOL SLP: Discuss service model options — pull-out, push-in, consultation, collaborative.
SCHOOL PRINCIPAL: If frequency/model is insufficient, escalate to administration.
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — advocacy support.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — if services are inadequate.
ASHA SERVICE DELIVERY: asha.org/practice/service-delivery — evidence-based service models.
CDE COMPLAINT: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — file if IEP services not delivered as written.

DOCUMENTS: IEP SERVICE PAGE MUST SPECIFY:
• Service model: pull-out, push-in, consultation, or combination
• Frequency: minutes per week (not 'as needed')
• Duration: session length
• Group size: individual, small group (specify max), classroom-based
• Location: speech room, classroom, other
• Provider: school SLP (qualifications)

PARENT TRACKS:
• Service delivery log — are sessions actually happening?
• Cancellation tracking (if SLP is absent, are sessions made up?)
• Progress toward goals by model type
• Your child's preference and comfort with model

TIMELINE: Established at IEP meeting. Reviewed annually.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: School defaults to pull-out only (easier to schedule). Consultation model used when direct service is warranted (saves district money). Frequency reduced due to SLP caseload, not child's needs. Group size too large for effective therapy. Service minutes not actually delivered as written.

RISKS: School defaults to pull-out only (easier to schedule). Consultation model used when direct service is warranted (saves district money). Frequency reduced due to SLP caseload, not child's needs. Group size too large for effective therapy. Service minutes not actually delivered as written.

