SLI — Step 4.3: 504 Plan Alternative (If IEP Not Warranted)

WHAT TO DO: Some children with speech/language concerns may not qualify for IEP but need accommodations. 504 Plan appropriate when: mild articulation errors that don't significantly impact academics, stuttering that primarily affects social participation, voice disorder that needs accommodations but not specialized instruction. 504 accommodations may include: extra time for verbal responses, alternative to oral presentations, preferential seating, reduced public speaking pressure for stuttering, classroom amplification system for voice conservation, note-taking support for children with language processing difficulties.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Some children with speech/language concerns may not qualify for IEP but need accommodations.
STEP 2: 504 Plan appropriate when: mild articulation errors that don't significantly impact academics, stuttering that primarily affects social participation, voice disorder that needs accommodations but not specialized instruction.
STEP 3: 504 accommodations may include: extra time for verbal responses, alternative to oral presentations, preferential seating, reduced public speaking pressure for stuttering, classroom amplification system for voice conservation, note-taking support for children with language processing difficulties.

CONTACT: SCHOOL 504 COORDINATOR: Usually the principal or counselor — ask the front office.
OCR (Office for Civil Rights): ed.gov/ocr — file complaint if 504 rights violated. Phone: (800) 421-3481. Regional Office serving CA: San Francisco OCR, (415) 486-5555.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — 504 rights guidance.
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — advocacy for 504 plans.
WRIGHTSLAW: wrightslaw.com/info/sec504.index.htm — 504 plan resources.

DOCUMENTS: 504 DOCUMENTS:
• Documentation of speech/language condition (evaluation report, medical diagnosis)
• Functional impact evidence — how SLI affects classroom performance
• Written 504 Plan with specific accommodations:
  - Preferential seating near teacher
  - Extended time for verbal responses
  - Visual supports for verbal instructions
  - Reduced verbal demands in assessment
  - Permission to use AAC/AT in class
  - Modified oral presentation requirements
• Annual 504 review documentation
• Teacher acknowledgment of accommodations

KEEP FOR FUTURE:
• Complete 504 accommodation history (needed for SAT/ACT/college)
• All medical/evaluation documentation

TIMELINE: No specific federal timeline.

AVOID: DO NOT: Some children with speech/language concerns may not qualify for IEP but need accommodations | 504 Plan appropriate when: mild articulation errors that don't significantly impact academics, stuttering that primarily affects social participation, voice disorder that needs accommodations but not specialized instruction | 504 accommodations may include: extra time for verbal responses, alternative to oral presentations, preferential seating, reduced public speaking pressure for stuttering, classroom amplification system for voice conservation, note-taking support for children with language processing difficulties

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: 504 not offered when IEP denied. Stuttering accommodations not included. Teacher doesn't implement 504 consistently. 504 not reviewed regularly.

RISKS: 504 not offered when IEP denied. Stuttering accommodations not included. Teacher doesn't implement 504 consistently. 504 not reviewed regularly.

