insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 5.1: Private Speech Therapy (Insurance/Medi-Cal)

SLI — Step 5.1: Private Speech Therapy (Insurance/Medi-Cal)

WHAT TO DO: Private speech therapy may supplement or replace school services. Insurance coverage: most plans cover speech therapy as 'habilitative' service (developing skills not previously had) or 'rehabilitative' (restoring lost skills). Medi-Cal: covers speech-language services with physician referral. Common therapy approaches by type: ARTICULATION — drill-based practice, minimal pairs, phonological awareness. DLD — language stimulation, narrative intervention, vocabulary instruction, Hanen strategies. STUTTERING — Lidcombe Program (ages 3-6), fluency shaping, stuttering modification, avoidance reduction therapy. Frequency: typically 1-2x/week, 30-60 min sessions.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Private speech therapy may supplement or replace school services.
STEP 2: Insurance coverage: most plans cover speech therapy as 'habilitative' service (developing skills not previously had) or 'rehabilitative' (restoring lost skills).
STEP 3: Medi-Cal: covers speech-language services with physician referral.
STEP 4: Common therapy approaches by type: ARTICULATION — drill-based practice, minimal pairs, phonological awareness.
STEP 5: DLD — language stimulation, narrative intervention, vocabulary instruction, Hanen strategies.

CONTACT: ASHA PROFIND: asha.org/profind — find SLPs by ZIP, specialty (articulation, language, fluency, voice, AAC), and language spoken.
INSURANCE: Call member services. Ask: 'What are my speech therapy benefits? Visit limit? Copay? Do I need a referral?'
MEDI-CAL: Speech therapy covered under EPSDT (Early and Periodic Screening, Diagnostic and Treatment) for children under 21. No visit limits for medically necessary services. Managed care plan handles authorization.
HANEN CENTRE: hanen.org — 'It Takes Two to Talk' and 'More Than Words' parent programs. Find CA practitioners.
NATIONAL STUTTERING ASSOCIATION: westutter.org — (800) 937-8888 — support groups, SLP referrals for stuttering.
UNIVERSITY SPEECH CLINICS (reduced cost): See Private SLP Evaluation step for list.
REGIONAL CENTER (ages 0-3 or 

DOCUMENTS: INSURANCE DOCUMENTS:
• Physician referral (if required by plan)
• Insurance pre-authorization
• Treatment plan from SLP
• Progress reports from SLP (quarterly)
• EOBs for each session
• Appeal letter if visits are denied

MEDI-CAL DOCUMENTS:
• Medi-Cal card
• Physician referral
• EPSDT request for services (for children under 21, no visit limits for medically necessary services)
• Managed care plan authorization

PARENT TRACKS:
• Session attendance log
• Insurance authorization dates and renewal deadlines
• Visit count vs authorized visits
• Coordination notes between private SLP and school SLP

TIMELINE: Insurance auth: 5-15 business days. Therapy: ongoing, reviewed every 6-12 months.

AVOID: DO NOT: Insurance coverage: most plans cover speech therapy as 'habilitative' service (developing skills not previously had) or 'rehabilitative' (restoring lost skills) | STUTTERING — Lidcombe Program (ages 3-6), fluency shaping, stuttering modification, avoidance reduction therapy

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Insurance caps sessions per year (often 20-30 visits). Prior authorization required for continued therapy. SLP shortage — waitlists common, especially for specialized areas (stuttering, AAC, bilingual SLPs). Medi-Cal reimbursement rates low. Telehealth may be effective but not offered. Private and school SLPs don't coordinate goals.

RISKS: Insurance caps sessions per year (often 20-30 visits). Prior authorization required for continued therapy. SLP shortage — waitlists common, especially for specialized areas (stuttering, AAC, bilingual SLPs). Medi-Cal reimbursement rates low. Telehealth may be effective but not offered. Private and school SLPs don't coordinate goals.

