insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 5.3: AAC (Augmentative and Alternative Communication) — If Needed

SLI — Step 5.3: AAC (Augmentative and Alternative Communication) — If Needed

WHAT TO DO: For children with severe expressive language impairments, AAC provides alternative ways to communicate. AAC ranges from LOW-TECH (picture boards, PECS — Picture Exchange Communication System) to HIGH-TECH (speech-generating devices, tablet apps like Proloquo2Go, TouchChat). KEY MYTH TO DISPEL: AAC does NOT prevent speech development — research consistently shows AAC SUPPORTS spoken language. AAC evaluation should be conducted by SLP with AAC expertise. Funding: school district responsible for AAC as assistive technology under IEP. Insurance/Medi-Cal may cover devices. Regional Center may fund AAC for children with autism/ID.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: For children with severe expressive language impairments, AAC provides alternative ways to communicate.
STEP 2: AAC ranges from LOW-TECH (picture boards, PECS — Picture Exchange Communication System) to HIGH-TECH (speech-generating devices, tablet apps like Proloquo2Go, TouchChat).
STEP 3: KEY MYTH TO DISPEL: AAC does NOT prevent speech development — research consistently shows AAC SUPPORTS spoken language.
STEP 4: AAC evaluation should be conducted by SLP with AAC expertise.
STEP 5: Funding: school district responsible for AAC as assistive technology under IEP.

CONTACT: SLP WITH AAC SPECIALTY: ASHA ProFind filter by 'Augmentative and Alternative Communication.' asha.org/profind
CA ASSISTIVE TECHNOLOGY PROGRAM (ATAP): atnet.org — device demonstrations, loans, and funding assistance.
PRAACTICALAAC.ORG: praacticalaac.org — free AAC implementation resources for families and therapists.
INSURANCE/MEDI-CAL: AAC devices are covered as durable medical equipment (DME). Medi-Cal covers under EPSDT.
SCHOOL DISTRICT AT TEAM: IEP must consider assistive technology including AAC. Request AT evaluation.
REGIONAL CENTER: May fund AAC devices and training for eligible children.
USSAAC: ussaac.org — United States Society for AAC — resources and advocacy.

DOCUMENTS: AAC DOCUMENTS:
• AAC evaluation report by qualified SLP
• AT assessment through school (IEP requirement)
• Device trial documentation (what was tried, results)
• Insurance/Medi-Cal pre-authorization for AAC device (covered as DME)
• IEP AT page specifying AAC device, training, and implementation plan
• AAC vocabulary/programming plan

TRAINING DOCUMENTATION:
• Staff training plan (all teachers/aides who work with child)
• Parent training on device operation and modeling
• AAC competency checklist for communication partners

PARENT TRACKS:
• Device usage log
• Communication growth with AAC
• Device maintenance and updates needed
• Insurance authorization renewals for device

TIMELINE: AAC evaluation: within IEP timeline. Device acquisition: varies (insurance 4-8 weeks, school district varies).

AVOID: DO NOT: KEY MYTH TO DISPEL: AAC does NOT prevent speech development — research consistently shows AAC SUPPORTS spoken language

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: AAC BARRIERS: School claims child must 'try to talk first' before getting AAC — WRONG, no prerequisite skills required. Device funding denied by insurance. School provides low-tech when high-tech is needed. Staff not trained to implement AAC across settings. AAC device stays at school — child can't communicate at home. Only one communication app evaluated instead of trial of multiple options.

RISKS: ★ AAC BARRIERS: School claims child must 'try to talk first' before getting AAC — WRONG, no prerequisite skills required. Device funding denied by insurance. School provides low-tech when high-tech is needed. Staff not trained to implement AAC across settings. AAC device stays at school — child can't communicate at home. Only one communication app evaluated instead of trial of multiple options.

