insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 6.1: Screen for Reading/Literacy Impact (DLD → SLD Risk)

SLI — Step 6.1: Screen for Reading/Literacy Impact (DLD → SLD Risk)

WHAT TO DO: DLD is a MAJOR risk factor for reading and learning disabilities. The oral language → written language connection: children who struggle to understand and use spoken language are at significantly higher risk for dyslexia, reading comprehension difficulties, and written expression problems. By school age, children with DLD often qualify under BOTH 'Speech-Language Impairment' AND 'Specific Learning Disability' categories. Important: as children age, SLI identification may shift to SLD — child 'exits' speech services but underlying language disorder continues to affect reading/writing. The language disorder doesn't go away — it shows up differently in academic demands.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: DLD is a MAJOR risk factor for reading and learning disabilities.
STEP 2: The oral language → written language connection: children who struggle to understand and use spoken language are at significantly higher risk for dyslexia, reading comprehension difficulties, and written expression problems.
STEP 3: By school age, children with DLD often qualify under BOTH 'Speech-Language Impairment' AND 'Specific Learning Disability' categories.
STEP 4: Important: as children age, SLI identification may shift to SLD — child 'exits' speech services but underlying language disorder continues to affect reading/writing.
STEP 5: The language disorder doesn't go away — it shows up differently in academic demands.

CONTACT: SCHOOL READING SPECIALIST: Request literacy assessment if DLD is diagnosed.
SCHOOL DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION: Request evaluation for SLD if reading concerns emerge. See SLD Journey Map.
CLASSROOM TEACHER: Ask about reading benchmark data (DIBELS, iReady, STAR).
IDA (International Dyslexia Association): dyslexiaida.org — if dyslexia suspected.
DECODING DYSLEXIA CA: decodingdyslexiaca.org — CA-specific dyslexia advocacy.
UNDERSTOOD: understood.org — LD screening and resources.
CROSS-REFERENCE: SLD Journey Map in this workbook for full reading disability pathway.

DOCUMENTS: LITERACY SCREENING DOCUMENTS:
• Reading benchmark data from school (DIBELS, iReady, STAR, SBAC)
• Reading fluency rates vs grade-level expectations
• Written language samples
• Teacher input on academic performance

IF SLD SUSPECTED:
• Written evaluation request for SLD (separate from SLI evaluation)
• Cross-reference: SLD Journey Map Step 1.3 for full evaluation request guidance
• Research showing DLD → SLD connection: ~50% of children with DLD develop reading difficulties

IEP UPDATES:
• Dual-eligibility documentation (SLI + SLD) if both qualify
• Updated goals addressing both oral and written language

TIMELINE: Screen at kindergarten entry and ongoing. Formal LD evaluation if concerns persist.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: CRITICAL RISK: Child exits speech therapy because articulation improved, but underlying language disorder (DLD) continues to impact reading and writing — now UNFUNDED. School doesn't connect prior SLI to current reading difficulties. Child re-evaluated years later for LD when language was the issue all along. 'Speech-only' IEP didn't address literacy connections. DLD goes 'underground' as child ages — shows up as poor reading comprehension, weak writing, difficulty with abstract language.

RISKS: ★ CRITICAL RISK: Child exits speech therapy because articulation improved, but underlying language disorder (DLD) continues to impact reading and writing — now UNFUNDED. School doesn't connect prior SLI to current reading difficulties. Child re-evaluated years later for LD when language was the issue all along. 'Speech-only' IEP didn't address literacy connections. DLD goes 'underground' as child ages — shows up as poor reading comprehension, weak writing, difficulty with abstract language.

