insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 6.2: Screen for Social-Emotional Impact

SLI — Step 6.2: Screen for Social-Emotional Impact

WHAT TO DO: Children with SLI frequently experience social-emotional difficulties: (1) Social isolation — difficulty making friends due to communication barriers. (2) Behavioral issues — frustration from inability to express needs leads to acting out. (3) Bullying — children with speech differences are targets. (4) Anxiety — fear of speaking, avoidance of social situations. (5) Low self-esteem — awareness of communication difficulties. (6) Juvenile justice risk — 60-64% of youth in juvenile justice system have language disorders (often undiagnosed). CRITICAL: language difficulties are often misinterpreted as behavior problems, defiance, or low intelligence.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Children with SLI frequently experience social-emotional difficulties: (1) Social isolation — difficulty making friends due to communication barriers.
STEP 2: (2) Behavioral issues — frustration from inability to express needs leads to acting out.
STEP 3: (3) Bullying — children with speech differences are targets.
STEP 4: (4) Anxiety — fear of speaking, avoidance of social situations.
STEP 5: (5) Low self-esteem — awareness of communication difficulties.

CONTACT: SCHOOL COUNSELOR: Request social-emotional screening and support.
PEDIATRICIAN: Discuss social-emotional concerns at well-child visit.
ERMHS (Educationally Related Mental Health Services): Request through IEP for school-based counseling.
COUNTY BEHAVIORAL HEALTH: Each CA county has a children's behavioral health program. Access through Medi-Cal managed care plan or call county directly.
NAMI CALIFORNIA: namica.org — family support groups, education programs.
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — peer support for families navigating emotional challenges.

DOCUMENTS: SOCIAL-EMOTIONAL ASSESSMENT:
• Behavioral rating scales (BASC-3, Conners if ADHD concern)
• Social skills assessment
• Parent/teacher input on social functioning
• Counselor observations

ERMHS DOCUMENTS (if requested through IEP):
• Written ERMHS request
• ERMHS assessment report
• Counseling goals added to IEP
• Progress reports from school counselor/psychologist

PARENT TRACKS:
• Social interaction observations
• Peer relationship concerns
• Self-esteem indicators
• Behavioral changes at home

TIMELINE: Screen at evaluation and ongoing.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Behavior problems treated without identifying underlying communication cause. Child punished for 'not listening' when they don't understand. Social skills groups use language above child's level. Bullying not addressed. Anxiety about speaking not recognized. Zero-tolerance discipline applied without communication disorder consideration.

RISKS: Behavior problems treated without identifying underlying communication cause. Child punished for 'not listening' when they don't understand. Social skills groups use language above child's level. Bullying not addressed. Anxiety about speaking not recognized. Zero-tolerance discipline applied without communication disorder consideration.

