insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 6.3: Address Co-occurring Conditions (Autism, ADHD, Hearing Loss)

SLI — Step 6.3: Address Co-occurring Conditions (Autism, ADHD, Hearing Loss)

WHAT TO DO: SLI frequently co-occurs with other conditions: (1) AUTISM — speech-language delay is often first sign; many children initially identified with SLI are later diagnosed with autism. If child has social communication deficits beyond language disorder, screen for autism. (2) ADHD — attention/executive function impacts language processing and therapy participation; 30-50% co-occurrence. (3) HEARING LOSS — even mild/fluctuating hearing loss (chronic ear infections) significantly impacts speech-language development. Hearing must be re-tested if progress is slow. (4) INTELLECTUAL DISABILITY — language disorder associated with ID has different trajectory than DLD. (5) APRAXIA OF SPEECH — motor planning disorder for speech, requires specialized treatment (not standard articulation therapy).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: SLI frequently co-occurs with other conditions: (1) AUTISM — speech-language delay is often first sign; many children initially identified with SLI are later diagnosed with autism.
STEP 2: If child has social communication deficits beyond language disorder, screen for autism.
STEP 3: (2) ADHD — attention/executive function impacts language processing and therapy participation; 30-50% co-occurrence.
STEP 4: (3) HEARING LOSS — even mild/fluctuating hearing loss (chronic ear infections) significantly impacts speech-language development.
STEP 5: Hearing must be re-tested if progress is slow.

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER: If autism suspected — self-refer for evaluation. dds.ca.gov/rc/listing
PEDIATRICIAN: ADHD screening (Vanderbilt/SNAP-IV). Hearing re-evaluation.
AUDIOLOGIST: Annual hearing monitoring recommended for any child with speech concerns.
CHADD: chadd.org — if ADHD is confirmed.
CROSS-REFERENCE JOURNEY MAPS:
• Autism Journey Map — if ASD suspected/diagnosed
• ADHD Journey Map — if attention concerns
• ID Journey Map — if cognitive delays co-occur
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — dual-eligibility IEP advocacy.

DOCUMENTS: COMPREHENSIVE EVALUATION DOCUMENTS:
• Autism screening results (M-CHAT, SCQ, ADOS-2 if pursued)
• ADHD screening (Vanderbilt rating scales from teacher and parent)
• Updated audiological evaluation (annual hearing monitoring recommended)
• Developmental evaluation from Regional Center (if autism suspected)
• Private developmental pediatrician or neuropsychologist evaluation

DUAL-ELIGIBILITY DOCUMENTS:
• IEP eligibility under multiple categories (SLI + Autism, SLI + OHI)
• Updated IEP with goals addressing all diagnosed conditions
• RC IPP (if autism/ID diagnosed — RC services supplement school)
• Coordinated service plan between school, RC, and private providers

TIMELINE: Ongoing — reassess as child develops and new concerns emerge.

AVOID: DO NOT: (5) APRAXIA OF SPEECH — motor planning disorder for speech, requires specialized treatment (not standard articulation therapy)

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Autism identified late because SLI was the initial diagnosis. Hearing re-check not done when progress plateaus. Apraxia misdiagnosed as typical articulation disorder (treatment approach differs significantly). ADHD medication helps attention but doesn't resolve language disorder. Multiple providers not coordinating care.

RISKS: Autism identified late because SLI was the initial diagnosis. Hearing re-check not done when progress plateaus. Apraxia misdiagnosed as typical articulation disorder (treatment approach differs significantly). ADHD medication helps attention but doesn't resolve language disorder. Multiple providers not coordinating care.

