insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 7.1: Ongoing IEP Review — SLI-Specific Monitoring

SLI — Step 7.1: Ongoing IEP Review — SLI-Specific Monitoring

WHAT TO DO: Annual IEP review should assess: (1) Is therapy frequency/model still appropriate? (2) Are goals advancing (not repeating same goals yearly)? (3) Has diagnosis type changed or clarified? (4) Is literacy impact being monitored? (5) Should eligibility category shift from SLI to SLD as academic impacts emerge? (6) Is dismissal from speech services appropriate? ASHA dismissal guidelines: (1) Goals met — communication no longer adversely affects educational performance. (2) Plateau — child has reached maximum benefit from current approach. (3) Progress maintained without direct services. IMPORTANT: dismissal from speech DOESN'T mean disorder is gone — child may need ongoing classroom supports.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Annual IEP review should assess: (1) Is therapy frequency/model still appropriate? (2) Are goals advancing (not repeating same goals yearly)? (3) Has diagnosis type changed or clarified? (4) Is literacy impact being monitored? (5) Should eligibility category shift from SLI to SLD as academic impacts emerge? (6) Is dismissal from speech services appropriate? ASHA dismissal guidelines: (1) Goals met — communication no longer adversely affects educational performance.
STEP 2: (2) Plateau — child has reached maximum benefit from current approach.
STEP 3: (3) Progress maintained without direct services.
STEP 4: IMPORTANT: dismissal from speech DOESN'T mean disorder is gone — child may need ongoing classroom supports.

CONTACT: IEP CASE MANAGER: Your child's school SLP or SpEd teacher.
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — annual IEP review support.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — if services not delivered or goals stagnant.
CDE COMPLAINT: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — file if IEP not implemented.
OAH (Office of Administrative Hearings): (916) 263-0880 — mediation or due process if needed.
TASK: taskca.org — parent training on IEP monitoring and advocacy.

DOCUMENTS: ANNUAL REVIEW DOCUMENTS:
• Quarterly progress reports (file chronologically)
• Annual IEP with updated goals
• Current standardized assessment data
• Language samples showing growth trajectory
• Teacher/SLP input forms
• Service delivery log (actual vs planned sessions)

TRIENNIAL RE-EVALUATION (every 3 years):
• Updated comprehensive speech-language evaluation
• Continued eligibility determination
• DO NOT waive triennial — updated data protects eligibility

PARENT'S MASTER BINDER:
• Section 1: Current IEP
• Section 2: All evaluations
• Section 3: Progress reports
• Section 4: Correspondence with school
• Section 5: Private SLP reports
• Section 6: Medical records

TIMELINE: Annual IEP review. Triennial reassessment every 3 years.

AVOID: DO NOT: Annual IEP review should assess: (1) Is therapy frequency/model still appropriate? (2) Are goals advancing (not repeating same goals yearly)? (3) Has diagnosis type changed or clarified? (4) Is literacy impact being monitored? (5) Should eligibility category shift from SLI to SLD as academic impacts emerge? (6) Is dismissal from speech services appropriate? ASHA dismissal guidelines: (1) Goals met — communication no longer adversely affects educational performance

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Same goals repeated for years without meaningful progress — approach needs changing. Child dismissed prematurely because articulation improved but language disorder persists. Triennial re-evaluation not comprehensive. SLP leaves and replacement doesn't understand child's history. Therapy minu

RISKS: Same goals repeated for years without meaningful progress — approach needs changing. Child dismissed prematurely because articulation improved but language disorder persists. Triennial re-evaluation not comprehensive. SLP leaves and replacement doesn't understand child's history. Therapy minutes reduced due to caseload, not child's needs. Parent not informed about dismissal criteria.

