insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 7.2: Transition Planning (Age 14+) — DLD-Specific

SLI — Step 7.2: Transition Planning (Age 14+) — DLD-Specific

WHAT TO DO: For children with persistent DLD (NOT resolved articulation): (1) Self-advocacy — understanding own communication needs and requesting accommodations. (2) SAT/ACT accommodations for language processing (extended time, reader for instructions). (3) College disability services — language disorder documentation for accommodations. (4) Career exploration — careers that match communication strengths. (5) Workplace communication strategies. (6) DOR referral for vocational support if needed. DLD impacts PERSIST into adulthood: difficulty with complex language, abstract reasoning, reading comprehension, written expression, social communication in professional settings.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: For children with persistent DLD (NOT resolved articulation): (1) Self-advocacy — understanding own communication needs and requesting accommodations.
STEP 2: (2) SAT/ACT accommodations for language processing (extended time, reader for instructions).
STEP 3: (3) College disability services — language disorder documentation for accommodations.
STEP 4: (4) Career exploration — careers that match communication strengths.
STEP 5: (5) Workplace communication strategies.

CONTACT: SCHOOL TRANSITION COORDINATOR: Part of IEP team for transition planning.
CA DOR (Dept of Rehabilitation): dor.ca.gov — vocational rehabilitation if DLD impacts employment. Referral through IEP by age 16.
COLLEGE BOARD SSD: collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities — (844) 255-7728 — SAT accommodations for language disorders.
ACT ACCOMMODATIONS: act.org/the-act/accommodations — (319) 337-1332.
COLLEGE DISABILITY OFFICES: Register BEFORE first semester. IEP does not transfer to college.
CALABLE: calable.ca.gov — if communication disability is significantly limiting.
ASHA: asha.org — adult communication resources.

DOCUMENTS: TRANSITION DOCUMENTS:
• ITP (Individual Transition Plan) in IEP — required by age 16
• Transition assessments (vocational, independent living, communication)
• Post-secondary goals (education, employment, independent living)
• DOR referral documentation (by age 16)

SAT/ACT ACCOMMODATION DOCUMENTS:
• Current evaluation (within 3 years)
• Complete accommodation history (IEP/504)
• Documentation of accommodation USE
• College Board SSD application or ACT application
• Submit 6+ months before test date

COLLEGE PREP PACKET:
• Most recent evaluation with diagnosis
• All IEP/504 plans showing accommodation history
• Summary of Performance (SOP) from school at graduation
• Self-advocacy plan

TIMELINE: Begin by age 14 (recommended), required by 16.

AVOID: DO NOT: For children with persistent DLD (NOT resolved articulation): (1) Self-advocacy — understanding own communication needs and requesting accommodations

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Child dismissed from speech services in elementary/middle school — transition plan doesn't address communication needs. College disability services unfamiliar with DLD. Workplace communication difficulties not recognized as disability-related. Self-advocacy not taught because child appeared to 'outgrow' the disorder.

RISKS: Child dismissed from speech services in elementary/middle school — transition plan doesn't address communication needs. College disability services unfamiliar with DLD. Workplace communication difficulties not recognized as disability-related. Self-advocacy not taught because child appeared to 'outgrow' the disorder.

