insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLI Step 7.3: Stuttering-Specific Long-Term Management

SLI — Step 7.3: Stuttering-Specific Long-Term Management

WHAT TO DO: For children/teens who stutter: stuttering management is LONG-TERM, not a 'cure.' Approaches evolve with age: (1) Preschool: Lidcombe Program (parent-delivered, most evidence). (2) School-age: fluency shaping + stuttering modification + avoidance reduction. Self-advocacy and disclosure skills. (3) Teen/adult: continued therapy as needed, support groups (NSA — National Stuttering Association), self-acceptance and desensitization. Key: goal is NOT fluent speech — it's effective communication with reduced impact on participation. IEP should address: classroom accommodations (extra time for verbal responses, alternative to oral reports), social-emotional support, self-advocacy.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: For children/teens who stutter: stuttering management is LONG-TERM, not a 'cure.
STEP 2: ' Approaches evolve with age: (1) Preschool: Lidcombe Program (parent-delivered, most evidence).
STEP 3: (2) School-age: fluency shaping + stuttering modification + avoidance reduction.
STEP 4: Self-advocacy and disclosure skills.
STEP 5: (3) Teen/adult: continued therapy as needed, support groups (NSA — National Stuttering Association), self-acceptance and desensitization.

CONTACT: NATIONAL STUTTERING ASSOCIATION (NSA): westutter.org — (800) 937-8888 — support groups (adult and teen chapters throughout CA), annual conference, peer support.
STUTTERING FOUNDATION: stutteringhelp.org — (800) 992-9392 — free resources, SLP referrals, workshops.
ASHA PROFIND: asha.org/profind — find SLPs specializing in fluency disorders.
FRIENDS (National Association of Young People Who Stutter): friendswhostutter.org — teen programs.
SLP WITH FLUENCY SPECIALTY: Board-recognized specialty — ask for fluency-specialist SLP specifically.
SCHOOL SLP: Ensure IEP addresses stuttering with appropriate goals (not 'fluent speech' but communication confidence and management strategies).

DOCUMENTS: STUTTERING-SPECIFIC DOCUMENTS:
• Comprehensive fluency evaluation (stuttering frequency, types, severity)
• IEP goals addressing: stuttering management strategies, communication confidence, self-advocacy, classroom participation
• NOT goals for 'fluent speech' — this is outdated and harmful
• IEP accommodations: extended time for verbal responses, no penalty for stuttering, alternative to oral reports if desired, safe speaking environment

NSA/FRIENDS RESOURCES:
• Support group participation records
• Conference attendance
• Self-advocacy development documentation

FOR OLDER TEENS/ADULTS:
• ADA workplace accommodation documentation
• Self-disclosure and advocacy plan
• Insurance coverage for ongoing fluency therapy

TIMELINE: Ongoing — long-term management, not cure.

AVOID: DO NOT: For children/teens who stutter: stuttering management is LONG-TERM, not a 'cure | (2) School-age: fluency shaping + stuttering modification + avoidance reduction | Key: goal is NOT fluent speech — it's effective communication with reduced impact on participation

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: SLP not specialized in stuttering — uses outdated approaches. School pressures child to speak in front of class. Peers/teachers interrupt or finish sentences. Child told to 'slow down' or 'take a breath' (increases anxiety, doesn't help). Stuttering dismissed as 'they'll grow out of it.' Covert stuttering (hiding it) not recognized.

RISKS: SLP not specialized in stuttering — uses outdated approaches. School pressures child to speak in front of class. Peers/teachers interrupt or finish sentences. Child told to 'slow down' or 'take a breath' (increases anxiety, doesn't help). Stuttering dismissed as 'they'll grow out of it.' Covert stuttering (hiding it) not recognized.

