insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 1.1: Parent or Teacher Notices Academic Struggles

SLD — Step 1.1: Parent or Teacher Notices Academic Struggles

WHAT TO DO: START TRACKING ACADEMIC CONCERNS IMMEDIATELY: (1) Keep dated samples of your child's work — homework, tests, writing samples, report cards. These become evidence. (2) Note SPECIFIC struggles: reading (decoding, fluency, comprehension?), writing (spelling, handwriting, composition?), math (number sense, computation, word problems?). (3) Compare to grade-level expectations, not siblings. (4) Track HOW LONG homework takes vs. expected time — if 20-min assignment takes 90 min, that's significant. (5) Note if child avoids reading/writing, complains of headaches when reading, loses their place, reverses letters past age 7, or has strong verbal skills but poor written output ('smart but can't show it on paper'). (6) LD patterns: DYSLEXIA = reading (most common, ~80% of LD); DYSCALCULIA = math (~5-8%); DYSGRAPHIA = writing (~7-15%). (7) Talk to the teacher FIRST — ask for specifics: 'Where does my child fall compared to grade-level benchmarks in reading, writing, and math?'

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Collect your child's last 3 report cards, last 5 graded assignments in areas of concern, and any standardized test scores (STAR/SBAC). Create a 'Concerns Folder.'
STEP 2: Write dated observations: 'Oct 5: 2nd grade reading homework (15 min expected) took 55 min. Child cried twice. Reversed b/d in 4 of 10 words. Read 42 WPM (grade level is 90 WPM).'
STEP 3: Email teacher: 'I've noticed [child] struggling in [specific area]. Can we schedule a brief meeting to discuss where they fall compared to grade-level expectations? I'd like to understand their benchmark assessment results.'
STEP 4: At teacher meeting, ask: 'What is their reading level/Lexile? How do they compare to grade benchmarks? What interventions have you tried? Can you share progress monitoring data?'
STEP 5: If teacher confirms struggles, say: 'I'd like to request a formal special education evaluation. I'll put that in writing.'

CONTACT: CLASSROOM TEACHER: Your first contact — request meeting through school email or front office.
SCHOOL PRINCIPAL: If teacher is unresponsive, escalate to principal.
SCHOOL READING SPECIALIST: Many schools have one — ask teacher or principal for referral.
CA STATEWIDE: Contact your local school district's Special Education office. Find your district: cde.ca.gov/ds/si/ds/faq.asp. CA Parent Training Centers: parentcenterhub.org.
PEDIATRICIAN: Request vision AND hearing screening to rule out as causes.
UNDERSTOOD.ORG: Free parent resource — understood.org — take the 'Could my child have a learning disability?' screener.

DOCUMENTS: BEGIN COLLECTING:
• Report cards (last 2-3 years)
• Graded work samples (keep originals/photos)
• Standardized test results (SBAC/STAR/iReady)
• Teacher comments and progress notes
• Benchmark assessment data (from teacher)
• Your dated observations journal
• Homework time log (assignment vs actual time)
• Any medical records (vision, hearing tests)
• Prior report cards from earlier grades

TIMELINE: Act NOW — do not wait. Key timing: if reading concerns exist by mid-1st grade, request evaluation. By mid-2nd grade, the gap between LD readers and peers begins to widen dramatically ('Matthew Effect' — strong readers get stronger, struggling readers fall further behind). Math LD may not surface until grade 3-4. Writing LD may not surface until grade 4-5.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept 'they're just lazy' or 'they'll catch up' — persistent academic struggles despite effort are the hallmark of LD. (2) Assume good grades in some subjects rule out LD — a child can be gifted in math and have severe dyslexia. (3) Wait for the school to notice — teachers manage 25+ students and may not flag a quiet, compliant struggling reader. (4) Confuse LD with low intelligence — LD means a GAP between ability and achievement (your child may be very smart). (5) Blame yourself or your child — LD is neurobiological, not caused by insufficient help or practice. (6) Compare to other children — each child's profile is unique. (7) Wait beyond mid-2nd grade if reading concerns exist — the 'window' for easiest intervention narrows significantly after age 8.

RISKS: ★ CRITICAL DELAYS: (1) Teacher says 'they'll catch up' — delays evaluation 1-2 years during critical window. (2) Gifted students mask LD through high IQ until middle school when demands exceed compensation strategies ('twice-exceptional'/2e). (3) Girls with LD less likely to be referred — they tend to be quiet and compliant rather than disruptive. (4) Minority students under-referred for LD evaluation. (5) Parents blame child's effort rather than recognizing neurobiological LD. (6) School attributes struggles to English learner status when LD is the actual cause. (7) ADHD co-occurs with LD in up to 50% of cases — if only ADHD is identified, LD goes untreated.

RESOURCES: FREE PARENT RESOURCES:
• Understood.org: understood.org — comprehensive LD parent resource, screeners, tech tools, expert Q&A
• Learning Disabilities Association (LDA): ldaamerica.org — parent resources, local chapters
• International Dyslexia Association (IDA): dyslexiaida.org — dyslexia fact sheets, provider directory
• Decoding Dyslexia CA: decodingdyslexiaca.org — CA-specific parent advocacy
• Reading Rockets: readingrockets.org — evidence-based reading strategies
• National Center for LD: ncld.org
• CA PTI: Your local Parent Training & Information center — parentcenterhub.org
BOOKS:
• 'Overcoming Dyslexia' by Sally Shaywitz (essential reading)
• 'The Dyslexia Empowerment Plan' by Ben Foss

