insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 1.2: School Implements Classroom Interventions (Pre-Referral / MTSS)

SLD — Step 1.2: School Implements Classroom Interventions (Pre-Referral / MTSS)

WHAT TO DO: UNDERSTAND YOUR RIGHTS DURING MTSS/RTI: (1) Many schools use Multi-Tiered System of Supports (MTSS) or Response to Intervention (RTI) — Tier 1 (whole class), Tier 2 (small group targeted intervention), Tier 3 (intensive individual intervention). (2) This is GOOD — it provides data and early intervention. BUT: RTI/MTSS CANNOT be used to DELAY your right to request a special education evaluation. (3) Ask the school: 'What specific Tier 2 intervention are you using? Is it evidence-based? How often? For how long? How are you measuring progress?' (4) Request to see progress monitoring data at least every 2 weeks. (5) If your child is in Tier 2 for 6+ weeks without progress, the school should move to Tier 3 or consider evaluation. (6) You can request a special education evaluation AT ANY TIME during RTI — you do not have to wait.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Ask teacher/principal: 'What intervention is my child receiving? What is the program name? How many minutes per day/week? In what group size? Who delivers it? How is progress measured?'
STEP 2: Request progress monitoring data in writing: 'I'd like to receive my child's progress monitoring data (CBM scores, reading fluency data, etc.) every 2 weeks.'
STEP 3: After 6-8 weeks, if inadequate progress: 'My child has been in Tier 2 intervention for [X weeks] and the progress data shows [limited/no improvement]. I am now requesting a formal special education evaluation in writing.'
STEP 4: PUT YOUR EVALUATION REQUEST IN WRITING — hand deliver AND email. This triggers the 15-day assessment plan timeline regardless of RTI status.
STEP 5: Continue RTI interventions during the evaluation process — they are not mutually exclusive.

CONTACT: SCHOOL RTI/MTSS COORDINATOR: Ask front office who manages interventions at your school.
SCHOOL PRINCIPAL: Escalate if RTI is being used to delay evaluation.
DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION: If school won't accept evaluation request.
CA STATEWIDE: Contact your school's RTI/MTSS coordinator through the front office or district Special Education department.
LEGAL HELP IF SCHOOL REFUSES EVALUATION:
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org
• Wrightslaw RTI guidance: wrightslaw.com/info/rti.index.htm

DOCUMENTS: REQUEST FROM SCHOOL:
• Written description of Tier 2 intervention (program name, frequency, duration, group size, provider)
• Progress monitoring data (graphs/charts showing your child's trajectory)
• Benchmark comparison data (your child vs grade level)
• Meeting notes from any Student Study Team (SST) or intervention team meetings

PARENT CREATES:
• Written log of all interventions tried
• Copies of progress monitoring data received
• Your own homework/academic tracking
• Written evaluation request (when ready)

TIMELINE: RTI/MTSS: Typically 6-12 weeks per tier. CRITICAL: RTI timeline does NOT extend the 15-day assessment plan deadline once you submit a written evaluation request. If school says 'we need 12 more weeks of RTI data,' respond: 'I've submitted my written request. The 15-day clock for the assessment plan has started.'

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Let RTI/MTSS delay a special education evaluation — this is the #1 barrier to LD identification in California. If school says 'we need to try RTI first before we can evaluate,' respond: 'Under IDEA, a parent's request for evaluation cannot be delayed by RTI. I'm requesting an evaluation now.' (2) Accept interventions that aren't evidence-based or implemented consistently. (3) Let the school use RTI for more than 8-12 weeks without meaningful progress before escalating. (4) Assume Tier 2 intervention = special education. It's not — it's general education. (5) Sign anything waiving your right to evaluation.

RISKS: ★ RTI GATEKEEPING — THE #1 SLD BARRIER: (1) District uses RTI to delay evaluation by months or years — telling parents 'we need more data.' This violates IDEA when parent has requested evaluation. (2) RTI interventions not evidence-based (e.g., using Reading Recovery for dyslexia when structured literacy is needed). (3) Interventions not implemented with fidelity (right program but wrong delivery). (4) Progress monitoring data not shared with parents. (5) Student Study Team (SST) used as additional delay before evaluation. (6) School tells parent 'they don't qualify for evaluation yet' — FALSE if parent has submitted written request.

RESOURCES: RTI/MTSS PARENT GUIDES:
• Wrightslaw RTI: wrightslaw.com/info/rti.index.htm
• Understood RTI guide: understood.org/en/articles/understanding-response-to-intervention
• NCLD RTI guide: ncld.org
• LDA RTI position: ldaamerica.org
• DRC SERR Manual Chapter 3: serr.disabilityrightsca.org
KEY LEGAL POINT:
• OSEP has confirmed RTI cannot delay parent-requested evaluation. Cite: OSEP Letter to Zirkel (2011), OSEP Memo on RTI (Jan 2011)

