insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 1.3: Parent Requests Formal Special Education Evaluation

SLD — Step 1.3: Parent Requests Formal Special Education Evaluation

WHAT TO DO: SUBMIT YOUR WRITTEN EVALUATION REQUEST: (1) Write a letter requesting a 'comprehensive psychoeducational evaluation in all areas of suspected disability.' (2) Be SPECIFIC about your concerns: 'My child struggles with reading decoding and fluency, written expression including spelling and handwriting, and reading comprehension. I suspect a specific learning disability.' (3) List ALL areas you want tested: cognitive ability, academic achievement (reading, math, written expression), auditory processing, visual processing, processing speed, working memory, executive function, social-emotional (LD causes anxiety/depression). (4) Hand-deliver AND email the letter — keep dated copies. (5) This triggers a 15-day legal clock — the district MUST provide an assessment plan within 15 calendar days.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Write your letter. Template: 'Dear [Special Education Director], I am the parent of [child's name], a student in [grade/teacher] at [school]. I am writing to formally request a comprehensive psychoeducational evaluation under IDEA. I have concerns about [specific academic areas]. I request evaluation in the following areas: cognitive/IQ, academic achievement in all areas, processing skills (auditory, visual, processing speed, working memory), social-emotional impact, and any other areas of suspected disability. Please provide the assessment plan within 15 calendar days per California Education Code § 56321. Sincerely, [your name, date].'
STEP 2: Print two copies. Hand-deliver one to the school office — ask them to date-stamp YOUR copy. Also email to principal AND Special Education Director.
STEP 3: Mark calendar: 15 days from delivery = deadline for assessment plan.
STEP 4: If no assessment plan in 15 days: send follow-up letter: 'My evaluation request was submitted on [date]. Under California law, you have 15 calendar days to provide an assessment plan. This deadline has passed. Please respond immediately or I will file a compliance complaint with CDE.'
STEP 5: When you receive the assessment plan, do NOT sign immediately — review it, compare to your requested areas, and add any missing areas before signing.

CONTACT: DELIVER YOUR LETTER TO:
• School office (hand-deliver, get date stamp)
• Email to: Principal AND Special Education Director/Coordinator
CA STATEWIDE: Contact your local school district's Special Education director. Find your district: cde.ca.gov. CDE complaint line: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp.
IF SCHOOL WON'T ACCEPT OR RESPOND:
• Disability Rights California: (800) 776-5746
• CDE Complaint: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org (Bay Area free advocacy)
• DREDF: dredf.org (Bay Area disability rights)
• Wrightslaw letter templates: wrightslaw.com/info/test.index.htm

DOCUMENTS: PARENT CREATES:
• Written evaluation request letter (CRITICAL — dated, keep copy with receipt stamp)
• Email copy with read receipt
• List of all areas to be evaluated

DISTRICT MUST PROVIDE (within 15 days):
• Assessment plan listing all areas to be evaluated
• Parent rights notification (Procedural Safeguards)
• OR: Prior Written Notice if refusing to evaluate (with reasons)

ALSO PREPARE:
• All collected work samples and report cards
• RTI/MTSS progress monitoring data
• Your observations journal
• Any prior private evaluations

TIMELINE: 15 CALENDAR DAYS: District must provide assessment plan after receiving your written request. If they fail, they are in violation. File CDE complaint. 60 CALENDAR DAYS: From your signed consent on the assessment plan to completed evaluation.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Make a VERBAL request only — there is no paper trail and no legal clock starts. (2) Let the school talk you out of requesting: 'Let's wait another semester' or 'They're not far enough behind.' (3) Accept 'we don't have the resources right now.' (4) Let the school limit the evaluation to just reading — if you suspect LD, request comprehensive evaluation covering ALL academic areas plus processing. (5) Wait for the school to suggest evaluation — YOU have the right to request at any time. (6) Assume you need teacher agreement — you don't.

RISKS: ★ CRITICAL: (1) Parent makes verbal request — no paper trail, no legal timeline triggered. (2) District discourages evaluation: 'Let's wait' / 'They're not behind enough' — delay tactic. (3) District says 'RTI must be completed first' — FALSE, violates IDEA. (4) District refuses evaluation — must provide Prior Written Notice with reasons (then parent can file complaint). (5) Assessment plan doesn't include all areas parent requested — parent should NOT sign until all areas are included. (6) Parent doesn't know they can request evaluation — waits for school to suggest it.

RESOURCES: EVALUATION REQUEST HELP:
• Wrightslaw: wrightslaw.com — sample letters, IEP strategies, law
• Understood: understood.org/en/articles/how-to-request-a-free-school-evaluation
• LDA: ldaamerica.org — parent guides
• DRC SERR Manual: serr.disabilityrightsca.org — comprehensive rights guide
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free advocacy (Bay Area)
• TASK: taskca.org — parent training on evaluation rights
• Matrix: matrixparents.org — Bay Area parent training

