insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 2.1: Review & Sign Assessment Plan

SLD — Step 2.1: Review & Sign Assessment Plan

WHAT TO DO: REVIEW THE ASSESSMENT PLAN CAREFULLY BEFORE SIGNING: (1) Compare what you requested vs what the district proposes to test. (2) For SLD, the assessment plan should include AT MINIMUM: cognitive/IQ (WISC-V or similar), academic achievement in ALL areas (WJ-IV, WIAT, KTEA — reading decoding, reading fluency, reading comprehension, math calculation, math reasoning, written expression, oral expression, listening comprehension), processing skills (processing speed, working memory, auditory processing, visual processing), classroom observation, teacher input, parent input, review of records, social-emotional assessment. (3) If any area is MISSING, write back immediately: 'I request the following areas be added to the assessment plan before I sign: [list areas].' (4) Once satisfied, sign and return — the 60-day evaluation clock starts when you sign.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: When you receive the assessment plan (within 15 days of your request), read every area listed. Highlight anything missing.
STEP 2: Compare to this SLD assessment checklist:
  ✓ Cognitive/IQ (WISC-V, SB-5)
  ✓ Reading (decoding, fluency, comprehension)
  ✓ Math (calculation, reasoning)
  ✓ Written expression (spelling, composition, handwriting)
  ✓ Processing speed
  ✓ Working memory
  ✓ Auditory processing
  ✓ Visual processing/motor
  ✓ Executive function
  ✓ Social-emotional/behavioral
  ✓ Classroom observation
  ✓ Teacher/parent input
STEP 3: If areas are missing, email the SpEd coordinator: 'Before signing the assessment plan, I request the following areas be added: [list]. Please send a revised plan.'
STEP 4: Once all areas are included, sign and return. Mark calendar: 60 days from signing = evaluation completion deadline.

CONTACT: SCHOOL PSYCHOLOGIST: Will conduct the evaluation — ask to speak with them before signing the plan. 'Can you walk me through what tests you'll administer and what areas they cover?'
SPECIAL EDUCATION COORDINATOR: Sends the assessment plan — your contact for adding areas.
CA STATEWIDE: Contact your district's Special Education office. Find your district: cde.ca.gov/ds/si/ds/faq.asp.
IF DISTRICT WON'T ADD REQUESTED AREAS:
• Write: 'I disagree with the proposed assessment plan. Under IDEA, the evaluation must assess in all areas of suspected disability. I request [areas] be added.'
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org

DOCUMENTS: DISTRICT PROVIDES:
• Assessment plan document
• Parent rights notification (Procedural Safeguards)
• Consent form for you to sign

PARENT SHOULD HAVE:
• Your original evaluation request letter
• Your assessment area checklist (compared to plan)
• Written additions if areas are missing
• All collected work samples, report cards, RTI data
• Signed copy (keep your copy, return district's copy)

TIMELINE: 15 calendar days: District provides assessment plan. Parent reviews and signs (take 1-5 days to review — no deadline on parent to sign, but don't delay unnecessarily). 60 CALENDAR DAYS: From signed consent to completed evaluation (excluding school breaks >5 days).

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Sign the assessment plan the same day you receive it — take it home and review carefully. (2) Sign without checking that ALL areas of concern are listed. (3) Let the district omit processing assessments (processing speed, working memory, auditory processing) — these are often the KEY to understanding WHY your child struggles. (4) Accept a plan that only tests reading if you also have math or writing concerns. (5) Forget that YOU have the right to ADD areas — the district's initial plan is a starting point, not final.

RISKS: ★ ASSESSMENT PLAN GAPS: (1) Plan only tests reading but child also has math/writing concerns — incomplete. (2) Processing assessments omitted (processing speed, working memory) — these explain the WHY behind academic struggles. (3) Executive function not assessed — impacts organization, task completion. (4) No observation planned — critical for seeing real-world performance. (5) Social-emotional impact not assessed — LD causes significant anxiety and self-esteem impact. (6) Parent signs without reviewing — loses opportunity to add areas.

RESOURCES: UNDERSTANDING EVALUATIONS:
• Wrightslaw evaluation guide: wrightslaw.com/info/test.index.htm
• Understood evaluation guide: understood.org/en/articles/the-evaluation-process
• DRC evaluation rights: disabilityrightsca.org/publications/understanding-psycho-educational-evaluations
• NCLD evaluation checklist: ncld.org
• LDA evaluation fact sheet: ldaamerica.org

