insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 2.2: School Conducts Psychoeducational Evaluation

SLD — Step 2.2: School Conducts Psychoeducational Evaluation

WHAT TO DO: PARTICIPATE ACTIVELY IN THE EVALUATION: (1) Complete the Parent Input Form thoroughly — this is your chance to describe your child's daily challenges, homework battles, emotional reactions, and history. Use specific examples with dates. (2) Share ALL your documentation with the school psychologist: work samples, RTI data, homework time logs, your observations journal, and any private evaluations. (3) Ask the school psychologist: 'When will you observe my child in the classroom? Can you observe during a reading/writing/math lesson when they typically struggle?' (4) Know what tests will be administered: IQ (WISC-V typical), achievement (WJ-IV, WIAT, or KTEA), processing, and behavioral rating scales (BASC-3 or similar). (5) California uses EITHER the discrepancy model (significant gap between IQ and achievement) OR RTI model — ask which your district uses.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: When you receive the Parent Input Form, set aside 1 hour to complete it thoughtfully. For EVERY question, use specific examples: not 'struggles with reading' but 'reads at 45 WPM in 3rd grade (benchmark is 100 WPM), frequently loses place, skips lines, guesses at words from first letter, avoids reading aloud, and takes 90 minutes for 20-minute homework assignments.'
STEP 2: Attach your documentation to the Parent Input Form: work samples, homework time log, report cards, RTI data.
STEP 3: Email the school psychologist: 'I've completed the Parent Input Form and attached additional documentation. I'd like to discuss my child's evaluation before testing begins. When can we talk?'
STEP 4: Track the 60-day timeline. At 30 days, email: 'Checking in on the evaluation timeline. When are testing sessions scheduled?'
STEP 5: After testing is complete, request ALL reports at least 5 days BEFORE the IEP eligibility meeting.

CONTACT: SCHOOL PSYCHOLOGIST: Primary evaluator — get their name, email, and direct phone from the SpEd coordinator.
SCHOOL SLP: If speech-language is part of the assessment.
SCHOOL OT: If handwriting/fine motor is a concern.
CA STATEWIDE: Contact your school psychologist through the district Special Education office.
IF EVALUATION IS DELAYED:
• Email SpEd coordinator: 'The 60-day evaluation deadline is [date]. Please confirm completion.'
• If deadline passes: File CDE compliance complaint at cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp

DOCUMENTS: PARENT COMPLETES:
• Parent Input Form (detailed, with examples)
• Attached documentation (work samples, RTI data, homework log, report cards)

SCHOOL PRODUCES:
• Psychoeducational evaluation report (cognitive, achievement, processing)
• Classroom observation report
• Teacher Input Form results
• Speech-language report (if assessed)
• OT report (if assessed)
• Social-emotional assessment results
• Summary of eligibility recommendation

REQUEST ALL REPORTS 5+ DAYS BEFORE IEP MEETING

TIMELINE: 60 CALENDAR DAYS from signed consent to completed evaluation (excluding school breaks >5 consecutive days). At 30 days: check in. At 50 days: confirm completion date. If deadline missed: document and file CDE complaint.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Skip or rush the Parent Input Form — it's often the most powerful evidence because it describes real-world functioning. (2) Fail to share your homework time data — this shows functional impact beyond test scores. (3) Assume the school psychologist knows everything about your child — they have limited time and dozens of evaluations. (4) Forget about the 60-day deadline — if approaching deadline, email: 'Evaluation was consented on [date]. The 60-day deadline is [date]. Please confirm completion schedule.' (5) Allow testing only on a 'good day' — if your child struggles in the morning, that's when testing should happen.

RISKS: ★ EVALUATION FAILURES: (1) School psychologist has 80+ evaluations — limited time per student. (2) Only a narrow test battery used — misses processing deficits. (3) Discrepancy model misses LD in gifted students ('scores are too high to show discrepancy'). (4) RTI model delays identification while collecting more data. (5) Testing not done in primary language — bilingual students may be misidentified or missed. (6) No classroom observation during area of difficulty. (7) Teacher Input Form incomplete or minimizing. (8) Parent Input Form not provided or completed hastily.

RESOURCES: EVALUATION PREPARATION:
• Wrightslaw: wrightslaw.com/info/test.index.htm — parent guide to evaluations
• Understood: understood.org — evaluation explainer with glossary
• DRC Psychoeducational Evaluation Guide: disabilityrightsca.org
• National Center for LD: ncld.org
• 'From Emotions to Advocacy' by Pam Wright — Chapter on evaluations
• Parent Input Form guide: wrightlaw.com/advoc/articles/parent.input.htm

