insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 2.3: Private Psychoeducational Evaluation (Optional but Powerful)

SLD — Step 2.3: Private Psychoeducational Evaluation (Optional but Powerful)

WHAT TO DO: CONSIDER A PRIVATE EVALUATION IF: (1) You want a more comprehensive assessment than school typically provides. (2) You suspect the school evaluation will be insufficient or biased toward denial. (3) You want specific dyslexia/LD identification that goes beyond school criteria. (4) You need documentation for both school AND private tutoring. PRIVATE EVALUATIONS: Neuropsychologist provides the most comprehensive assessment ($3K-$6K). Educational psychologist ($1.5K-$3K) may be sufficient. Some insurance covers under 'neurodevelopmental' or 'psychological testing' benefits. IEE (Independent Educational Evaluation): If you disagree with school's evaluation, you have the RIGHT to request an IEE at district expense. District must either pay for IEE or file due process to defend their evaluation.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Call insurance: 'Does my plan cover neuropsychological or psychoeducational testing for my child? What is my benefit? Do I need a referral?'
STEP 2: If pursuing private eval: search for neuropsychologists or educational psychologists specializing in LD. Ask: 'Do you evaluate for specific learning disabilities? What tests do you use? What is your experience with dyslexia/dyscalculia?'
STEP 3: If pursuing IEE (at district expense): write to the district: 'I disagree with the school evaluation completed on [date]. Under 34 CFR §300.502, I am requesting an Independent Educational Evaluation at public expense.'
STEP 4: District must either (a) agree and pay for IEE, or (b) file due process to defend their evaluation. Most districts will agree.
STEP 5: Whether private pay or IEE, share results with school for IEP consideration.

CONTACT: PRIVATE EVALUATORS (Bay Area):
• Psychology Today directory: psychologytoday.com — filter by 'learning disabilities,' 'neuropsychological testing,' children
• University psychology clinics — many CA universities offer sliding-scale assessments (search 'psychology clinic' + your nearest UC or CSU)
• Ask pediatrician for referral
IEE RIGHTS:
• DRC: (800) 776-5746 — IEE rights guidance
• Wrightslaw IEE guide: wrightslaw.com/info/test.iee.index.htm
INSURANCE:
• Call number on back of card — ask for 'psychological testing' or 'neurodevelopmental assessment' benefits

DOCUMENTS: PRIVATE EVALUATION PRODUCES:
• Comprehensive neuropsychological or psychoeducational report
• Specific LD diagnosis (if applicable)
• Detailed recommendations for school and home
• Supporting documentation for IEP/504

IEE DOCUMENTS:
• Written IEE request letter (keep copy)
• District response (agreement or due process filing)
• IEE evaluation report

INSURANCE DOCUMENTS:
• Referral from pediatrician
• Pre-authorization (if required)
• EOB after evaluation

TIMELINE: Private wait times: 2-8 weeks for neuropsychologist (faster than dev ped). Insurance pre-auth: 5-15 business days. IEE: district must respond within 'reasonable time' — typically 2-4 weeks. CANCEL LIST: Ask to be put on provider's cancellation list.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Pay for private evaluation before checking insurance — many plans cover neuropsychological testing. (2) Assume private eval replaces school eval — you need BOTH (school eval determines eligibility, private eval can support and supplement). (3) Use an evaluator who is not experienced with learning disabilities (look for specialty in LD, dyslexia, educational assessment). (4) Skip the IEE option — if you disagree with school's evaluation, the district may be required to pay for a private one.

RISKS: Cost $2K-$6K if insurance doesn't cover. Insurance denies 'educational' testing. Private eval disagrees with school — district may disregard. District fights IEE request. Parent doesn't know about IEE right. Long waitlists for qualified evaluators.

RESOURCES: IEE RIGHTS:
• Wrightslaw IEE: wrightslaw.com/info/test.iee.index.htm
• DRC: disabilityrightsca.org
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org
FINDING EVALUATORS:
• Psychology Today: psychologytoday.com (filter by specialty)
• IDA provider directory: dyslexiaida.org
• LDA: ldaamerica.org
• Your pediatrician's referral network

