insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 2.4: Eligibility Determination — SLD Criteria

SLD — Step 2.4: Eligibility Determination — SLD Criteria

WHAT TO DO: UNDERSTAND AND PREPARE FOR THE ELIGIBILITY DECISION: (1) IEP team (including you) reviews all evaluation data. (2) For SLD eligibility, team must determine ALL THREE: (a) Child has SLD (using discrepancy model OR RTI model — California allows both), (b) It adversely affects educational performance, (c) Child needs specially designed instruction. (3) SLD covers 7 areas under IDEA: oral expression, listening comprehension, written expression, basic reading skill, reading fluency, reading comprehension, mathematics calculation, mathematics reasoning. (4) If using discrepancy model: typically 1.5 standard deviation gap between IQ and achievement in one or more areas. (5) CRITICAL: Request reports 5+ days before the meeting. Review scores yourself. Use Wrightslaw's chart to understand standard scores, percentiles, and discrepancies.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Request all evaluation reports 5+ days before IEP meeting. Read them carefully.
STEP 2: For each test, note the standard score, percentile, and whether it's significantly below average (below 85 = low average, below 70 = significantly below).
STEP 3: Calculate discrepancies yourself: if IQ composite is 105 and reading composite is 78, that's a 27-point gap (significant). Use Wrightslaw discrepancy chart.
STEP 4: Prepare a written summary of your analysis for the meeting. Include: 'Based on the evaluation data, my child meets SLD criteria because [specific data points].'
STEP 5: At the meeting, ask: 'What specific eligibility criteria is the team using? Discrepancy model or RTI model?'
STEP 6: IF ELIGIBLE: proceed to IEP development. IF DENIED: say: 'I disagree. I request Prior Written Notice with specific reasons for denial. I also request a Section 504 evaluation as an alternative. And I may pursue an IEE at district expense.'
STEP 7: If denied, you have 2 years to file complaint or due process.

CONTACT: IEP TEAM MEETING: You, school psychologist, SpEd teacher, general ed teacher, administrator.
BRING AN ADVOCATE IF POSSIBLE:
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org — free IEP advocates (Bay Area)
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• TASK: taskca.org — parent mentors
• Matrix: matrixparents.org
IF DENIED:
• Request Prior Written Notice (legal document)
• Request 504 evaluation
• Consider IEE request
• Contact DRC: (800) 776-5746
• CDE complaint: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp

DOCUMENTS: FOR THE MEETING:
• All evaluation reports (reviewed, annotated)
• Your score analysis summary
• Parent Concerns Letter (updated)
• Work samples showing impact
• RTI/MTSS data
• Private evaluation (if obtained)

AFTER THE MEETING:
• Eligibility determination document
• Prior Written Notice (if denied)
• IEP document (if eligible, may be same meeting or scheduled within 30 days)
• Parent rights notification

TIMELINE: Eligibility meeting: within 60 days of signed consent (same deadline as evaluation completion). If eligible, IEP development typically same meeting or within 30 days. 2-YEAR statute of limitations for filing complaint or due process if denied.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Go to the eligibility meeting without reviewing all reports first. (2) Accept 'they're not far enough behind' without understanding the specific scores and criteria. (3) Accept denial without requesting Prior Written Notice (written explanation of why). (4) Forget that 504 Plan is a fallback if IEP is denied. (5) Assume a gifted child can't have SLD — 'twice-exceptional' (2e) children can have high IQ AND significant LD. The discrepancy model may actually help identify these students. (6) Let the team dismiss processing deficits as 'not educationally relevant.'

RISKS: ★ ELIGIBILITY BARRIERS: (1) Discrepancy threshold too high — child struggling but 'not far enough behind.' (2) Gifted student denied — scores 'too high' despite significant gap. (3) RTI model delays identification while collecting more data. (4) English learner exclusion used inappropriately — team must rule out language difference vs LD but often over-excludes. (5) District says 'lack of instruction' is the cause — must document adequate instruction was provided. (6) Team overrides school psychologist recommendation. (7) Parent outnumbered and pressured to accept denial.

RESOURCES: ELIGIBILITY PREPARATION:
• Wrightslaw eligibility guide: wrightslaw.com/info/sld.index.htm
• Wrightslaw discrepancy chart: wrightslaw.com/advoc/articles/tests.htm
• Understood: understood.org/en/articles/types-of-tests-for-learning-disabilities
• DRC SERR Manual Chapter 3: serr.disabilityrightsca.org
• 'Overcoming Dyslexia' by Sally Shaywitz — Chapter on school identification
• LDA position on eligibility: ldaamerica.org
• Decoding Dyslexia CA: decodingdyslexiaca.org

PRIMARY AGENCY: Local School District
(IEP Team)
OTHER AGENCIES: Parent
School psychologist
