insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 3.1: IEP Development — SLD-Specific Goals & Services

SLD — Step 3.1: IEP Development — SLD-Specific Goals & Services

WHAT TO DO: ADVOCATE FOR A STRONG IEP: (1) IEP must include: Present Levels of Academic Achievement and Functional Performance (PLAAFP), measurable annual goals, specially designed instruction (SDI) with specific methodology, accommodations, modifications, and related services. (2) For DYSLEXIA: demand STRUCTURED LITERACY instruction (Orton-Gillingham, Wilson Reading System, Barton, RAVE-O, Lindamood-Bell). These are evidence-based and EXPLICITLY teach phonics, decoding, fluency, and comprehension. Generic 'reading support' is NOT sufficient. (3) For DYSCALCULIA: math intervention using concrete-representational-abstract (CRA) approach, manipulatives, explicit instruction. (4) For DYSGRAPHIA: OT for handwriting, assistive technology (text-to-speech, speech-to-text, word prediction). (5) Goals must be SMART: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound. (6) DEMAND specific program names — not 'reading intervention' but 'Orton-Gillingham-based structured literacy, 5x/week, 45 min/session, delivered by trained specialist.'

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before IEP meeting, prepare a written 'IEP Request' document listing: (a) Specific goals you want in each area, (b) Specific programs/methodologies you want (e.g., 'structured literacy'), (c) Service frequency/duration (e.g., '45 min daily'), (d) Accommodations (list all needed), (e) Assistive technology requests.
STEP 2: Research evidence-based programs: For dyslexia, see IDA Structured Literacy page. Confirm your school has trained staff.
STEP 3: At IEP meeting, present your request. For each goal, ask: 'How will this be measured? How often will progress be reported? What evidence-based program will be used?'
STEP 4: Request AT consideration: 'Has AT been considered? My child would benefit from [text-to-speech/speech-to-text/audiobooks/graphic organizers].'
STEP 5: Request ESY evaluation if applicable: 'I want ESY considered — my child shows regression after school breaks.'
STEP 6: If you disagree with the offer, do NOT sign: 'I'd like to take this home to review.'

CONTACT: IEP TEAM: You, SpEd teacher, gen ed teacher, school psychologist, administrator. Bring advocate if possible.
STRUCTURED LITERACY INFORMATION:
• IDA: dyslexiaida.org/structured-literacy — explanation and program list
• Orton-Gillingham Academy: ortonacademy.org
• Wilson Reading System: wilsonlanguage.com
• Lindamood-Bell: lindamoodbell.com
ADVOCACY SUPPORT:
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• Wrightslaw IEP guide: wrightslaw.com/info/iep.index.htm
• TASK: taskca.org
• Matrix: matrixparents.org

DOCUMENTS: IEP DOCUMENT INCLUDES:
• PLAAFP (Present Levels)
• Annual goals (measurable, time-bound)
• SDI page (program, frequency, duration, provider, setting)
• Accommodations page
• Modifications page
• AT consideration
• ESY determination
• Placement (LRE)
• Services page (speech, OT, if applicable)
• Progress reporting schedule (quarterly)

PARENT BRINGS:
• Your written IEP request document
• Evaluation reports (annotated)
• Private eval (if applicable)
• Research on evidence-based programs
• Work samples

TIMELINE: IEP meeting: within 30 days of completed evaluation. IEP reviewed annually (every 12 months). Parent can request IEP meeting at ANY time. Progress reports: quarterly (aligned with grading periods). Services begin upon parent consent.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept vague goals ('improve reading' → instead: 'Read grade-level passages at 100 WPM with 95% accuracy by [date]'). (2) Accept vague SDI ('modified work' → instead: 'Structured literacy instruction using Wilson Reading System, 45 min daily, 1:1 or small group'). (3) Accept only 'extra time' and 'preferential seating' as accommodations — these are table stakes, not sufficient. (4) Let the school use a non-evidence-based reading program for dyslexia (many popular programs are NOT structured literacy). (5) Accept no assistive technology consideration — IEP team MUST consider AT for every student. (6) Skip requesting ESY (Extended School Year) if your child regresses over summer.

RISKS: ★ IEP PITFALLS FOR SLD: (1) Non-evidence-based reading program used — many schools use Fountas & Pinnell or balanced literacy rather than structured literacy for dyslexic students (research shows structured literacy is significantly more effective). (2) Goals are vague and unmeasurable. (3) SDI is 'pull-out' 30 min 2x/week — insufficient for moderate-severe LD. (4) AT not considered (checkbox left unchecked). (5) ESY denied despite regression evidence. (6) No specific program named — 'reading support' instead of 'Wilson Reading System.' (7) SpEd teacher not trained in structured literacy. (8) Accommodation list is only 'extra time' and 'preferential seating.'

RESOURCES: IEP FOR LD:
• Wrightslaw IEP strategies: wrightslaw.com/info/iep.index.htm
• Understood IEP guide: understood.org
• IDA IEP accommodations: dyslexiaida.org
• Decoding Dyslexia CA: decodingdyslexiaca.org
• LDA: ldaamerica.org
• NCLD: ncld.org
• AT for LD: understood.org/en/articles/assistive-technology-for-learning-disabilities
BOOKS:
• 'From Emotions to Advocacy' by Pam Wright
• 'The Complete IEP Guide' by Lawrence Siegel
• 'Overcoming Dyslexia' by Sally Shaywitz (school advocacy chapter)

PRIMARY AGENCY: Local School District
OTHER AGENCIES: Parent
General ed teacher
Special ed teacher
