insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 3.2: Assistive Technology & Accommodations

SLD — Step 3.2: Assistive Technology & Accommodations

WHAT TO DO: DEMAND ASSISTIVE TECHNOLOGY FOR YOUR CHILD: (1) IDEA requires IEP team to consider AT for EVERY student — it's not optional. (2) AT for dyslexia: text-to-speech (Learning Ally, NaturalReader, Bookshare — FREE for students with LD), audiobooks, read-aloud tools, colored overlays, large print. (3) AT for dysgraphia: speech-to-text (Google Voice Typing, Dragon), word prediction (Co:Writer), graphic organizers, laptop/tablet for writing, smart pen. (4) AT for dyscalculia: calculator (even on tests), graph paper, manipulatives apps, math notation tools. (5) ACCOMMODATIONS beyond basics: extended time (1.5x or 2x), separate testing environment, tests read aloud, reduced assignment length, no penalty for spelling in content areas, note-taking support, copy of teacher notes, preferential seating, movement breaks, chunked assignments.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Before IEP meeting, research AT options for your child's specific LD type. Create a list of requested AT tools.
STEP 2: At IEP meeting: 'I'd like to discuss assistive technology. Has the AT consideration been completed? I'm requesting [specific tools] for my child.'
STEP 3: If the team says AT isn't needed, ask: 'What specific AT tools were considered and why were they rejected? Please document this in the IEP.'
STEP 4: If AT evaluation needed: request one. Say: 'I request a formal AT evaluation to determine what tools would support my child's access to the curriculum.'
STEP 5: Register for Bookshare (bookshare.org — FREE for qualified students) and Learning Ally (learningally.org).
STEP 6: For accommodations, provide a comprehensive list organized by setting (classroom, homework, testing).

CONTACT: AT RESOURCES:
• Bookshare: bookshare.org — FREE digital library for LD (apply through school or individually)
• Learning Ally: learningally.org — human-narrated audiobooks (membership ~$99/yr but school can join)
• Google Accessibility: Chromebook built-in tools (Select-to-speak, dictation)
• ATAP (CA Assistive Technology Access Program): atnet.org
• CAST UDL: cast.org — Universal Design for Learning resources
AT EVALUATION:
• Request through IEP team
• School AT specialist (ask if your district has one)
• ATAP: atnet.org — state AT program

DOCUMENTS: AT DOCUMENTS:
• AT consideration documented in IEP (IDEA requirement)
• AT evaluation report (if formal eval conducted)
• AT listed on IEP accommodations page
• Bookshare membership documentation
• Learning Ally membership
• AT training plan for student and staff

PARENT TRACKS:
• Which AT tools are in place
• Whether AT is actually being used in classroom
• Student's progress with AT
• Any AT needs not yet met

TIMELINE: AT must be considered at EVERY IEP meeting (IDEA requirement). AT evaluation: within 60 days if requested as part of assessment. Bookshare: apply online, approved within days. Learning Ally: immediate membership.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Accept an IEP that says 'AT considered — not needed' without specific explanation. (2) Let AT be limited to special education settings only — AT must be available in general education too. (3) Forget about FREE AT resources: Bookshare (bookshare.org — free ebooks for LD students), Learning Ally (learningally.org — human-narrated audiobooks), Google Accessibility features. (4) Accept only 'extra time' as accommodation — most LD students need much more. (5) Let the school say 'we don't have that technology' — they must provide it if needed.

RISKS: ★ AT FAILURES: (1) AT 'considered' but not actually evaluated or provided. (2) AT only available in resource room, not general education. (3) Teacher not trained on student's AT tools. (4) Student embarrassed to use AT in class (needs desensitization and normalization). (5) AT device stays at school — student can't access at home for homework. (6) Free resources (Bookshare, Learning Ally) not activated. (7) School claims cost as barrier — AT is required regardless of cost.

RESOURCES: AT FOR LD:
• Understood AT guide: understood.org/en/articles/assistive-technology-for-learning-disabilities
• Bookshare: bookshare.org
• Learning Ally: learningally.org
• CAST UDL: cast.org
• ATAP: atnet.org
• Wrightslaw AT: wrightslaw.com/info/atech.index.htm
• IDA AT guide: dyslexiaida.org
• LD Online: ldonline.org — AT resources

PRIMARY AGENCY: Local School District
OTHER AGENCIES: AT specialist
