insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 5.1: Private Tutoring & Educational Therapy

SLD — Step 5.1: Private Tutoring & Educational Therapy

WHAT TO DO: SUPPLEMENT SCHOOL SERVICES WITH PRIVATE SUPPORT: (1) Many families use private structured literacy tutoring (Orton-Gillingham, Wilson, Barton) to supplement school services. (2) Educational therapists combine teaching with therapeutic support — particularly helpful for LD-related anxiety and self-esteem. (3) Costs: $75-$200/hour, typically 2-3x/week. (4) Insurance RARELY covers tutoring or educational therapy (it's 'educational' not 'medical'). (5) IEE AT DISTRICT EXPENSE: If you disagree with school evaluation, district may be required to pay for independent evaluation. (6) Tax deductions: Tutoring prescribed by a physician for a diagnosed LD may be tax-deductible as medical expense (get physician letter of medical necessity). (7) Ensure private tutoring goals ALIGN with IEP goals.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Search for structured literacy tutors: IDA provider directory (dyslexiaida.org), Orton-Gillingham Academy, Wilson-certified tutors, Barton System tutors.
STEP 2: Interview tutors: 'What methodology do you use? Are you certified in a structured literacy program? What is your experience with [dyslexia/dyscalculia/dysgraphia]?'
STEP 3: If cost is a barrier, ask school: 'Can the district provide structured literacy instruction as part of the IEP?' If they can't, document this.
STEP 4: For tax deduction: Get a letter from your pediatrician: 'Specialized tutoring is medically necessary for treatment of [child's] diagnosed learning disability.'
STEP 5: Track all expenses for tax filing: tutor fees, evaluation costs, AT purchases, transportation to tutoring.

CONTACT: FIND PRIVATE TUTORS:
• IDA provider directory: dyslexiaida.org/provider-directories
• Orton-Gillingham Academy: ortonacademy.org — find certified practitioners
• Wilson Language: wilsonlanguage.com — find Wilson-certified tutors
• Barton Reading & Spelling: bartonreading.com — train yourself or find tutor
• Learning Ally: learningally.org
• Academic Language Therapy Association: altaread.org
LOW-COST OPTIONS:
• University tutoring clinics (UC and CSU systems)
• University education departments (supervised student tutors)
• Barton System (parent-delivered at home, ~$400/level)

DOCUMENTS: PRIVATE SERVICE DOCUMENTS:
• Tutor credentials and methodology
• Treatment plan from tutor
• Progress reports from tutor (align with IEP goals)
• Physician letter of medical necessity (for tax deduction)
• Receipts for all LD-related expenses
• IEE documentation (if requesting at district expense)

TAX DOCUMENTS:
• All receipts for evaluations, tutoring, AT, therapy
• Physician letter
• IEP/504 documentation (proof of disability)
• IRS Form 1040 Schedule A (medical expenses >7.5% AGI)

TIMELINE: Private tutoring: begin as soon as possible (no waiting period). IEE: request at any time after school evaluation. Tax deduction: file with annual return. Structured literacy programs: typically 1-2 years minimum for meaningful progress.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Use private tutoring as substitute for school's legal obligation to provide FAPE — the school must provide appropriate education regardless. (2) Hire a tutor who isn't trained in structured literacy for dyslexia (generic tutoring often doesn't help). (3) Forget to coordinate private and school goals. (4) Skip getting a physician letter for tax deduction. (5) Ignore IEE option — district may be required to pay.

RISKS: Cost prohibitive ($150-400/month). No insurance coverage. Private tutor not trained in structured literacy. Private goals don't align with school IEP. IEE request rejected by district. Tax deduction missed. Quality varies enormously.

RESOURCES: PRIVATE SERVICES:
• IDA: dyslexiaida.org — provider directory, fact sheets
• Decoding Dyslexia CA: decodingdyslexiaca.org
• Barton System: bartonreading.com — parent-delivered option
• Wrightslaw IEE: wrightslaw.com/info/test.iee.index.htm
• LDA: ldaamerica.org
• NCLD: ncld.org
TAX INFO:
• IRS Publication 502 (medical expense deductions)
• Consult tax professional experienced with disability deductions

PRIMARY AGENCY: Private Providers
OTHER AGENCIES: Insurance (limited)
School district (IEE)
