insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 5.2: Medical Evaluation for Co-occurring Conditions

SLD — Step 5.2: Medical Evaluation for Co-occurring Conditions

WHAT TO DO: SCREEN FOR CO-OCCURRING CONDITIONS — THEY'RE THE RULE, NOT EXCEPTION: (1) ADHD co-occurs with LD in 30-50% of cases — if your child has LD AND attention difficulties, pursue ADHD evaluation. ADHD medication may help but does NOT remediate the LD. (2) ANXIETY co-occurs frequently — LD causes school-related anxiety, test anxiety, social anxiety from embarrassment. (3) DEPRESSION: repeated academic failure affects self-esteem. Watch for avoidance, withdrawal, 'I'm stupid' statements. (4) If ADHD is confirmed: child may qualify for IEP under BOTH SLD and OHI (Other Health Impairment) categories, or receive dual 504 accommodations. (5) Speech-language disorders: check for underlying language issues contributing to reading difficulties.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Ask pediatrician: 'My child has a learning disability. I'd like to screen for ADHD, anxiety, and depression as well. Can you administer Vanderbilt/SNAP-IV rating scales?'
STEP 2: If ADHD confirmed: pursue ADHD-specific accommodations in addition to LD accommodations. See ADHD Journey Map for full guidance.
STEP 3: If anxiety/depression: request school counseling or ERMHS (Educationally Related Mental Health Services) through IEP.
STEP 4: If speech-language concerns: request SLP evaluation through school.
STEP 5: Update IEP to address ALL conditions — not just the LD.

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN: Primary screener for ADHD, anxiety, depression.
CHILD PSYCHIATRIST: For medication evaluation if ADHD confirmed. Ask pediatrician for referral.
SCHOOL COUNSELOR: For school-based mental health support.
ERMHS: Request through IEP for educationally-related mental health services.
CHADD: chadd.org — ADHD resources if ADHD confirmed.
ADDITUDE: additudemag.com — LD + ADHD resources.

DOCUMENTS: CO-OCCURRING CONDITION DOCUMENTS:
• ADHD evaluation results (if applicable)
• Vanderbilt/SNAP-IV rating scales
• Psychiatric evaluation (if applicable)
• Anxiety/depression screening results
• ERMHS assessment (if requested)
• Updated IEP with dual eligibility (SLD + OHI)
• Medication management plan (if applicable)
• SLP evaluation (if language concerns)

TIMELINE: ADHD evaluation: 1-3 months. Psychiatric eval: 2-6 months wait. ERMHS: request through IEP, assessment within 60 days. Medication titration: 2-4 weeks.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Treat only LD while missing ADHD — medication + LD intervention together is more effective than either alone. (2) Dismiss anxiety as 'just nerves' — it may require treatment. (3) Attribute ALL struggles to LD when ADHD is also present — both need separate interventions. (4) Skip speech-language screening — some reading difficulties have underlying language disorder roots. (5) Assume medication will fix reading — ADHD meds improve attention but don't teach reading skills.

RISKS: ★ Co-occurring ADHD missed — child treated for LD only. Anxiety from LD not addressed. ADHD medication prescribed but LD intervention not started. ERMHS not offered. Dual eligibility (SLD + OHI) not pursued. Speech-language link to reading not evaluated.

RESOURCES: CO-OCCURRING CONDITIONS:
• CHADD: chadd.org — ADHD resources
• ADDitude: additudemag.com — LD + ADHD articles
• NAMI: nami.org — mental health support
• Anxiety and Depression Association: adaa.org
• ADHD Journey Map in this workbook — cross-reference
• SLI Journey Map — if speech-language concerns

PRIMARY AGENCY: Pediatrician /
Psychiatrist
OTHER AGENCIES: Insurance
School district
