insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 6.1: Annual IEP Review & Progress Monitoring

SLD — Step 6.1: Annual IEP Review & Progress Monitoring

WHAT TO DO: ACTIVELY MANAGE YOUR CHILD'S IEP YEAR-ROUND: (1) Request and review quarterly progress reports. If goals show insufficient progress, request an IEP meeting to revise approach. (2) At annual IEP review: (a) Are goals advancing or repeating? (b) Is the intervention program working? (c) Is service frequency sufficient? (d) Are accommodations being implemented consistently? (e) Does AT need updating? (3) TRACK GRADES AND STANDARDIZED TEST SCORES — if your child isn't making expected progress with services, the IEP needs revision. (4) Visit the classroom — you have the right to observe. (5) Monitor service delivery — are the minutes on the IEP actually being provided? (6) Triennial re-evaluation every 3 years — generally DO NOT waive this.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Create an 'IEP Monitoring Calendar': quarterly progress report review dates, annual IEP date, triennial date.
STEP 2: At each quarterly progress report, check: is your child on track to meet annual goals? If not, email: 'My child is not on track for [goal]. I'd like to discuss revising the approach at an IEP meeting.'
STEP 3: 30 days before annual IEP: write updated Parent Concerns Letter with current priorities.
STEP 4: At annual IEP: review each goal's progress, propose updated goals, review accommodations and services.
STEP 5: Every 3 years (triennial): participate actively in re-evaluation. Request it covers all areas. DO NOT waive.
STEP 6: If child is progressing well, maintain vigilance — academic demands INCREASE each year and LD may re-emerge at higher levels (e.g., reading comprehension issues in middle school, essay writing in high school).

CONTACT: IEP CASE MANAGER: Your child's SpEd teacher — primary contact for progress and concerns.
SCHOOL ADMINISTRATION: For systemic issues (services not delivered, teacher turnover).
ADVOCACY:
• CASE: caseadvocacy.org
• DRC: (800) 776-5746
• Wrightslaw: wrightslaw.com
• Understood: understood.org
IF SERVICES NOT PROVIDED:
• Document missing services
• Request compensatory education
• File CDE complaint if needed

DOCUMENTS: ONGOING DOCUMENTS:
• Quarterly progress reports (file chronologically)
• Annual IEP (keep every version)
• Report cards and standardized test results
• Work samples (periodic — save 1/month)
• Service delivery log (are minutes being provided?)
• Communication log (all emails with school)
• Triennial evaluation reports (every 3 years)
• Updated Parent Concerns Letters (annual)

CREATE A MASTER BINDER:
• Section 1: Current IEP
• Section 2: Evaluations
• Section 3: Progress reports
• Section 4: Correspondence
• Section 5: Work samples
• Section 6: Medical records

TIMELINE: Annual IEP review: every 12 months. Progress reports: quarterly. Triennial re-evaluation: every 3 years. Parent can request IEP meeting at ANY time.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Skip the annual IEP meeting or go unprepared. (2) Accept the same goals carried over year after year without progress — this means the program isn't working. (3) Waive the triennial re-evaluation — updated data protects eligibility. (4) Let service minutes be reduced without data justification. (5) Assume services are being delivered as written — verify. (6) Accept premature exit from special education — LD doesn't 'go away.'

RISKS: ★ Goals repeated without progress — program isn't working but nobody changes approach. Services reduced without data. Teacher turnover disrupts continuity. Triennial waived — updated data lost. Premature exit from SpEd. Services not actually delivered as written (especially with substitutes). Parent doesn't prepare for annual review.

