insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$SLD Step 6.2: Transition Planning & College Preparation (Age 14+)

SLD — Step 6.2: Transition Planning & College Preparation (Age 14+)

WHAT TO DO: PLAN FOR LIFE AFTER HIGH SCHOOL: (1) ITP (Individual Transition Plan) required in IEP by age 16 (start at 14). (2) KEY FOR LD: SAT/ACT ACCOMMODATIONS — apply 6+ months before test date through College Board SSD (collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities). Requires: current evaluation (within 3 years), documented history of accommodations USE, and specific disability documentation. (3) College disability services: student must self-identify and register with disability office BEFORE classes begin. IEP does NOT transfer to college — but 504 plan documentation does support accommodation requests. (4) Self-advocacy skills: teach your child to understand their LD, explain it to others, and request accommodations independently. (5) Career exploration: many successful adults have LD — focus on strengths. (6) DOR (Dept of Rehabilitation): may provide vocational support for significant LD. (7) CalABLE: available if LD is severe enough to substantially limit functioning.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 14-15: begin discussing transition goals at IEP meetings. Include self-advocacy goals.
STEP 2: By age 15-16: begin SAT/ACT accommodation application. Gather: current evaluation (within 3 years), 504/IEP history, documentation of accommodation use.
STEP 3: Apply for SAT/ACT accommodations through College Board SSD (collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities) or ACT (act.org/the-act/accommodations). Submit 6+ months before test.
STEP 4: Visit college disability offices during campus visits. Ask: 'What documentation do you require? What accommodations are typically provided for [LD type]?'
STEP 5: Create a 'College Prep Packet': all evaluations, IEP/504 history, accommodation documentation, physician letter.
STEP 6: Practice self-advocacy: role-play with your child: 'I have dyslexia. I learn best when I have extended time and text-to-speech. Can we discuss accommodations?'

CONTACT: SAT ACCOMMODATIONS:
• College Board SSD: collegeboard.org/students-with-disabilities — (844) 255-7728
• ACT accommodations: act.org/the-act/accommodations — (319) 337-1332
COLLEGE DISABILITY SERVICES:
• AHEAD (Association on Higher Ed & Disability): ahead.org
• Each college's disability office (find on college website)
DOR:
• CA DOR: dor.ca.gov — vocational rehabilitation
• DOR referral: request through IEP by age 16
CALABLE: calable.ca.gov (if LD is significantly limiting)
SELF-ADVOCACY:
• Eye to Eye: eyetoeyenational.org — LD mentoring program
• LD Online: ldonline.org
• Understood: understood.org

DOCUMENTS: TRANSITION DOCUMENTS:
• ITP (in IEP) with post-secondary goals
• Transition assessments
• SAT/ACT accommodation application
• College disability documentation packet
• Self-advocacy plan
• DOR referral (if applicable)
• Complete evaluation and accommodation history

COLLEGE PREP PACKET:
• Most recent evaluation (within 3 years)
• All IEP/504 plans (history of accommodations)
• Documentation of accommodation USE
• Physician/psychologist letter
• Summary of Performance (SOP) from school

TIMELINE: Transition plan: by age 16 (start at 14). SAT/ACT accommodations: apply 6+ months before test. College disability registration: before first semester. DOR referral: by age 16. Self-advocacy: ongoing from age 12+.

AVOID: DO NOT: (1) Wait until senior year to request SAT/ACT accommodations — start in sophomore year. (2) Assume IEP transfers to college — it does NOT. Student must self-identify and request accommodations. (3) Skip teaching self-advocacy — this is the most important transition skill. (4) Stop IEP too early — keep through high school graduation if LD persists. (5) Forget to keep ALL evaluation and accommodation records — college will need them. (6) Overlook trade/vocational schools as excellent options — LD doesn't limit career success.

RISKS: ★ SAT/ACT accommodations denied (requires current evaluation AND accommodation history). College disability office requires documentation student doesn't have. Student doesn't self-identify at college. Self-advocacy skills never taught. DOR services not explored. Transition plan is boilerplate. Student exits SpEd too early.

