* 4 School District IEP (Age 3+): School District Evaluation for ID Eligibility, IEP Development — ID-Specific Placement & Services, IEP Dispute Resolution — ID-Specific Issues
* 5 Regional Center IPP & Services (Lifelong): IPP Development — ID-Specific Services, Apply for Medi-Cal, SSI, IHSS, CalABLE
CalABLE DET
* 6 Therapy, Health & Daily Life: Coordinate Medical & Therapeutic Care, Build Adaptive Skills & Community Inclusion
* 7 Transition Planning (Age 14+): Begin Transition Planning — ITP Required, Conservatorship / Alternatives at Age 18, Adult Services Planning (RC IPP, Employment, Housi
* 8 Lifelong Support & Advocacy: Ongoing Benefits Management & Monitoring, Long-Term Advocacy & Community Engagement$kb$, $kb$journey_id$kb$, $kb$jrn-id-overview$kb$, $kb${"code":"jrn-id-overview","title":"ID Journey Map — Overview","category":"journey-id","subcategory":"overview","source_ref":"Entity Matrix v9.4","origin":"entity-matrix-v9.4"}$kb$::jsonb);
insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 1.1: Parent/Provider Notices Developmental Delays

ID — Step 1.1: Parent/Provider Notices Developmental Delays

WHAT TO DO: Parent or pediatrician observes significant delays across multiple developmental domains: motor milestones delayed (sitting, walking, reaching), speech/language delayed (late babbling, no words by 18 months), difficulty with problem-solving and learning for age, social engagement differences, self-care skills delayed (feeding, dressing). Associated conditions often identified first: Down syndrome (at birth via genetic testing), Fragile X, cerebral palsy, fetal alcohol spectrum disorder. For milder ID, delays may not be apparent until preschool or school age when academic demands increase. Males more commonly diagnosed than females. Prevalence: 1-3% of population (~4.6-7.7M in U.S.).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Parent or pediatrician observes significant delays across multiple developmental domains: motor milestones delayed (sitting, walking, reaching), speech/language delayed (late babbling, no words by 18 months), difficulty with problem-solving and learning for age, social engagement differences, self-care skills delayed (feeding, dressing).
STEP 2: Associated conditions often identified first: Down syndrome (at birth via genetic testing), Fragile X, cerebral palsy, fetal alcohol spectrum disorder.
STEP 3: For milder ID, delays may not be apparent until preschool or school age when academic demands increase.
STEP 4: Males more commonly diagnosed than females.
STEP 5: Prevalence: 1-3% of population (~4.

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN: Request developmental screening at 9, 18, 30 months (ASQ-3). Raise concerns about cognitive, motor, and adaptive delays.
REGIONAL CENTER: dds.ca.gov/rc/listing — 21 RCs statewide. Self-referral accepted, no doctor referral needed. ID is a Lanterman Act qualifying condition.
HELP ME GROW CA: helpmegrowca.org — call 211 for developmental screening and referral.
FIRST 5 CALIFORNIA: ccfc.ca.gov — local First 5 county commissions fund early childhood development.
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — Family Resource Centers provide peer support and information/referral.
CDC MILESTONE TRACKER: cdc.gov/milestones — free app to track developmental milestones.

DOCUMENTS: BEGIN COLLECTING:
• Dated observations journal: note specific delays across domains — 'Oct 5: At age 2, not walking independently (expected by 12-15 months), 5 single words (expected 50+ words), doesn't point to request'
• Video recordings of child in natural settings (play, mealtimes, with peers)
• CDC Milestone Tracker app results
• Well-child visit notes mentioning concerns
• Daycare/preschool teacher observations (request in writing)
• Growth charts and medical records
• Family history of developmental delays or ID (genetic component)

NO DOCUMENTS NEEDED TO SELF-REFER TO RC — just call.

TIMELINE: Ongoing — more severe ID identified earlier (birth-2). Milder ID may not be identified until school age.

AVOID: DO NOT: For milder ID, delays may not be apparent until preschool or school age when academic demands increase

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Milder ID missed because child is 'just a little behind.' Pediatrician uses 'wait and see' during critical early intervention window. Associated conditions (Down syndrome, CP) diagnosed but ID not formally assessed as separate condition. Cultural factors affect when families seek evaluation. Grief and denial delay parent action after initial concerns.

RISKS: ★ Milder ID missed because child is 'just a little behind.' Pediatrician uses 'wait and see' during critical early intervention window. Associated conditions (Down syndrome, CP) diagnosed but ID not formally assessed as separate condition. Cultural factors affect when families seek evaluation. Grief and denial delay parent action after initial concerns.

