insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 2.1: Regional Center Comprehensive Evaluation

ID — Step 2.1: Regional Center Comprehensive Evaluation

WHAT TO DO: RC conducts multidisciplinary evaluation: (1) Cognitive/IQ testing — standardized tests (WISC-V, Stanford-Binet 5, Bayley for infants). IQ ≤70 generally indicates ID (with 5-point measurement error allowing 70-75 range). (2) Adaptive behavior assessment — Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (VABS-3) or ABAS-3, measuring conceptual (language, reading, math), social (interpersonal, responsibility), and practical (self-care, daily living) skills. (3) Developmental history. (4) Medical evaluation. DSM-5 criteria: (A) Deficits in intellectual functions, (B) Deficits in adaptive functioning, (C) Onset during developmental period. DSM-5 severity levels based on ADAPTIVE functioning (not IQ): Mild, Moderate, Severe, Profound.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: RC conducts multidisciplinary evaluation: (1) Cognitive/IQ testing — standardized tests (WISC-V, Stanford-Binet 5, Bayley for infants).
STEP 2: IQ ≤70 generally indicates ID (with 5-point measurement error allowing 70-75 range).
STEP 3: (2) Adaptive behavior assessment — Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (VABS-3) or ABAS-3, measuring conceptual (language, reading, math), social (interpersonal, responsibility), and practical (self-care, daily living) skills.
STEP 4: (3) Developmental history.
STEP 5: (4) Medical evaluation.

CONTACT: YOUR RC SERVICE COORDINATOR: Assigned after intake — get name, direct phone, and email.
RC PSYCHOLOGIST: Conducts cognitive and adaptive behavior assessment.
★ LARRY P. COMPLIANCE: For African American/Black students — IQ tests CANNOT be used to identify or place in ID category in CA. RC must use alternative assessments. Contact DRC: (800) 776-5746 if IQ testing is proposed.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — evaluation rights, Larry P. compliance.
OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — if evaluation exceeds 120-day timeline.
UC DAVIS MIND INSTITUTE: (916) 703-0280 — comprehensive neurodevelopmental evaluations, accepts Medi-Cal.

DOCUMENTS: EVALUATION COMPONENTS FOR ID:
• Cognitive/IQ assessment (WISC-V, SB-5, Bayley-4) — NOTE: Larry P. prohibits IQ tests for Black students
• Adaptive behavior assessment (Vineland-3, ABAS-3) — THIS determines severity level, not IQ
• Developmental history
• Medical/genetic evaluation if indicated
• Speech-language evaluation
• Motor/OT evaluation
• Behavioral assessment

RC WILL PRODUCE:
• Comprehensive evaluation report
• Eligibility determination letter
• If eligible: IPP development meeting scheduled
• If denied: Prior Written Notice with appeal rights

PARENT KEEPS:
• ALL evaluation reports (request copies)
• Eligibility determination letter
• Calendar with 120-day deadline marked

TIMELINE: 120 days from referral to eligibility determination.

AVOID: DO NOT: DSM-5 severity levels based on ADAPTIVE functioning (not IQ): Mild, Moderate, Severe, Profound

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: EVALUATION ISSUES: Larry P. v. Riles ruling — in California, standardized IQ tests CANNOT be used to identify or place Black students in ID category. School districts must use alternative assessments for Black students. Culturally/linguistically biased testing may overidentify minority children or underidentify those from non-dominant cultures. IQ score near cutoff (70-75) creates eligibility disputes. Adaptive behavior not assessed comprehensively — only parent report collected, not teacher/clinician observation. Child performs better on structured testing than in daily life (or vice versa).

RISKS: ★ EVALUATION ISSUES: Larry P. v. Riles ruling — in California, standardized IQ tests CANNOT be used to identify or place Black students in ID category. School districts must use alternative assessments for Black students. Culturally/linguistically biased testing may overidentify minority children or underidentify those from non-dominant cultures. IQ score near cutoff (70-75) creates eligibility disputes. Adaptive behavior not assessed comprehensively — only parent report collected, not teacher/clinician observation. Child performs better on structured testing than in daily life (or vice versa).

