insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 2.2: Understand ID Severity Levels & What They Mean

ID — Step 2.2: Understand ID Severity Levels & What They Mean

WHAT TO DO: DSM-5 severity is based on ADAPTIVE FUNCTIONING (not IQ scores alone): MILD (IQ ~50-70, ~85% of ID population): can develop academic skills to ~6th grade level, live independently with support, competitive employment possible with accommodations. MODERATE (IQ ~35-49, ~10%): academic skills to ~2nd grade, semi-independent living with moderate support, supported employment. SEVERE (IQ ~20-34, ~3-4%): limited communication, significant daily support needed, may learn sight words and basic self-care. PROFOUND (IQ <20, ~1-2%): requires 24-hour support, very limited communication, may use AAC. KEY: ID is LIFELONG but with appropriate supports, functioning improves throughout life.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: DSM-5 severity is based on ADAPTIVE FUNCTIONING (not IQ scores alone): MILD (IQ ~50-70, ~85% of ID population): can develop academic skills to ~6th grade level, live independently with support, competitive employment possible with accommodations.
STEP 2: MODERATE (IQ ~35-49, ~10%): academic skills to ~2nd grade, semi-independent living with moderate support, supported employment.
STEP 3: SEVERE (IQ ~20-34, ~3-4%): limited communication, significant daily support needed, may learn sight words and basic self-care.
STEP 4: PROFOUND (IQ <20, ~1-2%): requires 24-hour support, very limited communication, may use AAC.
STEP 5: KEY: ID is LIFELONG but with appropriate supports, functioning improves throughout life.

CONTACT: YOUR RC SERVICE COORDINATOR: Explain severity determination and what it means for services.
UC DAVIS MIND INSTITUTE FAMILY NAVIGATOR: health.ucdavis.edu/mindinstitute — peer support, monthly virtual groups, parent coaching after ID diagnosis.
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — peer support from families with similar experiences.
THE ARC: thearc.org — largest national IDD advocacy org, 600+ local chapters. Find your local chapter.
PARENTS HELPING PARENTS (PHP): php.com — (408) 727-5775 — one-on-one consultations, support groups.
NDSS (National Down Syndrome Society): ndss.org — (800) 221-4602 — if Down syndrome is the cause.
AAIDD (American Association on Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities): aaidd.org — professional resources on ID.

DOCUMENTS: EVALUATION REPORT SHOULD INCLUDE:
• DSM-5 severity level: Mild (85%), Moderate (10%), Severe (3-4%), Profound (1-2%)
• Based on ADAPTIVE FUNCTIONING (not IQ): conceptual, social, practical domains
• Vineland-3 or ABAS-3 scores with domain breakdowns
• Cognitive assessment scores (with Larry P. caveat for Black students)
• Functional impact across settings (home, school, community)
• Prognosis and support needs
• Recommendations for services

PARENT SHOULD HAVE:
• Copy of complete evaluation report
• Written summary of severity level and what it means for daily life
• List of recommended services
• Questions for follow-up with RC service coordinator

TIMELINE: At evaluation completion.

AVOID: DO NOT: DSM-5 severity is based on ADAPTIVE FUNCTIONING (not IQ scores alone): MILD (IQ ~50-70, ~85% of ID population): can develop academic skills to ~6th grade level, live independently with support, competitive employment possible with accommodations

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Severity level misunderstood as 'ceiling' for growth. Parents given prognosis without hope. Medical model focuses on deficits rather than strengths and support needs. Severity level drives expectations rather than individualized assessment. Older terminology (mental retardation, EMR/TMR) still used by some professionals — stigmatizing.

RISKS: Severity level misunderstood as 'ceiling' for growth. Parents given prognosis without hope. Medical model focuses on deficits rather than strengths and support needs. Severity level drives expectations rather than individualized assessment. Older terminology (mental retardation, EMR/TMR) still used by some professionals — stigmatizing.

