insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 3.1: Early Start IFSP — ID-Specific Services

ID — Step 3.1: Early Start IFSP — ID-Specific Services

WHAT TO DO: For children under 3 with ID: Regional Center Early Start provides family-centered intervention. IFSP services typically include: speech-language therapy, occupational therapy, physical therapy (especially for motor delays), developmental specialist, parent training/coaching, behavioral support if needed, assistive technology/AAC. Services in natural environments (home, daycare). For ID with known etiology (Down syndrome, genetic conditions): medical management and monitoring also critical. Frequency should match severity — more severe ID needs more intensive early intervention. Hanen 'More Than Words' program often used for children with limited communication.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: For children under 3 with ID: Regional Center Early Start provides family-centered intervention.
STEP 2: IFSP services typically include: speech-language therapy, occupational therapy, physical therapy (especially for motor delays), developmental specialist, parent training/coaching, behavioral support if needed, assistive technology/AAC.
STEP 3: Services in natural environments (home, daycare).
STEP 4: For ID with known etiology (Down syndrome, genetic conditions): medical management and monitoring also critical.
STEP 5: Frequency should match severity — more severe ID needs more intensive early intervention.

CONTACT: YOUR REGIONAL CENTER: dds.ca.gov/rc/listing — Early Start intake for ages 0-3.
CA DDS EARLY START: dds.ca.gov/services/early-start — program information.
RC SERVICE COORDINATOR: Primary contact for IFSP development and service authorization.
VENDORED PROVIDERS: RC authorizes early intervention providers — SLP, OT, PT, developmental specialist, parent training.
FRCNCA: frcnca.org — peer support during Early Start.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — if services are delayed or inadequate.
OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — free advocacy for IFSP disputes.

DOCUMENTS: IFSP DOCUMENTS:
• IFSP document (Individualized Family Service Plan)
• Service authorizations for each provider (SLP, OT, PT, developmental specialist, parent training)
• Provider contact information for all services
• Service schedule (days, times, locations)
• IFSP goals with measurable outcomes
• 6-month review schedule
• Annual IFSP re-evaluation plan

PARENT CREATES:
• Service tracking log (dates, attendance, notes from each session)
• Progress observations journal
• Questions for next IFSP review
• Video recordings showing progress

TIMELINE: 45 days from referral to IFSP. Services begin immediately after IFSP.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Service intensity insufficient for child's needs. Therapy focused on deficit remediation rather than functional participation. Parent coaching not provided — parent becomes passive observer of therapy. Transition to school at age 3 not planned early enough. Medical management not coordinated with developmental services.

RISKS: Service intensity insufficient for child's needs. Therapy focused on deficit remediation rather than functional participation. Parent coaching not provided — parent becomes passive observer of therapy. Transition to school at age 3 not planned early enough. Medical management not coordinated with developmental services.

