insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 3.2: Transition from Early Start to School District + Ongoing RC (Age 3)

ID — Step 3.2: Transition from Early Start to School District + Ongoing RC (Age 3)

WHAT TO DO: At age 3, DUAL SYSTEM begins: child transitions to school district IEP AND continues Regional Center services. This is DIFFERENT from autism (where some children exit RC if mild) — ID almost always continues with RC. School district evaluation and IEP must be in place by 3rd birthday. RC continues IPP (Individual Program Plan) with different but complementary services. Key: school provides EDUCATIONAL services (classroom instruction, school-based therapies). RC provides COMMUNITY/HOME services (respite, after-school programs, parent training, behavioral support, community inclusion). Parent must coordinate between two systems.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 3, DUAL SYSTEM begins: child transitions to school district IEP AND continues Regional Center services.
STEP 2: This is DIFFERENT from autism (where some children exit RC if mild) — ID almost always continues with RC.
STEP 3: School district evaluation and IEP must be in place by 3rd birthday.
STEP 4: RC continues IPP (Individual Program Plan) with different but complementary services.
STEP 5: Key: school provides EDUCATIONAL services (classroom instruction, school-based therapies).

CONTACT: SCHOOL DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION: ID = 'intellectual disability' under IDEA — 34 CFR §300.8(c)(6)
IMPORTANT CA LAW: Larry P. v. Riles — IQ tests CANNOT be used to place Black students in ID category
CA DEPT OF EDUCATION: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — free legal advocacy (priority for ID families)
SERR MANUAL: serr.disabilityrightsca.org — comprehensive rights guide

DOCUMENTS: Transition meeting (RC + school district). School evaluation consent. IFSP records. RC IPP continuation.

TIMELINE: Transition conference: 90+ days before 3rd birthday. IEP in place by 3rd birthday.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: DUAL SYSTEM CONFUSION: Parents don't understand RC continues after school starts — they think school 'replaces' RC. Services duplicated or gaps where each system thinks the other covers it. RC service coordinator and school case manager don't communicate. IPP goals and IEP goals not aligned. School provides less therapy than Early Start — parents feel 'cliff.'

RISKS: ★ DUAL SYSTEM CONFUSION: Parents don't understand RC continues after school starts — they think school 'replaces' RC. Services duplicated or gaps where each system thinks the other covers it. RC service coordinator and school case manager don't communicate. IPP goals and IEP goals not aligned. School provides less therapy than Early Start — parents feel 'cliff.'

