insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 4.1: School District Evaluation for ID Eligibility

ID — Step 4.1: School District Evaluation for ID Eligibility

WHAT TO DO: Under IDEA, ID is defined as 'significantly subaverage general intellectual functioning, existing concurrently with deficits in adaptive behavior and manifested during the developmental period that adversely affects a child's educational performance' (34 CFR §300.8(c)(6)). California Code of Regulations (5 CCR §3030). School evaluation includes: IQ testing, adaptive behavior assessment, academic achievement testing, speech/language evaluation, motor assessment, social-emotional assessment, observation. CALIFORNIA-SPECIFIC: Larry P. v. Riles (1979/1986) — IQ tests CANNOT be used to place Black students in ID category or substantially equivalent placements. Alternative assessments required.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Under IDEA, ID is defined as 'significantly subaverage general intellectual functioning, existing concurrently with deficits in adaptive behavior and manifested during the developmental period that adversely affects a child's educational performance' (34 CFR §300.
STEP 2: California Code of Regulations (5 CCR §3030).
STEP 3: School evaluation includes: IQ testing, adaptive behavior assessment, academic achievement testing, speech/language evaluation, motor assessment, social-emotional assessment, observation.
STEP 4: CALIFORNIA-SPECIFIC: Larry P.
STEP 5: Riles (1979/1986) — IQ tests CANNOT be used to place Black students in ID category or substantially equivalent placements.

CONTACT: SCHOOL DISTRICT SPECIAL EDUCATION: Submit written evaluation request to SpEd Director.
★ LARRY P. COMPLIANCE: For Black students — school district CANNOT use IQ tests to identify or place in ID category. Must use alternative assessments. This is CA-specific law (Larry P. v. Riles, 1979/1986).
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — free IEP advocacy.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — Larry P. compliance, evaluation rights.
CDE COMPLAINT: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/cmplntproc.asp — file if district uses prohibited testing.
OAH: (916) 263-0880 — mediation/due process for evaluation disputes.

DOCUMENTS: PARENT CREATES:
• Written evaluation request letter (hand-deliver AND email, keep date-stamped copy)
• Parent Input Form (detailed — describe cognitive, adaptive, academic functioning)
• Larry P. compliance note if child is Black/African American

DISTRICT PROVIDES:
• Assessment plan within 15 calendar days
• Evaluation report: cognitive assessment (alternative for Black students), adaptive behavior, academic achievement, related assessments
• Eligibility determination under IDEA 34 CFR §300.8(c)(6)
• Parent rights notification

SHARE WITH SCHOOL:
• All RC evaluation reports
• RC IPP (shows services already in place)
• Medical records and genetic testing results
• Your observations journal and videos

TIMELINE: 15 days to present assessment plan. 60 days from consent to completed evaluation.

AVOID: DO NOT: Riles (1979/1986) — IQ tests CANNOT be used to place Black students in ID category or substantially equivalent placements

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Larry P. COMPLIANCE: School district uses IQ test for Black student — ILLEGAL in California for ID placement. Alternative assessments for Black students may be less well-standardized. Adaptive behavior assessment incomplete. Child tested in non-native language. Evaluation focuses on deficits only — no strengths documented. Mild ID may not be identified if child compensates well in structured testing.

RISKS: ★ Larry P. COMPLIANCE: School district uses IQ test for Black student — ILLEGAL in California for ID placement. Alternative assessments for Black students may be less well-standardized. Adaptive behavior assessment incomplete. Child tested in non-native language. Evaluation focuses on deficits only — no strengths documented. Mild ID may not be identified if child compensates well in structured testing.

