insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 4.2: IEP Development — ID-Specific Placement & Services

ID — Step 4.2: IEP Development — ID-Specific Placement & Services

WHAT TO DO: IEP for ID must address: (1) PLACEMENT on the continuum — from general education with supports (LRE principle) through resource specialist, special day class (SDC), to separate school. LRE = Least Restrictive Environment. Child must be educated with non-disabled peers to maximum extent appropriate. (2) SERVICES: specialized academic instruction (modified curriculum), speech-language therapy, OT, PT, behavioral support, adaptive PE, assistive technology/AAC, 1:1 aide if needed. (3) GOALS: functional academics, communication, self-care/adaptive skills, social skills, community skills, pre-vocational skills. (4) MODIFICATIONS (not just accommodations): modified curriculum, alternative assessments (California Alternate Assessment for state testing). Extended School Year (ESY) — many students with ID qualify because of regression risk.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: IEP for ID must address: (1) PLACEMENT on the continuum — from general education with supports (LRE principle) through resource specialist, special day class (SDC), to separate school.
STEP 2: LRE = Least Restrictive Environment.
STEP 3: Child must be educated with non-disabled peers to maximum extent appropriate.
STEP 4: (2) SERVICES: specialized academic instruction (modified curriculum), speech-language therapy, OT, PT, behavioral support, adaptive PE, assistive technology/AAC, 1:1 aide if needed.
STEP 5: (3) GOALS: functional academics, communication, self-care/adaptive skills, social skills, community skills, pre-vocational skills.

CONTACT: IEP TEAM: You, SpEd teacher, gen ed teacher, school psychologist, administrator, related services (SLP, OT, PT as applicable).
FAMILY EMPOWERMENT CENTERS: cde.ca.gov/sp/se/qa/fecprgm.asp — free IEP advocacy.
INCLUSION ADVOCATES:
• TASH: tash.org — inclusion advocacy, research, policy
• Inclusion Collaborative (Santa Clara Co): inclusion.scusd.edu
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — LRE (Least Restrictive Environment) advocacy. IDEA's strong preference for education with non-disabled peers.
TASK: taskca.org — parent training on IEP development.
SERR MANUAL: serr.disabilityrightsca.org — comprehensive rights reference.
CDE ALTERNATE ASSESSMENT: caaspp.cde.ca.gov — California Alternate Assessment info for students with significant cognitive disabilities.

DOCUMENTS: IEP DOCUMENT FOR ID MUST INCLUDE:
• PLAAFP across all domains (academic, functional, communication, motor, social)
• Annual goals: academic AND functional/adaptive
• SDI (Specially Designed Instruction) with methodology
• Related services: SLP, OT, PT, behavioral, adapted PE
• Accommodations and modifications
• ★ LRE (Least Restrictive Environment): IDEA's strong preference for inclusion with non-disabled peers
• ESY (Extended School Year): ID students often qualify due to regression risk
• CA Alternate Assessment participation (if significant cognitive disability)
• AT consideration
• Transition plan (by age 16)

PARENT BRINGS:
• RC evaluation reports and IPP
• Parent Concerns Letter
• Inclusion research and advocacy resources
• Private evaluation (if obtained)
• Work samples and progress

TIMELINE: 30 days from completed evaluation to IEP meeting.

AVOID: DO NOT: (4) MODIFICATIONS (not just accommodations): modified curriculum, alternative assessments (California Alternate Assessment for state testing)

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: PLACEMENT BATTLES: Child automatically placed in most restrictive setting (separate school) without considering LRE. 'Modified curriculum' used to justify segregated placement. General education teachers untrained for inclusive practices. District claims inclusion is 'too expensive.' Parents not informed of full placement continuum. IDEA's strong preference for inclusion not honored. Paraprofessional support used as substitute for qualified teacher instruction. ESY denied despite regression evidence.

RISKS: ★ PLACEMENT BATTLES: Child automatically placed in most restrictive setting (separate school) without considering LRE. 'Modified curriculum' used to justify segregated placement. General education teachers untrained for inclusive practices. District claims inclusion is 'too expensive.' Parents not informed of full placement continuum. IDEA's strong preference for inclusion not honored. Paraprofessional support used as substitute for qualified teacher instruction. ESY denied despite regression evidence.

