insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 5.1: IPP Development — ID-Specific Services

ID — Step 5.1: IPP Development — ID-Specific Services

WHAT TO DO: Regional Center IPP (Individual Program Plan) is the LIFELONG service plan for individuals with ID. IPP services may include: respite care (relief for families), behavioral support services, day programs/after-school programs, social/recreational activities, community integration support, independent living skills training, supported employment, residential services (as adult), transportation, assistive technology, parent training, crisis intervention. IPP is reviewed at least annually. Services are individualized — no 'menu' of standard services. POS (Purchase of Service) codes fund specific services. Family must actively participate in IPP development. CA DDS budget: $18.7B (FY 2025-26, largest in history).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Regional Center IPP (Individual Program Plan) is the LIFELONG service plan for individuals with ID.
STEP 2: IPP services may include: respite care (relief for families), behavioral support services, day programs/after-school programs, social/recreational activities, community integration support, independent living skills training, supported employment, residential services (as adult), transportation, assistive technology, parent training, crisis intervention.
STEP 3: IPP is reviewed at least annually.
STEP 4: Services are individualized — no 'menu' of standard services.
STEP 5: POS (Purchase of Service) codes fund specific services.

CONTACT: YOUR RC SERVICE COORDINATOR: Primary contact for IPP development. IPP is LIFELONG service plan — reviewed annually.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — IPP rights, service denial appeals.
OCRA: (800) 390-7032 — free advocacy for IPP disputes.
THE ARC: thearc.org — local chapter can help families understand service options.
CA DDS SELF-DETERMINATION PROGRAM: dds.ca.gov/initiatives/sdp — participant-directed budget option. Available statewide.
★ POS (Purchase of Services) SPENDING DATA: Check your RC's POS data at dds.ca.gov/rc/purchase-of-service-data — document disparities if services seem insufficient.
DDS OMBUDSMAN: (877) 658-9731 — if RC is unresponsive.

DOCUMENTS: IPP DOCUMENT INCLUDES:
• Life goals across all domains: health, safety, education, employment, recreation, social, residential
• Specific services authorized: respite, behavioral support, day program, transportation, specialized equipment, home modifications
• Service frequency and provider information
• Annual review date
• Emergency contact plan

PARENT BRINGS TO IPP:
• Your priorities list for each life domain
• Current IEP (to coordinate school + RC goals)
• Medical records and provider list
• Daily routine and support needs assessment

★ POS EQUITY CHECK:
• Check your RC's POS spending data: dds.ca.gov/rc/purchase-of-service-data
• Compare your family's services to RC averages
• Document any spending disparities for advocacy

TIMELINE: IPP within 60 days of eligibility. Annual review required.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: RC SERVICE GAPS: Service coordinator caseload too high (100+ clients) — limited advocacy capacity. POS spending disparities by race/ethnicity well-documented. Services denied as 'not necessary' without adequate justification. Family not informed of available services. Generic IPP not individualized to child's needs. Wait lists for behavioral support, respite, SLS. RC says 'school is responsible' for things RC should provide (and vice versa).

RISKS: ★ RC SERVICE GAPS: Service coordinator caseload too high (100+ clients) — limited advocacy capacity. POS spending disparities by race/ethnicity well-documented. Services denied as 'not necessary' without adequate justification. Family not informed of available services. Generic IPP not individualized to child's needs. Wait lists for behavioral support, respite, SLS. RC says 'school is responsible' for things RC should provide (and vice versa).

