insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 6.1: Coordinate Medical & Therapeutic Care

ID — Step 6.1: Coordinate Medical & Therapeutic Care

WHAT TO DO: Many children with ID have complex medical needs alongside developmental needs. Common co-occurring medical conditions: seizure disorders/epilepsy (~30% of ID), hearing and vision impairments, cardiac conditions (especially Down syndrome), feeding/swallowing difficulties, sleep disorders, dental issues, obesity. Therapeutic services: SLP (communication/AAC/feeding), OT (fine motor, sensory, self-care), PT (gross motor, mobility), behavioral support (ABA may be appropriate depending on co-occurring autism), psychology/psychiatry (mental health — ID + mental health co-occurs 3-4x more than general population).

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Many children with ID have complex medical needs alongside developmental needs.
STEP 2: Common co-occurring medical conditions: seizure disorders/epilepsy (~30% of ID), hearing and vision impairments, cardiac conditions (especially Down syndrome), feeding/swallowing difficulties, sleep disorders, dental issues, obesity.
STEP 3: Therapeutic services: SLP (communication/AAC/feeding), OT (fine motor, sensory, self-care), PT (gross motor, mobility), behavioral support (ABA may be appropriate depending on co-occurring autism), psychology/psychiatry (mental health — ID + mental health co-occurs 3-4x more than general population).

CONTACT: PEDIATRICIAN / PRIMARY CARE: Coordinate with specialists.
SPECIALISTS BY CO-OCCURRING CONDITION:
• Neurology (epilepsy ~30% of ID): request referral from PCP
• Cardiology (especially Down syndrome): contact through PCP
• Gastroenterology (feeding/swallowing): request referral
• Psychiatry (mental health 3-4x higher): county behavioral health or private
CA CHILDREN'S SERVICES (CCS): dhcs.ca.gov/services/ccs — specialized medical services for eligible children with qualifying conditions.
COUNTY BEHAVIORAL HEALTH: Each CA county has children's behavioral health. Access through Medi-Cal managed care plan.
REGIONAL CENTER: Funds behavioral services, respite, specialized therapies through IPP.

DOCUMENTS: MEDICAL COORDINATION DOCUMENTS:
• Master provider list (PCP, specialists, therapists, RC providers, school providers)
• Medication log (if applicable)
• Specialist reports: neurology (epilepsy), cardiology (Down syndrome), GI (feeding), psychiatry
• Therapy progress reports from all providers
• Medical appointment calendar
• ★ Mental health screening documentation — psychiatric conditions co-occur 3-4x more often in ID but are frequently attributed to ID rather than diagnosed separately ('diagnostic overshadowing')
• CCS (California Children's Services) enrollment if eligible
• Insurance authorizations and renewal dates
• Emergency medical plan

TIMELINE: Ongoing — medical care is lifelong for many ID-associated conditions.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Medical needs overshadow developmental needs (or vice versa). No medical home coordination. Specialists don't communicate with each other or with RC/school. Insurance denies therapies. Mental health needs unaddressed — behavior attributed to ID rather than co-occurring depression/anxiety. Polypharmacy without adequate monitoring.

RISKS: Medical needs overshadow developmental needs (or vice versa). No medical home coordination. Specialists don't communicate with each other or with RC/school. Insurance denies therapies. Mental health needs unaddressed — behavior attributed to ID rather than co-occurring depression/anxiety. Polypharmacy without adequate monitoring.

