insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 6.2: Build Adaptive Skills & Community Inclusion

ID — Step 6.2: Build Adaptive Skills & Community Inclusion

WHAT TO DO: Core focus for ID families: building functional independence through adaptive skill development. Daily living skills: self-care (hygiene, dressing, toileting), meal preparation, household tasks, money management, time management, safety skills. Community skills: transportation, shopping, restaurant, recreation, social interaction. Self-advocacy: understanding own needs, communicating preferences, making choices. KEY PRINCIPLE: Presume competence — assume the individual CAN learn, given appropriate support and instruction. Community inclusion: Special Olympics, adaptive recreation, inclusive community programs, faith community inclusion. Self-Determination movement: individuals with ID have the right to direct their own lives.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: Core focus for ID families: building functional independence through adaptive skill development.
STEP 2: Daily living skills: self-care (hygiene, dressing, toileting), meal preparation, household tasks, money management, time management, safety skills.
STEP 3: Community skills: transportation, shopping, restaurant, recreation, social interaction.
STEP 4: Self-advocacy: understanding own needs, communicating preferences, making choices.
STEP 5: KEY PRINCIPLE: Presume competence — assume the individual CAN learn, given appropriate support and instruction.

CONTACT: SPECIAL OLYMPICS CA: sosc.org (Southern CA), sonc.org (Northern CA) — sports and social inclusion.
YOUR LOCAL PARKS & RECREATION: Many CA cities offer adaptive recreation programs.
THE ARC: thearc.org — local chapters offer social programs, recreation, community inclusion.
REGIONAL CENTER: Funds day programs, social skills groups, community integration services.
PEOPLE FIRST OF CA: peoplefirstofcalifornia.org — self-advocacy organization.
BEST BUDDIES CA: bestbuddies.org/california — peer friendships and inclusion programs.
CA STATE COUNCIL ON DEVELOPMENTAL DISABILITIES (SCDD): scdd.ca.gov — advocacy and community inclusion.

DOCUMENTS: ADAPTIVE SKILLS DOCUMENTS:
• Adaptive behavior assessment (Vineland-3, ABAS-3) — baseline and updates
• Daily living skills checklist and progress tracking
• Community skills development plan
• Self-advocacy skills curriculum
• Special Olympics registration and participation records
• Adaptive recreation program enrollment
• Best Buddies or social inclusion program participation
• Respite care authorization and provider info

IEP FUNCTIONAL GOALS:
• Goals addressing daily living skills
• Community-based instruction (CBI) documentation
• Transition assessment results (functional, vocational)

TIMELINE: Ongoing — skill building is lifelong with evolving goals.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Overprotection prevents skill development. Opportunities for community inclusion limited. Social isolation, especially in adolescence. Community programs not accessible or welcoming. Skills taught in isolation (school) don't transfer to community. Peer relationships limited to disability-specific settings.

RISKS: Overprotection prevents skill development. Opportunities for community inclusion limited. Social isolation, especially in adolescence. Community programs not accessible or welcoming. Skills taught in isolation (school) don't transfer to community. Peer relationships limited to disability-specific settings.

