insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 7.2: Conservatorship / Alternatives at Age 18

ID — Step 7.2: Conservatorship / Alternatives at Age 18

WHAT TO DO: At age 18, child becomes legal adult regardless of cognitive ability. Options: (1) FULL CONSERVATORSHIP: most restrictive — another person makes all decisions. Court process, attorney required, costly, removes nearly all rights. Should be LAST resort. (2) LIMITED CONSERVATORSHIP: most common for ID — conservator has authority in specific areas (residence, medical, education, social) but individual retains other rights. Preferred over full conservatorship. (3) SUPPORTED DECISION-MAKING (SDM): emerging alternative — individual retains all legal rights but designates trusted supporters to help with decisions. Growing movement. (4) POWER OF ATTORNEY: if individual can understand and consent. Start planning 12-18 months before 18th birthday.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: At age 18, child becomes legal adult regardless of cognitive ability.
STEP 2: Options: (1) FULL CONSERVATORSHIP: most restrictive — another person makes all decisions.
STEP 3: Court process, attorney required, costly, removes nearly all rights.
STEP 4: Should be LAST resort.
STEP 5: (2) LIMITED CONSERVATORSHIP: most common for ID — conservator has authority in specific areas (residence, medical, education, social) but individual retains other rights.

CONTACT: ★ START PLANNING 12-18 MONTHS BEFORE 18th BIRTHDAY:
PROBATE COURT: File conservatorship petition in your county Superior Court. Limited conservatorship is most common for ID (7 specific powers).
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — alternatives to conservatorship guidance. DRC advocates for least-restrictive options.
SUPPORTED DECISION-MAKING (SDM): CA AB 1663 (2023) codified SDM as alternative. supporteddecisions.org — national resources.
LEGAL AID: Find your county's legal aid organization for conservatorship help. lawhelpcalifornia.org
SPECIAL NEEDS TRUST ATTORNEY: specialneedsalliance.org — conservatorship often established alongside SNT.
YOUR RC SERVICE COORDINATOR: Can help connect to legal resources and explain RC's role at age 18.
SCDD: scdd.ca.gov — policy guidance on conservatorship alternative

DOCUMENTS: CONSERVATORSHIP DOCUMENTS:
• Petition for Limited Conservatorship (file in county Superior Court — Probate Division)
• RC letter supporting conservatorship (or alternatives)
• Medical/psychological reports documenting need
• Investigation report by court investigator
• Court-appointed attorney for conservatee
• Letters of conservatorship (if granted)
• Annual accounting requirements

ALTERNATIVES TO CONSERVATORSHIP:
• Supported Decision-Making Agreement (CA AB 1663): supporteddecisions.org — template agreements
• Power of Attorney (if individual can consent)
• Representative Payee for SSI (through SSA)
• HIPAA authorization for medical decisions
• Advance healthcare directive

★ START 12-18 MONTHS BEFORE 18th BIRTHDAY

TIMELINE: Begin planning at 16-17. File 6-12 months before 18th birthday if conservatorship needed.

AVOID: DO NOT: Options: (1) FULL CONSERVATORSHIP: most restrictive — another person makes all decisions

AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: Conservatorship pursued by default without considering SDM or limited conservatorship. Full conservatorship used when limited would suffice. Court process overwhelming and expensive ($3,000-5,000+). Individual's preferences not considered. RC doesn't inform families about SDM alternative. Conservatorship restricts voting rights in some cases.

RISKS: ★ Conservatorship pursued by default without considering SDM or limited conservatorship. Full conservatorship used when limited would suffice. Court process overwhelming and expensive ($3,000-5,000+). Individual's preferences not considered. RC doesn't inform families about SDM alternative. Conservatorship restricts voting rights in some cases.

