insert into public.knowledge_embeddings (content, source, section, metadata) values ($kb$ID Step 7.3: Adult Services Planning (RC IPP, Employment, Housing)

ID — Step 7.3: Adult Services Planning (RC IPP, Employment, Housing)

WHAT TO DO: After school exit (22 or diploma): Regional Center becomes primary service system. Adult IPP should address: (1) DAY SERVICES: day program, supported employment, community-based activities. (2) EMPLOYMENT: CIE (Competitive Integrated Employment) is the GOLD STANDARD — supported employment with job coach, customized employment, self-employment. DOR provides vocational rehabilitation. (3) RESIDENTIAL: family home with supports, supported living services (SLS), independent living, group homes, specialized residential. (4) RECREATION/SOCIAL: adaptive recreation, social groups, community inclusion programs. (5) HEALTH: continued medical management, mental health support. RC budget for adult services is largest portion of $18.7B DDS budget.

HOW TO ACT: STEP 1: After school exit (22 or diploma): Regional Center becomes primary service system.
STEP 2: Adult IPP should address: (1) DAY SERVICES: day program, supported employment, community-based activities.
STEP 3: (2) EMPLOYMENT: CIE (Competitive Integrated Employment) is the GOLD STANDARD — supported employment with job coach, customized employment, self-employment.
STEP 4: DOR provides vocational rehabilitation.
STEP 5: (3) RESIDENTIAL: family home with supports, supported living services (SLS), independent living, group homes, specialized residential.

CONTACT: REGIONAL CENTER: IPP continues through adult life. Annual review required.
CA DOR: dor.ca.gov — Competitive Integrated Employment (CIE) services.
CA DDS EMPLOYMENT FIRST POLICY: dds.ca.gov/initiatives/employment-first — CIE as preferred outcome.
HOUSING OPTIONS:
• CA HCD: hcd.ca.gov — housing resources
• HUD Section 8/Housing Choice Voucher: contact your local housing authority
• RC-funded supported living: through IPP
THE ARC: thearc.org — Achieve with Us employment, local chapter community programs.
SCDD: scdd.ca.gov — community living and employment advocacy.
DRC: (800) 776-5746 — housing rights, employment rights, IPP advocacy.

DOCUMENTS: ADULT SERVICES DOCUMENTS:
• RC IPP (annual review required)
• DOR IPE (Individualized Plan for Employment)
• CIE (Competitive Integrated Employment) goal documentation
• Supported Employment provider information
• Housing applications (Section 8 waitlist — apply YEARS in advance)
• Supported living services authorization through RC
• Day program enrollment (if applicable)
• Self-Determination Program budget (if enrolled)

BENEFITS COORDINATION:
• SSI adult redetermination (age 18)
• IHSS reassessment for adult services
• Medi-Cal continuation
• CalABLE/SNT coordination plan

TIMELINE: Ongoing — annual IPP review. DOR services until employed. Housing: multi-year waitlists.

AVOID: AVOID THESE COMMON PITFALLS: HOUSING CRISIS: Affordable accessible housing waitlists are YEARS long. Sheltered workshops still exist despite federal push for CIE. Day programs are 'custodial' rather than skill-building. RC service coordinator overwhelmed — insufficient advocacy. Family aging out (parent caregivers aging). Self-determination program underutilized. Racial disparities in adult service access persistent.

RISKS: ★ HOUSING CRISIS: Affordable accessible housing waitlists are YEARS long. Sheltered workshops still exist despite federal push for CIE. Day programs are 'custodial' rather than skill-building. RC service coordinator overwhelmed — insufficient advocacy. Family aging out (parent caregivers aging). Self-determination program underutilized. Racial disparities in adult service access persistent.

